-- Each operation admits only its signature-listed attributes; target is first when present and remaining attributes are alphabetical.` — An Action attribute is valid only when it is listed by the operation signature and its value has the governing category or literal form named by attribute-name; a category value may be a category symbol, a typed binding holding one, or a homogeneous list when that attribute permits LIST[STRING]. STRING remains accepted only for attributes explicitly marked as unresolved N3 build-phase violations in attribute-name. For search_web, target is required and optional attributes are emitted alphabetically. For support workflows, check_support_status uses target=order for order metrics and target=customer for buyer metrics; offer_discount and offer_expedited_shipping use target=order.
- [revise] Generate: grammar `generation ::= "GENERATE" "(" generate-attr ("," generate-attr)* ")" "->" IDENT ":" gen_type
generate-attr ::= attribute-name "=" value
-- Legal attributes: target, audience, content, duration, duration_unit, format, locale, quantity, style, tone, stop_min_per_day, stop_requirement, max_walk_duration, max_walk_duration_unit.` — GENERATE target is artifact-value; audience is recipient-value; tone, format, style, locale, and duration units use their corresponding value categories; numeric constraints use NUMBER. stop_requirement is an entity-name symbol, an entity-name-compatible typed binding, or a LIST[STRING] of such symbols, and identifies what each daily minimum counts. stop_min_per_day applies to each calendar day represented by the generated itinerary. max_walk_duration with max_walk_duration_unit bounds travel time between each pair of consecutive itinerary stops. target is first and all optional attributes follow alphabetically. content remains a STRING only as an explicitly unresolved N3 build-phase violation. A single requested artifact returns STRING; independently usable multiple artifacts return LIST[STRING].
- [revise] Utterance: grammar `utterance ::= "UTTER" speech-act "(" utter-attr ("," utter-attr)* ")" ("->" IDENT)?
speech-act ::= operation-vocabulary member
utter-attr ::= attribute-name "=" value` — Only audience, content, recipient, target, and tone are legal UTTER attributes and they must be alphabetical. recipient and audience require recipient-value, tone requires tone-value, and target requires its attribute-name governing category. content remains a transitional STRING payload and an explicit unresolved N3 build-phase violation; it is not a glossary value set. A bound UTTER result has type STRING and must use content, content_2, content_3, and so on.
