name, recipient-value, artifact-value, or any other glossary category. REF[STRING] and LIST[REF[STRING]] are reference domains, not category-valued STRINGs.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Reuse a selected temple as an itinerary stop requirement.", "braincode": "LET required_stop : STRING = temple\nGENERATE(target=art_itinerary, content=\"Kyoto itinerary\", stop_min_per_day=1, stop_requirement=required_stop) -> plan : STRING"}, "glossary_gloss": "Vocabulary symbols carry the glossary category they came from when they are bound and reused. A plain text STRING does not gain a category merely because it contains similar text.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "This repairs the ambiguity created when all vocabulary symbols type-check as STRING. It supplies the static rule required to validate category-valued attributes deterministically without closing existing STRING channels prematurely.", "cites_source": "Why Agentic Systems Need Ontologies", "addresses_note": "N3"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Action", "grammar": "action ::= \"ACTION\" operation-id \"(\" action-attrs? \")\" (\"->\" IDENT \":\" type)?\naction-attr ::= attribute-name \"=\" value\n-- target is first when present; remaining attributes are alphabetical.\n-- Target domains: selector operations (search_web, pick_up, browse_trending) accept entity-name; list operations (sort, extract) accept LIST[REF[STRING]]; selected-object operations (place, heat, chill, rinse, slice, pour, add_to_cart) accept REF[STRING]; check_replied accepts recipient-value; check_support_status accepts entity-name or REF[STRING]; code operations accept platform-name or entity-name; other signatures state their named target category.", "semantics": "Each Action accepts only signature-listed attributes whose values satisfy attribute-name's governing domain and provenance rule. Target is operation-specific: search_web and pick_up select an entity-name; sort and extract consume LIST[REF[STRING]]; place, heat, chill, rinse, slice, pour, and add_to_cart consume a selected REF[STRING]; check_replied consumes recipient-value; check_support_status consumes an entity-name or selected REF[STRING]. search_web additionally permits compatible_with=REF[STRING], meaning every returned candidate is compatible with that selected reference; compatible_with follows target and precedes other optional attributes alphabetically. STRING remains valid only for attributes explicitly marked unresolved N3 build-phase violations.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Add a motherboard under $200 and a compatible processor to the cart.", "braincode": "ACTION search_web(target=motherboard, currency=curr_usd, max_price=200) -> motherboards : LIST[REF[STRING]]\nACTION extract(target=motherboards, limit=1) -> motherboard_pick : REF[STRING]\nACTION add_to_cart(target=motherboard_pick)\nACTION search_web(target=processor, compatible_with=motherboard_pick) -> processors : LIST[REF[STRING]]\nACTION extract(target=processors, limit=1) -> processor_pick : REF[STRING]\nACTION add_to_cart(target=processor_pick)"}, "glossary_gloss": "Action performs an external operation using the value domains specified for that particular operation. Some operations select named kinds of things, while later operations consume selected references or lists of references.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Operation-specific target domains preserve ordinary selection pipelines while making target validation unambiguous. compatible_with expresses a relationship to the selected motherboard instead of an unqualified compatibility fact.", "cites_source": "Why Agentic Systems Need Ontologies", "addresses_note": "N3"}, {"op": "revise", "kind": "vocabulary", "construct_name": "attribute-name", "glossary_gloss": "This category indexes each legal attribute and the exact value domain it accepts. A category domain accepts only its glossary symbols or bindings retaining the required provenance; NUMBER, REF, and literal domains follow their stated type rules.", "closed": false, "members": [{"symbol": "target", "gloss": "Operation-specific Action target domain; Action signatures may require entity-name, recipient-value, REF[STRING], LIST[REF[STRING]], platform-name, or another named category. Generate target is artifact-value and UTTER target is artifact-value or entity-name.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "compatible_with", "gloss": "A selected REF[STRING] that a search result must be compatible with.", "nl_synonyms": ["compatible with"]}, {"symbol": "budget", "gloss": "A maximum monetary amount as NUMBER, interpreted with currency.", "nl_synonyms": ["budget", "spend up to", "maximum cost"]}, {"symbol": "currency", "gloss": "Currency from currency-value, used with monetary amounts including budget.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "stop_requirement", "gloss": "Required itinerary-stop class from entity-name, semantic-category-value, a provenance-valid binding, or a permitted homogeneous list thereof.", "nl_synonyms": []}], "membership_rule": "All existing members retain their current stated domains. Attributes not listed as unresolved N3 violations must name a category, typed reference domain, NUMBER, BOOL, ISO_DATE, UTC_TIMESTAMP, or another glossary-defined literal rule; STRING alone is never a governed category domain.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Search for processors compatible with the selected motherboard.", "braincode": "ACTION search_web(target=processor, compatible_with=motherboard_pick) -> processors : LIST[REF[STRING]]"}, "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "This corrects the false claim that all Action targets are entity-name values and adds explicit governed entries for compatibility and budget.", "addresses_note": "N3"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Generate", "grammar": "generation ::= \"GENERATE\" \"(\" generate-attr (\",\" generate-attr)* \")\" \"->\" IDENT \":\" gen_type\n-- Legal attributes: target, audience, budget, content, currency, duration, duration_unit, format, locale, quantity, style, tone, stop_min_per_day, stop_requirement, max_walk_duration, max_walk_duration_unit.", "semantics": "GENERATE target requires artifact-value; audience requires recipient-value; tone, format, style, locale, currency, and duration units require their named categories; numeric constraints including budget require NUMBER. budget is the maximum total monetary cost in currency. stop_requirement accepts an entity-name symbol, semantic-category-value symbol, a provenance-valid binding in either category, or a homogeneous LIST[STRING] retaining one of those provenances. stop_min_per_day counts only stops matching stop_requirement. max_walk_duration bounds each consecutive pair of stops. target is first and optional attributes follow alphabetically. content remains an explicitly unresolved build-phase STRING channel.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Plan a three-day Kyoto itinerary with one temple per day, a 20-minute walking limit, and a $1,000 budget.", "braincode": "GENERATE(target=art_itinerary, budget=1000, content=\"Kyoto itinerary\", currency=curr_usd, duration=3, duration_unit=unit_day, max_walk_duration=20, max_walk_duration_unit=unit_minute, stop_min_per_day=1, stop_requirement=temple) -> plan : STRING"}, "glossary_gloss": "Generate creates a pure artifact using governed descriptive values and numeric constraints. It can state what qualifies as a required itinerary stop and the artifact's maximum monetary budget.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "This replaces the undefined phrase entity-name-compatible binding with provenance validation and adds the structured monetary constraint required for itinerary planning.", "cites_source": "HTML", "addresses_note": "N3"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Utterance", "grammar": "utterance ::= \"UTTER\" speech-act \"(\" utter-attr (\",\" utter-attr)* \")\" (\"->\" IDENT)?\nutter-attr ::= attribute-name \"=\" value\n-- Legal attributes: audience, budget, content, currency, recipient, target, tone.", "semantics": "UTTER attributes are alphabetical. recipient and audience require recipient-value; tone requires tone-value; target requires artifact-value or entity-name according to the utterance intent; budget requires NUMBER and currency requires currency-value. In a REVISES turn, an UTTER with target=art_itinerary and budget/currency replaces the prior lineage's itinerary budget constraint under Conversation's whole-body replacement rule. content remains a transitional STRING payload and an explicit unresolved N3 violation.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Revise the itinerary budget to 1 million Chilean pesos.", "braincode": "CONVO ReviseItalyBudget {\n  TURN t1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER inform(content=\"Italy itinerary\", recipient=role_agent, target=art_itinerary)\n  }\n  TURN t2 SPEAKER=USER REVISES t1 {\n    UTTER inform(budget=1000000, currency=curr_clp, recipient=role_agent, target=art_itinerary)\n  }\n}"}, "glossary_gloss": "Utterance records a speech act and can carry structured monetary constraints about a named target. A revision turn replaces the earlier request body, so its budget becomes the current itinerary budget.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Conversation already provides deterministic revision linkage through REVISES; adding governed target, budget, and currency attributes lets a budget revision be expressed structurally instead of only in content.", "cites_source": "Why Agentic Systems Need Ontologies", "addresses_note": "N3"}, {"op": "revise", "kind": "vocabulary", "construct_name": "currency-value", "glossary_gloss": "Canonical currency-code symbols for monetary attributes such as currency and budget. Each symbol provides the unit for a numeric monetary amount.", "closed": false, "members": [{"symbol": "curr_usd", "gloss": "United States dollar.", "nl_synonyms": ["$", "usd", "dollars"]}, {"symbol": "curr_eur", "gloss": "Euro.", "nl_synonyms": ["eur", "euros"]}, {"symbol": "curr_clp", "gloss": "Chilean peso.", "nl_synonyms": ["clp", "Chilean pesos", "Chilean peso"]}], "membership_rule": "An atomic symbol matching curr_[a-z]{3} naming an ISO-4217-style currency code. A currency-valued binding is valid only when it retains currency-value provenance.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Set a budget of 1 million Chilean pesos.", "braincode": "UTTER inform(budget=1000000, currency=curr_clp, recipient=role_agent, target=art_itinerary)"}, "change_type": "MINOR", "rationale": "curr_clp is required to represent the exposed budget request with a glossary-defined currency symbol.", "addresses_note": "N3"}, {"op": "add", "kind": "vocabulary", "construct_name": "semantic-category-value", "glossary_gloss": "Canonical symbols for semantic classes used as structured constraints, such as a class of place or item rather than one specific entity.", "closed": false, "members": [{"symbol": "class_temple", "gloss": "A temple as an itinerary-stop class.", "nl_synonyms": ["temple", "temples"]}, {"symbol": "class_town", "gloss": "A town as a place class.", "nl_synonyms": ["town", "towns"]}], "membership_rule": "An atomic symbol matching class_[a-z_]+ that names a semantic class usable where an attribute explicitly permits semantic-category-value. Members are added to the glossary before use.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Require one temple-class stop each day.", "braincode": "GENERATE(target=art_itinerary, content=\"Kyoto itinerary\", stop_min_per_day=1, stop_requirement=class_temple) -> plan : STRING"}, "change_type": "MINOR", "rationale": "A semantic class distinguishes a required kind of itinerary stop from a particular entity and gives stop_requirement a complete, look-upable alternative domain.", "addresses_note": "N3"}], "_provider_used": "openai", "_model_used": "gpt-5.6-terra"}
Critic assessment: {"decision": "needs-rework", "kpi_assessment": {"coverage": {"impact": "benefit", "reasoning": "Operation-specific targets preserve previously difficult selected-object pipelines: add_to_cart can consume a selected reference, sort/extract can consume lists, and compatible_with preserves the motherboard/processor dependency. Structured budget, currency, and itinerary-stop constraints improve coverage for the exposed planning requests, although software and prose-content attributes remain transitional STRING channels."}, "expressivity": {"impact": "benefit", "reasoning": "The proposal adds previously missing structured meaning for compatibility, monetary budgets, currency, stop classes, and revision of itinerary budget constraints. The simulations still require prose STRING payloads for code details and open-content requests, so the improvement is real but incomplete under N3/N4."}, "determinism": {"impact": "mixed", "reasoning": "Canonical target-first/alphabetical attribute ordering and provenance validation improve convergence for Action and Generate. However, provenance unions are permitted without a grammar for declaring them, entity-name and semantic-category-value overlap on generic classes such as temple, and most Action signatures remain referenced rather than exhaustively specified; translators therefore still have multiple plausible encodings."}, "interpretability": {"impact": "mixed", "reasoning": "A reader can now distinguish a named entity selector, selected REF, and list-of-REF target in important shopping and embodied pipelines. But the new static provenance system is underspecified syntactically, and the semantic-category-value category duplicates the current entity-name definition of class-like nouns, making category choice difficult to recover reliably."}, "improvement": {"impact": "benefit", "reasoning": "The structured shopping and itinerary expressions give a weaker agent useful executable constraints that prose content or an unqualified compatibility metric would hide. This is especially clear in the processor-compatible-with-selected-motherboard and Chilean-peso budget cases."}}, "simulations": [{"item_id": "dev-embodied-2", "source": "seed_tasks", "nl": "Put both pillows from the sofa onto the armchair, one at a time.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT MovePillows\nTASK MovePillows {\n  ACTION pick_up(target=pillow, source=sofa) -> pillow_1 : REF[STRING]\n  ACTION place(target=pillow_1, destination=armchair, relation=on)\n  ACTION pick_up(target=pillow, source=sofa) -> pillow_2 : REF[STRING]\n  ACTION place(target=pillow_2, destination=armchair, relation=on)\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "The revised Action target domains help: pick_up selects entity-name and place consumes REF[STRING]. The exact necessity of relation=on remains a canonicalization question."}, {"item_id": "dev-embodied-1", "source": "seed_tasks", "nl": "Rinse the mug in the sink, then put it in the coffee maker.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT RinseThenPlace\nTASK RinseThenPlace {\n  ACTION pick_up(target=mug) -> mug_ref : REF[STRING]\n  ACTION rinse(target=mug_ref, destination=sink)\n  ACTION place(target=mug_ref, destination=coffee_maker, relation=in)\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "Selected-object target validation is directly useful for rinse and place."}, {"item_id": "86afd67c-1bff-455c-baa7-e18dcb64b0f3", "source": "Mind2Web", "nl": "Find comedy tv shows on netflix sorted by audience score.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT FindComedyShows\nTASK FindComedyShows : LIST[REF[STRING]] {\n  ACTION search_web(target=tv_show, genre=genre_comedy, platform=netflix) -> tv_shows : LIST[REF[STRING]]\n  ACTION sort(target=tv_shows, rank_direction=dir_desc, rank_field=rank_rating) -> sorted_tv_shows : LIST[REF[STRING]]\n  RETURN sorted_tv_shows\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "The list target for sort is covered. rank_rating is the available proxy for audience score; a dedicated audience-score ranking distinction would be needed if that distinction matters."}, {"item_id": "bf469f30-6628-4017-b963-672645d7feab", "source": "Mind2Web", "nl": "Find the highest rated dog collar under 10 dollar.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT FindDogCollar\nTASK FindDogCollar : LIST[REF[STRING]] {\n  ACTION search_web(target=dog_collar, currency=curr_usd, max_price=10) -> collars : LIST[REF[STRING]]\n  ACTION sort(target=collars, rank_direction=dir_desc, rank_field=rank_rating) -> sorted_collars : LIST[REF[STRING]]\n  RETURN sorted_collars\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "Currency and numeric maximum are governed. The proposal's operation-specific list target makes the sorting stage well typed."}, {"item_id": "trial_T20190907_223758_523581#1", "source": "ALFRED", "nl": "Microwave a slice of apple and place it on the table.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT MicrowaveApple\nTASK MicrowaveApple {\n  ACTION pick_up(target=apple) -> apple_ref : REF[STRING]\n  ACTION slice(target=apple_ref) -> apple_slice : REF[STRING]\n  ACTION heat(target=apple_slice, destination=microwave) -> heated_slice : REF[STRING]\n  ACTION place(target=heated_slice, destination=table, relation=on)\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "The proposed selected-object domains support slice, heat, and place. The complete return and optional-attribute signature of heat must still be stated normatively."}, {"item_id": "trial_T20190908_224836_919680#2", "source": "ALFRED", "nl": "Put a microwaved slice of apple on the table in the corner.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT PutMicrowavedApple\nTASK PutMicrowavedApple {\n  ACTION pick_up(target=apple, state=state_microwaved) -> apple_slice : REF[STRING]\n  ACTION place(target=apple_slice, destination=table, location=corner, relation=on)\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "The existing state-value category captures the requested object state; selected-object placement is covered by the Action revision."}, {"item_id": "scikit-learn__scikit-learn-11578", "source": "SWE-bench_Verified", "nl": "Fix LogisticRegressionCV probabilistic scoring so the LogisticRegression constructed in _log_reg_scoring_path inherits multi_class, and test the correction.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT FixLogisticRegressionScoring\nTASK FixLogisticRegressionScoring {\n  ACTION modify_code(target=sklearn, file=\"sklearn/linear_model/logistic.py\", method=\"_log_reg_scoring_path\", revision=\"construct LogisticRegression with multi_class=multi_class and preserve relevant constructor parameters\")\n  ACTION run_tests(target=sklearn, content=\"test multinomial probabilistic scorer behavior\")\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "The code-file, method, revision, and test details remain explicitly unresolved STRING attributes. The Action proposal does not add the missing code-value categories."}, {"item_id": "django__django-13112", "source": "SWE-bench_Verified", "nl": "Fix makemigrations crashing for a ForeignKey with a mixed-case Django app name.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT FixMixedCaseMigration\nTASK FixMixedCaseMigration {\n  ACTION modify_code(target=django, file=\"django/db/models/fields/related.py\", revision=\"preserve mixed-case installed app names when resolving lazy ForeignKey references\")\n  ACTION run_tests(target=django, content=\"mixed-case app-name ForeignKey migration regression test\")\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "The actionable software-fix core is represented only through transitional STRING fields; N3-compliant code path, code change, and assertion categories are still absent."}, {"item_id": "c6516", "source": "PRISM", "nl": "Can you give me some great places to visit in the Netherlands?", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT SuggestNetherlandsPlaces\nCONVO SuggestNetherlandsPlaces {\n  TURN t1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER ask(content=\"recommend great places to visit in the Netherlands\", recipient=role_agent)\n  }\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "The speech act and recipient are structured, but the request substance remains an unresolved content STRING, violating N3 and N4."}, {"item_id": "c3388", "source": "PRISM", "nl": "hi how hs your day been?", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT PainConversation\nCONVO PainConversation {\n  TURN t1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER ask(content=\"how has your day been\", recipient=role_agent)\n  }\n  TURN t2 SPEAKER=USER REPLY_TO t1 {\n    UTTER correct(content=\"ask how my day has been\", recipient=role_agent)\n  }\n  TURN t3 SPEAKER=USER REPLY_TO t2 {\n    UTTER inform(content=\"my day has been boring and I have been in pain for nearly three years\", recipient=role_agent)\n  }\n  TURN t4 SPEAKER=USER REPLY_TO t3 {\n    UTTER ask(content=\"tell me a joke to distract me from pain\", recipient=role_agent)\n  }\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "Conversation sequencing is represented, but all conversational substance remains prose content rather than glossary values."}, {"item_id": "c2067", "source": "PRISM", "nl": "Can you breifly explain American politics to me?", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT AmericanPoliticsConversation\nCONVO AmericanPoliticsConversation {\n  TURN t1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER ask(content=\"briefly explain American politics\", recipient=role_agent, tone=tone_concise)\n  }\n  TURN t2 SPEAKER=USER REPLY_TO t1 {\n    UTTER ask(content=\"why is the Supreme Court part of government\", recipient=role_agent)\n  }\n  TURN t3 SPEAKER=USER REPLY_TO t2 {\n    UTTER ask(content=\"are Supreme Court justices elected by the people\", recipient=role_agent)\n  }\n  TURN t4 SPEAKER=USER REPLY_TO t3 {\n    UTTER ask(content=\"if appointed by the president, how is that fair\", recipient=role_agent)\n  }\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "The follow-up sequence and concise-register request are captured, but political content is an unresolved STRING payload."}, {"item_id": "c2904", "source": "PRISM", "nl": "Tell me about what is happening in New Zealand Politically", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT CurrentAotearoaPolitics\nCONVO CurrentAotearoaPolitics {\n  TURN t1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER ask(content=\"explain current political developments in New Zealand\", recipient=role_agent)\n  }\n  TURN t2 SPEAKER=USER REPLY_TO t1 {\n    UTTER ask(content=\"state whether your information is current\", recipient=role_agent)\n  }\n  TURN t3 SPEAKER=USER REVISES t1 {\n    UTTER ask(content=\"explain current political events in Aotearoa for 2023\", recipient=role_agent)\n  }\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "REVISES captures the correction structure, but its substantive time/place/topic constraints remain prose content."}, {"item_id": "wildchat1m_en3u-73283", "source": "PATHs", "nl": "Introduce the topic of the impact of dam construction on seasonal precipitation pattern, land use/cover, and socio-economic conditions in semi-arid Eastern Sudan.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT IntroduceDamTopic\nTASK IntroduceDamTopic : STRING {\n  GENERATE(target=art_technical_explanation, content=\"introduce impacts of dam construction on seasonal precipitation, land use cover, and socio-economic conditions in semi-arid Eastern Sudan\") -> introduction : STRING\n  RETURN introduction\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "Artifact type is governed, but the requested subject matter remains an explicit unresolved content STRING."}, {"item_id": "wildchat1m_en3u-76731", "source": "PATHs", "nl": "Try to squeeze some Holy Blood and Holy Grail-like conspiracy ideas from The Brothers Karamazov.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT CompareLiteraryThemes\nCONVO CompareLiteraryThemes {\n  TURN t1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER ask(content=\"identify Holy Blood and Holy Grail-like conspiracy themes in The Brothers Karamazov\", recipient=role_agent)\n  }\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "The request is a question to the agent, but its literary entities and comparison relation have no symbolic content encoding."}, {"item_id": "wildchat1m_en3u-137561", "source": "PATHs", "nl": "Write a story of when Gloopy and Glitter met the scientist, who is amazed they can speak.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT WriteGloopiterMeeting\nTASK WriteGloopiterMeeting : STRING {\n  GENERATE(target=art_story, content=\"story in which Gloopy and Glitter meet a scientist who is amazed that they can speak\") -> story : STRING\n  RETURN story\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "The requested artifact is structured as art_story, but characters, events, and the premise remain prose and violate N3/N4."}, {"item_id": "wildchat1m_en3u-78000", "source": "PATHs", "nl": "Add a binding signature line to the speaker-to-client high-demand speaking-engagement contract.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT ReviseSpeakerContract\nTASK ReviseSpeakerContract : STRING {\n  GENERATE(target=art_contract, content=\"speaker-to-client high-demand speaking engagement contract including a binding client signature and date line\") -> contract : STRING\n  RETURN contract\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "The artifact target is governed under the assumed complete glossary, but contractual parties, obligations, and signature requirement remain unresolved prose content."}, {"item_id": "1776009617894", "source": "ThoughtTrace", "nl": "Can you suggest a posting schedule to help the Hamoudi educational YouTube channel grow quickly in its first month?", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT PlanHamoudiSchedule\nTASK PlanHamoudiSchedule : STRING {\n  GENERATE(target=art_plan, content=\"first-month posting schedule for the Hamoudi educational YouTube channel, optimized for growth\") -> schedule : STRING\n  RETURN schedule\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "Planning artifact structure is present, but channel identity, publishing cadence, and growth goal are all hidden in content STRING."}, {"item_id": "1775429344935", "source": "ThoughtTrace", "nl": "Help me to create a text about my decision to go to Japan to learn about SGI. Only 100 words.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT WriteJapanSGIText\nTASK WriteJapanSGIText : STRING {\n  GENERATE(target=art_short_text, content=\"personal decision to travel to Japan to learn about SGI\", quantity=100) -> text : STRING\n  RETURN text\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "quantity=100 has no stated word-count unit or semantics, so the exact 100-word constraint is not preserved; the topic also remains prose content."}, {"item_id": "1775752130482", "source": "ThoughtTrace", "nl": "Hello, I want to plan a 3 day safari in eastern cape south africa.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT PlanEasternCapeSafari\nTASK PlanEasternCapeSafari : STRING {\n  GENERATE(target=art_itinerary, content=\"safari in Eastern Cape South Africa\", duration=3, duration_unit=unit_day) -> itinerary : STRING\n  RETURN itinerary\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "Duration and its unit are governed, but the destination and safari subject remain content STRING rather than structured location/category values."}, {"item_id": "1775479438859", "source": "ThoughtTrace", "nl": "Gain a short certification if it opens a stable field: examples in UK context?", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT SuggestUKCertifications\nTASK SuggestUKCertifications : STRING {\n  GENERATE(target=art_plan, content=\"short UK certifications that can lead to stable mortgage-friendly employment\") -> recommendations : STRING\n  RETURN recommendations\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "The answer artifact is represented, but jurisdiction, certification concepts, job fields, and mortgage criterion are all prose payload."}], "cross_check": {"used": true, "agreement": "partial", "notes": "Independent translators agree on the broad embodied and shopping decompositions, but differ on whether place requires relation=on, whether the apple slice receives a new REF handle, whether an open request is TASK/GENERATE or CONVO/UTTER, and whether a software request is a gap or transitional STRING-based Action. Gemini also orders sort attributes contrary to the proposed alphabetical rule, demonstrating that the canonicalization rule is not yet reliably followed."}, "required_changes": [{"target": "Types & Lexicon", "change": "Define a concrete grammar and static-checking syntax for provenance-qualified bindings and finite provenance unions, or remove union acceptance until it has such syntax. Specify how provenance is assigned to direct symbols, retained through LET, list literals, Action/Generate results, parameters, and task-call results; an unannotated STRING must never acquire provenance."}, {"target": "Action", "change": "Publish a complete normative signature table for every existing operation, including legal attributes, required/optional status, each attribute domain, result type/provenance, and canonical order. Replace the non-normative statement that unspecified operations 'state their named target category'; it currently leaves heat, slice, rinse, place, modify_code, run_tests, and existing search operations underspecified."}, {"target": "semantic-category-value", "change": "Eliminate the overlap with entity-name or define a non-overlapping boundary. The current entity-name rule already defines class-like nouns such as temple, while semantic-category-value also defines class_temple; specify one canonical category and encoding rule for generic classes versus concrete named entities, then revise all itinerary examples consistently."}, {"target": "attribute-name", "change": "Add individually indexed entries and exact domains for every remaining attribute used by all constructs and Action signatures, including max_price, min_rating, rank_field, rank_direction, limit, availability, genre, category, platform, deadline, date, and all code-operation attributes. Mark only deliberately transitional STRING attributes as N3 violations and name their replacement category."}, {"target": "Generate", "change": "Define quantity's measurement semantics or add a governed unit/value mechanism for output length. In particular, state how an exact word-count requirement is encoded so quantity=100 cannot ambiguously mean characters, sections, items, or words."}, {"target": "basis", "change": "Continue the N3 build by replacing the remaining STRING-governed attribute channels with named glossary categories, start