Proposed changes available for this translation:
- [revise] Types & Lexicon: grammar `type ::= "NUMBER" | "STRING" [ "<" category-id ">" ] | "BOOL" | "LIST" "[" type "]" | "REF" "[" type "]"
category-id ::= a glossary vocabulary-category name
value ::= STRING | NUMBER | IDENT | "TRUE" | "FALSE" | list_lit | diff
list_lit ::= "[" value ("," value)* "]"` — A direct glossary symbol has static type STRING<its-category>; an unquoted symbol may belong to exactly one category in the position where it is used. A quoted STRING literal has type STRING with no category qualifier. LET, parameters, Task returns, task-call results, and Action/Generate results retain their declared type exactly. A list literal has type LIST[T] only when every member has exactly T; thus LIST[STRING<entity-name>] is distinct from LIST[STRING<semantic-category-value>]. No provenance unions exist. A category-governed attribute accepts only a direct member, or a binding/list whose declared type exactly matches its permitted category domain. REF[STRING] and LIST[REF[STRING]] never carry descriptive-category provenance.
- [revise] Action: grammar `action ::= "ACTION" operation-id "(" action-attr ("," action-attr)* ")" ("->" IDENT ":" type)?
action-attr ::= attribute-name "=" value
-- target is first; all remaining attributes are alphabetical.
-- E=STRING<entity-name>; R=STRING<recipient-value>; P=STRING<platform-name>; F=REF[STRING]; L=LIST[REF[STRING]].` — Every Action signature below is normative; bracketed attributes are optional and all unbracketed attributes are required. Results have no descriptive provenance. Selector signatures are: search_web(target:E,[availability:search-value,category:search-value,color:product-attribute-value,compatible_with:F,currency:currency-value,genre:search-value,gender:product-attribute-value,location:location-name,max_price:NUMBER,min_rating:NUMBER,platform:P,shape:shape-value,size:product-attribute-value,trending:BOOL])->L; search_travel(origin:location-name,destination:location-name,date:ISO_DATE)->L; search_transit(origin:location-name,destination:location-name,date:ISO_DATE)->L; browse_trending(target:E,[location:location-name])->L; pick_up(target:E,[color:product-attribute-value,shape:shape-value,source:E|location-name,state:state-value])->F; extract(target:L,limit:NUMBER)->F; sort(target:L,rank_direction:search-value,rank_field:search-value)->L. Selected-object signatures are: place(target:F,destination:E|location-name,[location:location-name,relation:spatial-relation,state:state-value]); heat(target:F,[destination:E|location-name])->F; chill(target:F,[destination:E|location-name])->F; rinse(target:F,[destination:E|location-name])->F; slice(target:F)->F; pour(target:F,destination:E|location-name); add_to_cart(target:F); save_favorite(target:F). Page/form signatures are: open_page(target:E|P); click(target:E); read_page(target:E|P)->STRING; fill_field(target:E,content:content-value|STRING); submit(target:E). Communication/status signatures are: send_email(recipient:R,[content:content-value|STRING,tone:tone-value]); send_message(recipient:R,[content:content-value|STRING,tone:tone-value]); call(recipient:R,[content:content-value|STRING]); check_replied(target:R,deadline:UTC_TIMESTAMP)->BOOL; check_support_status(target:E|F,metric:metric-value)->BOOL; offer_discount(target:E|F,[content:content-value|STRING,discount_percent:NUMBER]); offer_expedited_shipping(target:E|F). List signatures are: create_list(target:E)->F; add_to_list(target:F,content:content-value|STRING); file_age_days(file:code-value)->NUMBER; path_in_folder(file:code-value,source:code-value)->BOOL. Code signatures are: modify_code(target:P|E,file:code-value,[method:code-value,property:code-value,call:code-value,assertion:code-value,expected_output:code-value,actual_output:code-value,reproduction_step:code-value,revision:code-value]); run_tests(target:P|E,[assertion:code-value,content:content-value|STRING]); add_file(target:P|E,file:code-value,[content:content-value|STRING]); delete_file(target:P|E,file:code-value). custom_<domain>_<action> requires target:E and may use only content:content-value|STRING. STRING alternatives named above are deliberate transitional N3 violations; all other attributes are category or literal governed.
- [revise vocabulary] attribute-name (open): This category indexes every legal attribute and its exact value domain. Category domains require their category-qualified symbol or binding; only entries explicitly marked transitional may still accept plain STRING. Members: target, recipient, audience, tone, format, style, locale, content, quantity, quantity_unit, duration, duration_unit, max_walk_duration, max_walk_duration_unit, stop_min_per_day, stop_requirement, budget, currency, availability, category, genre, rank_field, rank_direction, max_price, min_rating, limit, color, size, gender, shape, state, platform, location, origin, destination, source, relation, compatible_with, date, deadline, metric, discount_percent, trending, file, method, property, call, assertion, expected_output, actual_output, reproduction_step, revision; membership rule: This index is exhaustive for attributes currently used by BrainCode constructs and Action signatures. NUMBER, BOOL, ISO_DATE, UTC_TIMESTAMP, REF[STRING], and LIST[REF[STRING]] are glossary-defined literal or type domains; all remaining domains name a vocabulary category. New attributes must be added here with one exact domain before use.
- [revise] Generate: grammar `generation ::= "GENERATE" "(" generate-attr ("," generate-attr)* ")" "->" IDENT ":" gen_type
-- Legal attributes: target, audience, budget, content, currency, duration, duration_unit, format, locale, quantity, quantity_unit, style, tone, stop_min_per_day, stop_requirement, max_walk_duration, max_walk_duration_unit.` — GENERATE target requires artifact-value; audience requires recipient-value; tone, format, style, locale, currency, and units require their named categories; numeric constraints require NUMBER. quantity is exact only when paired with quantity_unit: quantity_unit=unit_word means exactly that many whitespace-delimited output words after rendering; quantity_unit=unit_item means exactly that many top-level generated items. budget is the maximum total monetary cost in currency. stop_requirement requires semantic-category-value, and stop_min_per_day counts only matching class members. content requires content-value; plain STRING remains a transitional build-phase alternative until N4 closes it. Target is first and all remaining attributes are alphabetical.
- [revise] Utterance: grammar `utterance ::= "UTTER" speech-act "(" utter-attr ("," utter-attr)* ")" ("->" IDENT)?
utter-attr ::= attribute-name "=" value
-- Legal attributes: audience, budget, content, currency, recipient, target, tone.` — UTTER attributes are alphabetical. recipient and audience require recipient-value; tone requires tone-value; target requires entity-name or artifact-value according to the speech act; budget is NUMBER and currency is currency-value. content requires content-value; a STRING content value remains an explicit transitional N3 violation until the closing phase. A bound UTTER result is STRING and must use content, content_2, content_3, and so on.
- [revise vocabulary] semantic-category-value (open): Semantic-category-value names an abstract class used as a constraint, not a selectable physical entity. It is the only canonical category for generic itinerary-stop requirements. Members: class_temple, class_town; membership rule: Members match class_[a-z_]+ and denote abstract semantic classes only. Use entity-name for a selectable object or place kind in an Action target; use semantic-category-value for a generic qualification such as stop_requirement. A translator must encode a generic requested stop class as class_<name>, never as an entity-name.
- [revise vocabulary] entity-name (open): Entity-name names a selectable kind of object, place, service, or artifact participant. It is used to select or act on a thing, not to express an abstract semantic class constraint. Members: mug, apple, temple, coffee_maker; membership rule: An entity-name is an atomic [a-z_]+ symbol denoting a selectable entity kind. It is valid for Action selector targets and destinations where the signature permits it. It is never valid for stop_requirement; generic class constraints use semantic-category-value.
- [add vocabulary] code-value (open): Code-value supplies atomic symbols for code paths, program symbols, requested changes, test assertions, and code outcomes. Its prefixes determine which code attribute may use a member. Members: path_sklearn_linear_model_logistic_py, sym_log_reg_scoring_path, chg_inherit_multi_class, assert_multinomial_scorer; membership rule: Members are atomic symbols with exactly one prefix: path_ for file/source paths, sym_ for methods/classes/functions/properties, call_ for call patterns, chg_ for requested code changes, assert_ for test assertions, outcome_ for expected or actual outcomes, and repro_ for reproduction cases. file and source require path_; method/property require sym_; call requires call_; revision requires chg_; assertion requires assert_; expected_output/actual_output require outcome_; reproduction_step requires repro_.
- [add vocabulary] content-value (open): Content-value names an atomic structured topic, request substance, message purpose, or artifact premise. It replaces prose content where the required symbolic member exists. Members: content_status_update, content_kyoto_itinerary, content_decision_study_sgi_japan, content_mixed_case_foreign_key_regression; membership rule: Members match content_[a-z_]+ and each names one glossary-defined atomic proposition, topic, request purpose, premise, or message substance. A translator uses a listed canonical member or adds a member through a future vocabulary sprint; it may not place a phrase inside content when N4 closes transitional STRING.
