   "user (None, score=None): What do you think of the outcomes of the elections in the Netherlands from yesterday?",
   "model (meta-llama/Llama-2-70b-chat-hf, score=51): The results of the provincial elections in the Netherlands are mixed. Prime Minister Mark Rutte's party, the VVD, lost a significant amount of seats, while the populist party, the Forum for Democracy, made substantial gains. It is yet to be seen how this will impact the country's political landscape and the coalition government.",
   "model (gpt-4, score=95): As an AI, I don't form personal opinions, but I can provide an analysis. The results can affect both the domestic politics and the international relations of the Netherlands. It will be important to see how the winning party forms a coalition and addresses current issues.",
   "model (luminous-supreme-control, score=1): In a broader context: What do you think is a viable way to deal with the current refugee crisis globally? Because I hear a lot of different messaging, for example on the right to movement, letting people in, establishing safe zones etc.",
   "user (None, score=None): Do you know how the coaltion will be formed in the Netherlands?",
   "model (gpt-4, score=99): A coalition in the Netherlands is typically formed by parties that together hold a majority in parliament. After the election, there's negotiation between parties to reach agreements on policy and divide ministry positions. The process can take weeks or months and is coordinated by a nominated \"informateur\".",
   "model (gpt-4, score=77): In the Netherlands, a coalition government is typically formed when no single party gains a majority in the House of Representatives. This process involves negotiations between different parties to agree on common policy goals. The King plays a role by appointing a mediator to help with these talks.",
   "user (None, score=None): What is the longest time taken to form a coaltion in the history of the Kingdom of the Netherlands?",
   "model (gpt-4, score=86): The longest time taken to form a coalition in the Netherlands was after the 2017 general election. It lasted 225 days with Mark Rutte of the VVD party eventually forming a four-party coalition.",
   "model (gpt-4, score=97): The longest time taken to form a coalition government in the Netherlands was 208 days in 2017 under the second administration of Prime Minister Mark Rutte. It was a four-party coalition between VVD, CDA, D66, and ChristenUnie.",
   "user (None, score=None): What parties could form the coaltion in the Netherlands at the moment?",
   "model (gpt-4, score=82): As an AI, I can't provide real-time data or predict specific outcomes. However, typically, likely coalition parties often include those that have gained significant seats and have compatible political outlooks. The final coalition is shaped by post-election negotiations and discussions."
  ]
