its prefix requirement is not stated in the signature table; target=file_system and other target symbols need declared category domains. Define whether operation result references retain any category provenance or are intentionally REF[STRING] only."}, {"target": "basis", "change": "Keep the three accepted additive vocabulary changes, then continue N3 by replacing the remaining explicit content:...|STRING alternatives and other transitional STRING-governed attributes with category domains. Add composable content/constraint categories rather than relying solely on one opaque content-value atom for source preservation, revision constraints, structured transformations, and open-request details."}], "logic_issues": ["The proposal's Action semantics calls its signature table normative and exhaustive, but it omits multiple operations currently listed in operation-vocabulary and previously expressible in the language. This regresses build-phase coverage.", "The proposed Action signatures use P|E and E|location-name union notation, but Types & Lexicon defines no union grammar or static semantics and expressly says no provenance unions exist.", "category-id is described only as a glossary vocabulary-category name. Existing category names such as semantic-category-value contain hyphens, while the grammar supplies no category-id lexical rule.", "The entity-name revision's displayed member set would replace rather than preserve the existing category's members unless explicitly stated otherwise, invalidating numerous current expressions and sampled household tasks.", "The new attribute-name index claims to be exhaustive but omits current legacy attributes and does not reconcile the old Action attribute ordering with its revised table.", "The content-value worked example emits content_gloopy_glitter_meet_scientist, which is not among its listed members; this is permissible only under N6's assumed-complete-glossary direction, but the proposal should state that examples may use assumed members rather than appearing as a closed current list."], "notes_assessment": {"N2": {"status": "violated", "trend": "regressed", "reasoning": "The proposal adds useful descriptive categories, but its exhaustive Action replacement and abbreviated entity-name revision remove previously expressible operations and entity symbols during the mandated no-coverage-reduction build phase."}, "N3": {"status": "violated", "trend": "improved", "reasoning": "Category-qualified bindings, individually indexed domains, code-value, content-value, semantic-category-value, and unit_word/unit_item are substantial progress. N3 remains violated because Action, Generate, and Utterance explicitly retain content-value|STRING channels, and several other legacy STRING channels are not governed by this revision."}, "N7": {"status": "violated", "trend": "improved", "reasoning": "Qualified categories and canonical attribute ordering improve deterministic typing and encoding. Canonical expressions are still not assured: type unions are undefined, handle names remain unconstrained, independent translations diverge, and the revised Action surface is incomplete."}, "N1": {"status": "violated", "trend": "unchanged", "reasoning": "The build phase properly retains STRING, but expressions may still use free identifiers and quoted strings. The added categories do not close the vocabulary."}, "N4": {"status": "violated", "trend": "unchanged", "reasoning": "The proposal correctly labels prose STRING alternatives as transitional, but retains them in Action, Generate, and Utterance."}, "N5": {"status": "violated", "trend": "improved", "reasoning": "Atomic code-value and content-value symbols reduce dependence on English prose. STRING and broad free identifiers remain, so the language is not self-contained."}, "N6": {"status": "satisfied", "trend": "improved", "reasoning": "The new grammar is generally written over category names, and open categories with membership rules support assuming needed members