- [revise] Generate: grammar `generation ::= "GENERATE" "(" generate-attr ("," generate-attr)* ")" "->" IDENT ":" gen_type
-- Legal attributes: target, audience, budget, constraints, content, currency, duration, duration_unit, format, locale, quantity, quantity_unit, stop_min_per_day, stop_requirement, style, topic, transformations, tone.` — target requires STRING<artifact-value>; audience requires STRING<recipient-value>; tone, format, style, locale, currency, and units require their named categories; numeric constraints require NUMBER. constraints requires LIST[STRING<constraint-value>]; transformations requires LIST[STRING<transformation-value>]. topic requires STRING<topic-value>. content requires STRING<content-value>. quantity is exact only when paired with quantity_unit. budget is the maximum total monetary cost in currency. stop_requirement requires STRING<semantic-category-value>. Target is first; remaining attributes are alphabetical.
- [revise] Utterance: grammar `utterance ::= "UTTER" speech-act "(" utter-attr ("," utter-attr)* ")" ("->" IDENT)?
utter-attr ::= attribute-name "=" value
-- Legal attributes: audience, budget, constraints, content, currency, recipient, target, tone, topic.` — UTTER attributes are alphabetical. recipient and audience require STRING<recipient-value>; tone requires STRING<tone-value>; target requires STRING<entity-name> or STRING<artifact-value>; budget is NUMBER and currency is STRING<currency-value>. topic requires STRING<topic-value>; constraints requires LIST[STRING<constraint-value>]; content requires STRING<content-value>. A bound UTTER result is STRING and must use content, content_2, etc.
- [revise vocabulary] attribute-name (open): This category indexes every legal attribute and its exact value domain. All attributes now require their category-qualified symbol, list, or literal type. Members: target, recipient, audience, tone, format, style, locale, content, topic, constraints, transformations, quantity, quantity_unit, duration, duration_unit, max_walk_duration, max_walk_duration_unit, stop_min_per_day, stop_requirement, budget, currency, availability, category, genre, rank_field, rank_direction, max_price, min_rating, limit, color, size, gender, shape, state, platform, location, origin, destination, source, relation, compatible_with, date, deadline, metric, discount_percent, trending, file, method, property, call, assertion, expected_output, actual_output, reproduction_step, revision, ordering, procedure, food, meal, message_structure, policy, amenity, ticket, seat; membership rule: This index is exhaustive across all retained constructs and Action signatures. Legacy attributes use transitional STRING pending their dedicated categories. New attributes must be added here with one exact domain before use.
- [revise vocabulary] entity-name (open): Entity-name names a selectable kind of object, place, service, or artifact participant. It is used to select or act on a thing, not to express an abstract semantic class constraint. Members: mug, rag, tub, book, mirror, sink, counter, cupboard, refrigerator, spoon, bowl, pillow, sofa, armchair, toilet_paper, holder, wall, motherboard, processor, decorative_led_candles, cup, coffee_maker, microwave, toilet, apple, tv_show, dog_collar, box, keys, chair, file_system, store, temple; membership rule: An entity-name is an atomic [a-z_]+ symbol denoting a selectable entity kind. It is valid for Action selector targets and destinations where the signature permits it. It is never valid for stop_requirement; generic class constraints use semantic-category-value.
