      "semantics": "A bare glossary symbol has static type STRING<its-category>. Category provenance is resolved purely from the signature position: an unquoted symbol is assigned the category required by the parameter it binds to. This unambiguously resolves overlapping symbols (e.g., `call` as an operation vs. `call` as a code-value). ISO_DATE and UTC_TIMESTAMP are named types for date/time literal strings. A quoted STRING literal has type STRING with no category qualifier. LET, parameters, and results retain their declared type exactly. An attribute in an Action, Generate, or Utterance signature MUST declare a specific STRING<category-id> or base type; unqualified STRING is illegal for attributes, enforcing N3. A list literal has type LIST[T] only when every member has exactly T.",
