- [revise] Types & Lexicon: grammar `type ::= "NUMBER" | "STRING" [ "<" category-id ">" ] | "BOOL" | "LIST" "[" type "]" | "REF" "[" type "]" | "ISO_DATE" | "UTC_TIMESTAMP"
category-id ::= [a-z0-9-]+
value ::= STRING | NUMBER | IDENT | "TRUE" | "FALSE" | list_lit | diff
list_lit ::= "[" value ("," value)* "]"
diff ::= IDENT "-" NUMBER
IDENT ::= [a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z0-9_]*
STRING ::= "\"" ( [^"\\\n] | "\\\"" | "\\\\" | "\\n" | "\\t" | "\\u" HEXHEXHEXHEX )* "\""
COMMENT ::= "#" through end of physical line.` — A bare glossary symbol has static type STRING<its-category>. Category provenance is resolved purely from the signature position: an unquoted symbol is assigned the category required by the parameter it binds to. This unambiguously resolves overlapping symbols (e.g., `call` as an operation vs. `call` as a code-value). ISO_DATE and UTC_TIMESTAMP are named types for date/time literal strings. A quoted STRING literal has type STRING with no category qualifier. LET, parameters, and results retain their declared type exactly. An attribute in an Action, Generate, or Utterance signature MUST declare a specific STRING<category-id> or base type; unqualified STRING is illegal for attributes, enforcing N3. A list literal has type LIST[T] only when every member has exactly T.
