_unit", "gloss": "Duration unit.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "max_walk_duration", "gloss": "Numeric walking bound.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "max_walk_duration_unit", "gloss": "Walking-bound unit.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "max_prep_time", "gloss": "Max prep time.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "stop_min_per_day", "gloss": "Numeric daily minimum.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "stop_requirement", "gloss": "Required stop class.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "budget", "gloss": "Maximum monetary amount.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "currency", "gloss": "Monetary unit.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "availability", "gloss": "Availability.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "category", "gloss": "Search category.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "genre", "gloss": "Genre.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "rank_field", "gloss": "Ranking field.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "rank_direction", "gloss": "Ranking direction.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "max_price", "gloss": "Maximum price.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "min_rating", "gloss": "Minimum rating.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "limit", "gloss": "Maximum extracted count.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "color", "gloss": "Color.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "size", "gloss": "Size.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "gender", "gloss": "Demographic facet.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "shape", "gloss": "Shape.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "state", "gloss": "Condition.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "platform", "gloss": "Platform.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "location", "gloss": "Location.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "origin", "gloss": "Travel origin.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "destination", "gloss": "Destination.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "destinations", "gloss": "List of destinations.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "source", "gloss": "Source.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "relation", "gloss": "Spatial relation.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "relations", "gloss": "List of relations.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "date", "gloss": "Calendar date.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "deadline", "gloss": "Deadline.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "metric", "gloss": "Status metric.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "discount_percent", "gloss": "Percentage.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "trending", "gloss": "Boolean trending filter.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "file", "gloss": "Code path.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "method", "gloss": "Code symbol.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "property", "gloss": "Code symbol.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "call", "gloss": "Code-call symbol.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "assertion", "gloss": "Test assertion.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "expected_output", "gloss": "Expected code outcome.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "actual_output", "gloss": "Actual code outcome.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "reproduction_step", "gloss": "Reproduction case.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "revision", "gloss": "Code change.", "nl_synonyms": []}], "membership_rule": "This index is exhaustive across all retained constructs and Action/Generate/Utterance signatures. Legacy transitional STRING attributes have been removed to enforce N3. The exact domain is determined by the specific operation signature.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Find dog collars.", "braincode": "ACTION search_web(target=dog_collar) -> collars : LIST[REF[STRING]]"}, "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Removes transitional STRING attributes entirely to comply with N3. Defers exact domain typing to operation signatures. Adds compositional attributes for relations, participants, predicates, ingredients, and evidence.", "addresses_note": "N3"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Generate", "grammar": "generation ::= \"GENERATE\" \"(\" generate-attr (\",\" generate-attr)* \")\" \"->\" IDENT \":\" type\ngenerate-attr ::= attribute-name \"=\" value\n-- Legal attributes: target, audience, budget, character_properties, constraints, content, currency, dietary_restrictions, duration, duration_unit, events, evidence_requirements, format, ingredients, locale, max_prep_time, max_walk_duration, max_walk_duration_unit, participants, predicates, quantity, quantity_unit, stop_min_per_day, stop_requirement, style, tone, topic, transformations.", "semantics": "target requires STRING<artifact-value>; audience requires STRING<recipient-value>; tone, format, style, locale, currency, duration_unit, max_walk_duration_unit, and quantity_unit require their named categories; duration, quantity, max_walk_duration, max_prep_time, stop_min_per_day, budget require NUMBER. constraints requires LIST[STRING<constraint-value>]; transformations requires LIST[STRING<transformation-value>]; events requires LIST[STRING<event-value>]; character_properties requires LIST[STRING<character-property-value>]; participants requires LIST[STRING<participant-value>]; predicates requires LIST[STRING<predicate-value>]; evidence_requirements requires LIST[STRING<evidence-value>]; ingredients requires LIST[STRING<food-value>]; dietary_restrictions requires LIST[STRING<dietary-value>]. topic requires STRING<topic-value>. content requires STRING<content-value>. stop_requirement requires STRING<semantic-category-value>. Target is first; remaining attributes are alphabetical.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Plan a healthy light dinner.", "braincode": "GENERATE(target=art_meal_plan, dietary_restrictions=[dietary_healthy, dietary_light, dietary_satiating], ingredients=[food_simple_ingredients], max_prep_time=30, topic=topic_tonight_dinner) -> dinner_plan : STRING"}, "glossary_gloss": "Generate creates a non-effectful artifact with governed descriptive constraints, topics, events, food properties, and transformations.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Adds compositional lists for events, character_properties, dietary, ingredients, and evidence to improve expressivity and fully decompose requests.", "cites_source": "HTML", "addresses_note": "N3"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Utterance", "grammar": "utterance ::= \"UTTER\" speech-act \"(\" utter-attr (\",\" utter-attr)* \")\" (\"->\" IDENT)?\nutter-attr ::= attribute-name \"=\" value\n-- Legal attributes are determined by the specific speech-act signature.", "semantics": "UTTER signatures are normative. ask(topic:STRING<topic-value>,[constraints:LIST[STRING<constraint-value>],evidence_requirements:LIST[STRING<evidence-value>],participants:LIST[STRING<participant-value>],predicates:LIST[STRING<predicate-value>],recipient:STRING<recipient-value>,tone:STRING<tone-value>]); inform(topic:STRING<topic-value>,[constraints:LIST[STRING<constraint-value>],evidence_requirements:LIST[STRING<evidence-value>],participants:LIST[STRING<participant-value>],predicates:LIST[STRING<predicate-value>],recipient:STRING<recipient-value>,tone:STRING<tone-value>]); respond(topic:STRING<topic-value>,[recipient:STRING<recipient-value>]); propose(topic:STRING<topic-value>,[recipient:STRING<recipient-value>]); correct(topic:STRING<topic-value>,[recipient:STRING<recipient-value>]); acknowledge([recipient:STRING<recipient-value>]); confirm([recipient:STRING<recipient-value>,topic:STRING<topic-value>]); decline([recipient:STRING<recipient-value>,topic:STRING<topic-value>]). A bound UTTER result is STRING and must use content, content_2, etc.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Are androids better than iphones?", "braincode": "CONVO AskPhoneComparison {\n  TURN t1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER ask(participants=[part_android, part_iphone], predicates=[pred_better_than], topic=topic_phone_comparison)\n  }\n}"}, "glossary_gloss": "Utterance records a speech act using exact normative signatures for ask, inform, etc., with named recipients, tones, topics, and structured compositional attributes.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Defines exact legal attribute signatures for each speech act. Adds compositional participant, predicate, and evidence attributes to decompose complex propositions.", "cites_source": "Why Agentic Systems Need Ontologies", "addresses_note": "N3"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Task", "grammar": "document ::= entrypoint (task | conversation)*\nentrypoint ::= \"ENTRYPOINT\" IDENT\ntask ::= \"TASK\" IDENT param_list? (\"CONTEXT\" execution_date_param)? (\":\" type)? (\"DECREASES\" IDENT)? \"{\" step+ return_stmt? \"}\"\nexecution_date_param ::= \"execution_date\" \"=\" ISO_DATE\nparam_list ::= \"(\" param (\",\" param)* \")\"\nparam ::= IDENT \":\" type\nreturn_stmt ::= \"RETURN\" value\nstep ::= action | flow_if | flow_for | bind | task_call | utterance | generation\ntask_call ::= IDENT arg_list? ( \"->\" IDENT )?\narg_list ::= \"(\" arg (\",\" arg)* \")\"\narg ::= IDENT \"=\" value", "semantics": "A BrainCode document declares exactly one ENTRYPOINT naming a top-level TASK or CONVO. Canonical encoding rules for N7: (1) Mandatory document wrapping: every request must be wrapped in an ENTRYPOINT and corresponding block. (2) Handle naming: bound variable names derive from the target semantic name (e.g., `farms` for a list of farms). (3) Canonical Attribute Selection: Do not mint a single `topic` or `content` atom for a complex request. Decompose it: use `events` for narrative sequences; `character_properties` for traits; `constraints` for explicit rules; `participants` for entities involved in a claim; `predicates` for relations/comparisons between participants; `evidence_requirements` for source rules. Use `topic` ONLY for the high-level subject matter. Use `content` only for literal text payloads. `CONTEXT execution_date=ISO_DATE` is a translation-time-only annotation.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Go to the flight booking site, search flights from Tel Aviv to Berlin next Tuesday, and list the three cheapest options.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT BookFlight\n\nTASK BookFlight CONTEXT execution_date=\"2025-06-03\" : LIST[REF[STRING]] {\n  ACTION open_page(target=flight_booking_site)\n  ACTION search_travel(date=\"2025-06-10\", destination=berlin, origin=tel_aviv) -> raw_flights : LIST[REF[STRING]]\n  ACTION sort(rank_direction=dir_asc, rank_field=rank_price, target=raw_flights) -> sorted_flights : LIST[REF[STRING]]\n  ACTION extract(limit=3, target=sorted_flights) -> cheapest_flights : LIST[REF[STRING]]\n  RETURN cheapest_flights\n}"}, "glossary_gloss": "Task is a document-level container of steps. It enforces canonical encoding rules for document wrapping, handle naming, and attribute selection (decomposing propositions) to ensure deterministic translation.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Adds canonical encoding rules for N7 convergence, specifically the canonical selection rule for topic versus compositional attributes, as requested by the Critic. Fixes quoted strings in example.", "cites_source": null, "addresses_note": "N7"}, {"op": "add", "kind": "vocabulary", "construct_name": "predicate-value", "glossary_gloss": "Canonical symbols for relations, comparisons, and causal links between participants in a proposition.", "closed": false, "members": [{"symbol": "pred_better_than", "gloss": "Is superior to.", "nl_synonyms": ["better than"]}, {"symbol": "pred_causes", "gloss": "Causes or leads to.", "nl_synonyms": ["causes", "leads to"]}, {"symbol": "pred_transmits_to", "gloss": "Transmits to or spreads to.", "nl_synonyms": ["spreads", "transmits"]}, {"symbol": "pred_increases_pay", "gloss": "Results in higher compensation.", "nl_synonyms": ["paid more"]}], "membership_rule": "An atomic symbol matching `pred_[a-z_]+` naming a relational, comparative, causal, or temporal predicate.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Are androids better than iphones?", "braincode": "UTTER ask(participants=[part_android, part_iphone], predicates=[pred_better_than], topic=topic_phone_comparison)"}, "change_type": "MINOR", "rationale": "Provides a compositional construct for comparisons, causal relations, and temporal ordering, preventing complex propositions from being collapsed into a single topic atom.", "addresses_note": "N3"}, {"op": "add", "kind": "vocabulary", "construct_name": "participant-value", "glossary_gloss": "Canonical symbols for entities, body parts, concepts, or groups involved in a proposition or claim.", "closed": false, "members": [{"symbol": "part_android", "gloss": "Android phones.", "nl_synonyms": ["androids"]}, {"symbol": "part_iphone", "gloss": "Apple iPhones.", "nl_synonyms": ["iphones"]}, {"symbol": "part_foot_fungus", "gloss": "Foot fungus.", "nl_synonyms": ["fungo nos dedos do pé"]}, {"symbol": "part_foot", "gloss": "The foot.", "nl_synonyms": ["foot", "pé"]}, {"symbol": "part_other_body_part", "gloss": "Another body part.", "nl_synonyms": ["outra parte do corpo"]}, {"symbol": "part_qualifications", "gloss": "Educational or professional qualifications.", "nl_synonyms": ["qualifications"]}], "membership_rule": "An atomic symbol matching `part_[a-z_]+` naming a participant in a proposition.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Can foot fungus spread to another body part?", "braincode": "UTTER ask(participants=[part_foot_fungus, part_foot, part_other_body_part], predicates=[pred_transmits_to], topic=topic_fungus_transmission)"}, "change_type": "MINOR", "rationale": "Provides a compositional construct for participants and body parts in claims, preventing complex propositions from being collapsed into a single topic atom.", "addresses_note": "N3"}, {"op": "add", "kind": "vocabulary", "construct_name": "evidence-value", "glossary_gloss": "Canonical symbols for evidence, source, and recency requirements in claims or generated artifacts.", "closed": false, "members": [{"symbol": "evid_recent", "gloss": "Requires recent information.", "nl_synonyms": ["up to date", "current"]}, {"symbol": "evid_primary_source", "gloss": "Requires primary sources.", "nl_synonyms": ["primary sources"]}, {"symbol": "evid_peer_reviewed", "gloss": "Requires peer-reviewed evidence.", "nl_synonyms": ["peer reviewed"]}], "membership_rule": "An atomic symbol matching `evid_[a-z_]+` naming an evidence or source requirement.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Give me current events.", "braincode": "UTTER ask(evidence_requirements=[evid_recent], topic=topic_events)"}, "change_type": "MINOR", "rationale": "Provides a compositional construct for evidence and recency requirements.", "addresses_note": "N3"}, {"op": "add", "kind": "vocabulary", "construct_name": "food-value", "glossary_gloss": "Canonical symbols for ingredients, foods, and meals.", "closed": false, "members": [{"symbol": "food_simple_ingredients", "gloss": "Simple, basic ingredients.", "nl_synonyms": ["simple ingredients"]}, {"symbol": "food_chicken", "gloss": "Chicken meat.", "nl_synonyms": ["chicken"]}, {"symbol": "food_rice", "gloss": "Rice.", "nl_synonyms": ["rice"]}], "membership_rule": "An atomic symbol matching `food_[a-z_]+` naming an ingredient, food item, or meal.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Plan a dinner with simple ingredients.", "braincode": "GENERATE(target=art_meal_plan, ingredients=[food_simple_ingredients], topic=topic_dinner) -> plan : STRING"}, "change_type": "MINOR", "rationale": "Provides a typed category for ingredients and foods, required for structured meal planning.", "addresses_note": "N3"}, {"op": "add", "kind": "vocabulary", "construct_name": "dietary-value", "glossary_gloss": "Canonical symbols for dietary restrictions, nutrition targets, and satiety levels.", "closed": false, "members": [{"symbol": "dietary_healthy", "gloss": "Healthy nutrition profile.", "nl_synonyms": ["healthy"]}, {"symbol": "dietary_light", "gloss": "Light, low-calorie.", "nl_synonyms": ["light"]}, {"symbol": "dietary_satiating", "gloss": "High satiety, keeps one full.", "nl_synonyms": ["keeps me full", "avoids hunger"]}, {"symbol": "dietary_vegan", "gloss": "Vegan diet.", "nl_synonyms": ["vegan"]}], "membership_rule": "An atomic symbol matching `dietary_[a-z_]+` naming a dietary restriction, nutrition goal, or satiety level.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Plan a healthy light dinner that keeps me full.", "braincode": "GENERATE(target=art_meal_plan, dietary_restrictions=[dietary_healthy, dietary_light, dietary_satiating], topic=topic_dinner) -> plan : STRING"}, "change_type": "MINOR", "rationale": "Provides a typed category for dietary, nutrition, and satiety constraints, required for structured meal planning.", "addresses_note": "N3"}], "_provider_used": "gemini", "_model_used": "gemini-3.1-pro-preview"}
Critic assessment: {"decision": "needs-rework", "kpi_assessment": {"coverage": {"impact": "mixed", "reasoning": "The new Action signatures and food, dietary, participant, predicate, and evidence categories improve coverage for embodied placement, meal planning, code changes, and structured conversational claims. However, coverage regresses for previously legal quoted entity and named-recipient values: the proposed signatures require STRING<entity-name> and STRING<recipient-value>, while the current glossary explicitly retains quoted-string transitional exceptions for these values. The sampled meal request also cannot express crispy frying because Generate has no preparation-method attribute/category."}, "expressivity": {"impact": "benefit", "reasoning": "Compositional participants, predicates, evidence requirements, dietary restrictions, ingredients, events, and multi-destination placement preserve substantially more structure than opaque topic/content atoms. Nevertheless, proposition roles, polarity, quantification, and temporal/causal argument structure remain underspecified: participants plus pred_transmits_to cannot state which participant transmits to which, and a list of relations is positionally paired with destinations without an explicit stated pairing rule."}, "determinism": {"impact": "mixed", "reasoning": "Fixed attribute ordering and the topic/content decomposition guidance are useful progress, and the proposed signature-based typing is clearer than the current bare-STRING treatment. But overloaded signatures such as open_page(target: entity-name) and open_page(target: platform-name) leave category provenance unresolved when a symbol belongs to both categories; the signature position alone does not choose an overload. Handle naming is still illustrative rather than algorithmic, and the independent support translations disagree on whether the expedited-shipping branch must also require lateness."}, "interpretability": {"impact": "mixed", "reasoning": "Operation-specific typed signatures and named semantic categories make expressions easier to inspect. The very large Action signature paragraph, duplicated pick_up overloads, context-dependent symbol categories, and unconstrained pred_/part_/food_ minting make a reader unable to reliably determine a symbol's category or an operation's intended overload from the expression and glossary alone."}, "improvement": {"impact": "benefit", "reasoning": "The structured placement, support, travel, code, and dinner-plan representations give a weaker planner actionable decomposition rather than prose labels. The gain is limited where key task constraints have no field, notably food preparation method and proposition argument roles, and where code or conversational content is represented as one broad atomic code/topic symbol."}}, "simulations": [{"item_id": "held-support-1", "source": "seed_tasks", "nl": "If the customer's order is late and they're a first-time buyer, offer a 10% discount; if they're a repeat buyer, offer free expedited shipping instead.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT HandleOrderStatus\nTASK HandleOrderStatus {\n  ACTION check_support_status(metric=metric_order_late, target=order) -> is_late : BOOL\n  ACTION check_support_status(metric=metric_first_time_buyer, target=customer) -> is_new : BOOL\n  ACTION check_support_status(metric=metric_repeat_buyer, target=customer) -> is_repeat : BOOL\n  IF is_late AND is_new THEN {\n    ACTION offer_discount(discount_percent=10, target=order)\n  } ELSE {\n    IF is_repeat THEN {\n      ACTION offer_expedited_shipping(target=order)\n    }\n  }\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "Action signature typing supports the task. The source is semantically ambiguous about whether repeat-buyer expedited shipping also requires lateness; the proposed canonical rules do not resolve that encoding choice."}, {"item_id": "dev-code-1", "source": "seed_tasks", "nl": "Given a list of file paths, delete every file older than 30 days unless it's in the 'archive' folder.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT DeleteOldFiles\nTASK DeleteOldFiles(files: LIST[STRING<code-value>]) {\n  FOR EACH file_path IN files {\n    ACTION file_age_days(file=file_path) -> age_days : NUMBER\n    ACTION path_in_folder(file=file_path, source=path_archive) -> in_archive : BOOL\n    IF age_days > 30 AND NOT in_archive THEN {\n      ACTION delete_file(file=file_path, target=file_system)\n    }\n  }\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "The proposed code-value typing and file actions cover the actionable core, assuming code-value contains path_archive under the N6 complete-glossary rule."}, {"item_id": "8c5ccffa-b88e-4928-9394-6d46cfd36e59", "source": "Mind2Web (osunlp/Mind2Web, train, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "What are today's Limited Time Offers deals?", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT GetLimitedTimeOffers\nTASK GetLimitedTimeOffers : LIST[REF[STRING]] {\n  ACTION search_web(category=cat_limited_time_offers, target=deal) -> offers : LIST[REF[STRING]]\n  RETURN offers\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "search_web plus the existing category symbol captures the finite actionable core; the click trajectory is implementation detail rather than a required outcome."}, {"item_id": "b49f88ac-b407-41af-a9e7-f5e4cd1f970a", "source": "Mind2Web (osunlp/Mind2Web, train, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "Check the rating and user reviews for the game \"Deathloop\".", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT CheckDeathloopReviews\nTASK CheckDeathloopReviews {\n  ACTION search_web(target=deathloop) -> results : LIST[REF[STRING]]\n  ACTION extract(limit=1, target=results) -> deathloop_ref : REF[STRING]\n  ACTION read_page(target=deathloop)\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "The proposal has no typed review/rating extraction or inspection operation/attribute. The read_page action does not state that rating and user reviews must be returned, and it cannot take the selected REF result."}, {"item_id": "trial_T20190912_052310_164968#2", "source": "ALFRED (json_2.1.0, train)", "nl": "To move a full roll of toilet paper and an empty roll to the counter.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT MoveToiletPaper\nTASK MoveToiletPaper {\n  ACTION pick_up(state=state_full, target=toilet_paper) -> full_roll : REF[STRING]\n  ACTION place(destination=counter, target=full_roll)\n  ACTION pick_up(state=state_empty, target=toilet_paper) -> empty_roll : REF[STRING]\n  ACTION place(destination=counter, relation=left_of, target=empty_roll)\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "state-value and REF placement capture both rolls, but the source trajectory requires the empty roll to be left of the full roll. The proposed relation form can only relate empty_roll to counter unless destination=REF[STRING] is used; using full_roll would omit its counter destination. The multi-destination form does not support a REF destination."}, {"item_id": "trial_T20190919_003451_692296#1", "source": "ALFRED (json_2.1.0, train)", "nl": "Move a sponge and bowl from a counter to a cupboard", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT MoveSpongeAndBowl\nTASK MoveSpongeAndBowl {\n  ACTION pick_up(source=counter, target=sponge) -> sponge_ref : REF[STRING]\n  ACTION pick_up(source=counter, target=bowl) -> bowl_ref : REF[STRING]\n  ACTION place(destination=bowl_ref, relation=in, target=sponge_ref)\n  ACTION pick_up(target=bowl) -> bowl_with_sponge_ref : REF[STRING]\n  ACTION place(destination=cupboard, relation=in, target=bowl_with_sponge_ref)\n  ACTION close_container(target=cupboard)\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "REF destination support helps express putting the sponge in the acquired bowl. But close_container only accepts REF[STRING], whereas cupboard is a descriptor; acquiring the cupboard would alter the intended task, so the final required closure is not expressible."}, {"item_id": "sphinx-doc__sphinx-8035", "source": "SWE-bench_Verified (princeton-nlp/SWE-bench_Verified, test, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "Support defining specific `:private-members:` for autodoc.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT SupportPrivateMembers\nTASK SupportPrivateMembers {\n  ACTION modify_code(expected_output=outcome_documents_specified_private_members, file=path_sphinx_ext_autodoc_init_py, revision=chg_private_members_accept_arguments, target=sphinx)\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "The proposed code-value category can carry the file, requested revision, and expected behavior as atomic governed values under N6."}, {"item_id": "sphinx-doc__sphinx-7462", "source": "SWE-bench_Verified (princeton-nlp/SWE-bench_Verified, test, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "`IndexError: pop from empty list` for empty tuple type annotation.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT FixEmptyTupleAnnotation\nTASK FixEmptyTupleAnnotation {\n  ACTION modify_code(actual_output=outcome_index_error_pop_empty_list, expected_output=outcome_empty_tuple_annotation_valid, file=path_sphinx_domains_python_py, reproduction_step=repro_empty_tuple_annotation, revision=chg_handle_empty_tuple_annotation, target=sphinx)\n  ACTION modify_code(file=path_sphinx_pycode_ast_py, revision=chg_handle_empty_tuple_annotation, target=sphinx)\n  ACTION run_tests(assertion=assert_empty_tuple_annotation_builds, target=sphinx)\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "The code-value category and typed code fields cover the file set, observed outcome, reproduction, expected outcome, revision, and test."}, {"item_id": "c3312", "source": "PRISM (HannahRoseKirk/prism-alignment, conversations config, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "All people should switch to veganism on moral and ethical grounds", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT VeganismClaim\nCONVO VeganismClaim {\n  TURN t1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER inform(participants=[part_all_people, part_veganism, part_moral_grounds, part_ethical_grounds], predicates=[pred_should_switch_to, pred_justified_by], topic=topic_veganism)\n  }\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "participant-value and predicate-value improve over a single topic atom, but no construct binds predicate arguments, universal quantification, or the two grounds to their respective relations. Thus the expression does not recover the claim's full logical structure."}, {"item_id": "c4642", "source": "PRISM (HannahRoseKirk/prism-alignment, conversations config, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "What does christianity mean?", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT ChristianityMeaning\nCONVO ChristianityMeaning {\n  TURN t1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER ask(topic=topic_christianity_meaning)\n  }\n  TURN t2 SPEAKER=USER REPLY_TO t1 {\n    UTTER ask(topic=topic_god_existence)\n  }\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "The question speech acts and follow-up sequence are represented symbolically. No additional explicit constraints appear in the user turns."}, {"item_id": "c5110", "source": "PRISM (HannahRoseKirk/prism-alignment, conversations config, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "Boris Johnson good or bad", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT BorisJohnsonEvaluation\nCONVO BorisJohnsonEvaluation {\n  TURN t1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER ask(participants=[part_boris_johnson], predicates=[pred_good_or_bad], topic=topic_boris_johnson_evaluation)\n  }\n  TURN t2 SPEAKER=USER REPLY_TO t1 {\n    UTTER ask(participants=[part_boris_johnson, part_covid_19], predicates=[pred_response_to], topic=topic_boris_johnson_covid)\n  }\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "The category rules allow atomic participant and predicate symbols, but pred_good_or_bad and pred_response_to lack glossary definitions in the proposal and their argument roles/polarity are not structurally represented."}, {"item_id": "c74", "source": "PRISM (HannahRoseKirk/prism-alignment, conversations config, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "Should Israel exist in the land of Levant?", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT IsraelLevantQuestion\nCONVO IsraelLevantQuestion {\n  TURN t1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER ask(participants=[part_israel, part_levant], predicates=[pred_should_exist_in], topic=topic_israel_levant)\n  }\n  TURN t2 SPEAKER=USER REPLY_TO t1 {\n    UTTER ask(participants=[part_israel, part_levant], predicates=[pred_should_have_been_formed], topic=topic_israel_formation_history)\n  }\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "The two user questions are sequenced, but the modality, historical framing, and participant roles are opaque newly minted predicate/topic atoms rather than compositional semantics."}, {"item_id": "wildchat1m_en3u-149175", "source": "PATHs (microsoft/prototypical-hai-collaborations, wildchat1m_en3u-task_utterance_wintent_anns)", "nl": "Write 10 funny tweets about how I found these two quahogs on the beach this afternoon.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT GenerateQuahogTweets\nTASK GenerateQuahogTweets : LIST[STRING] {\n  GENERATE(events=[event_find_two_quahogs_on_beach_this_afternoon], participants=[part_user, part_quahog], quantity=10, quantity_unit=unit_item, style=style_funny, target=art_tweet, topic=topic_quahogs) -> tweets : LIST[STRING]\n  RETURN tweets\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "The new event and participant composition captures the finding event, count, artifact type, humorous style, and tweet quantity without prose."}, {"item_id": "wildchat1m_en3u-30995", "source": "PATHs (microsoft/prototical-hai-collaborations, wildchat1m_en3u-task_utterance_wintent_anns)", "nl": "Can you give me novel and useful ideas for my mental-health presentation script and add them to my script?", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT ReviseMentalHealthPresentation\nTASK ReviseMentalHealthPresentation : STRING {\n  GENERATE(constraints=[constraint_novel, constraint_useful, constraint_integrate_with_supplied_script, constraint_ordinary_audience], topic=topic_mental_health_hong_kong, transformations=[transform_expand_presentation_script], target=art_presentation_script) -> revised_script : STRING\n  RETURN revised_script\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "Generate captures the revision intent, artifact, topic, and audience constraint. The supplied script and requested additions have no structured input/reference mechanism, so the requirement to add ideas into that specific script is not fully preserved."}, {"item_id": "wildchat1m_en3u-69869", "source": "PATHs (microsoft/prototypical-hai-collaborations, wildchat1m_en3u-task_utterance_wintent_anns)", "nl": "Create personalities, dynamics, roles, appearances, and relationships for the listed fictional characters and Trinidad Osborn.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT GenerateCharacterDynamics\nTASK GenerateCharacterDynamics : STRING {\n  GENERATE(character_properties=[char_historical_expert, char_exceptional_memory, char_persuasive_speaker, char_dark_side_controlled], constraints=[constraint_include_appearances, constraint_include_personalities, constraint_include_relationship_dynamics, constraint_include_roles], participants=[part_trinidad_osborn, part_hinata_hyuga, part_konan, part_temari, part_hanabi_hyuga, part_samui, part_karui, part_ino_yamanaka, part_shiki_ryougi, part_altair, part_anko_mitarashi, part_kushina_uzumaki, part_mabui, part_tsunade, part_mei_terumi, part_mikoto_uchiha], target=art_character_profile, topic=topic_trinidad_osborn_character_group) -> profiles : STRING\n  RETURN profiles\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "Participants and character properties help, but the request's per-character appearance, personality, power, role, and relationship mappings cannot be associated with individual participants. A flat participant/property list loses those bindings."}, {"item_id": "wildchat1m_en3u-65289", "source": "PATHs (microsoft/prototical-hai-collaborations, wildchat1m_en3u-task_utterance_wintent_anns)", "nl": "Write a detailed scene where Chandler Hallow learns while filming that his wife Dasha and child died, including his hysteria and horror.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT GenerateGriefScene\nTASK GenerateGriefScene : STRING {\n  GENERATE(events=[event_chandler_learns_family_death_during_filming], participants=[part_chandler_hallow, part_dasha, part_chandler_child, part_mrbeast_team], quantity=1, style=style_detailed, target=art_script_scene, topic=topic_grief_reaction) -> scene : STRING\n  RETURN scene\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "The artifact, event, participants, and detailed-style request are captured, but no category encodes the requested emotional intensity, team discovery sequence, or scene-level causality beyond an opaque event symbol."}, {"item_id": "1775938591749", "source": "ThoughtTrace (SCAI-JHU/ThoughtTrace, Parquet column-projected sample, JSON-decoded messages)", "nl": "Hi, I need help organising my bookshelves.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT BookshelfConversation\nCONVO BookshelfConversation {\n  TURN t1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER ask(topic=topic_bookshelf_organisation)\n  }\n  TURN t2 SPEAKER=USER REPLY_TO t1 {\n    UTTER inform(participants=[part_books], predicates=[pred_organize_by_genre], topic=topic_bookshelf_organisation)\n  }\n  TURN t3 SPEAKER=USER REPLY_TO t2 {\n    UTTER ask(topic=topic_language_bookshelf_section)\n  }\n  TURN t4 SPEAKER=USER REPLY_TO t3 {\n    UTTER ask(topic=topic_literary_fiction_definition)\n  }\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "Conversation order and follow-ups are preserved. The desired organization criterion and book-category questions are represented largely as opaque predicate/topic atoms, with no category taxonomy or classification relation."}, {"item_id": "1775498956373", "source": "ThoughtTrace (SCAI-JHU/ThoughtTrace, Parquet column-projected sample, JSON-decoded messages)", "nl": "Problem solving from work.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT DifficultCoworkerConversation\nCONVO DifficultCoworkerConversation {\n  TURN t1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER ask(topic=topic_work_decision_framework)\n  }\n  TURN t2 SPEAKER=USER REPLY_TO t1 {\n    UTTER ask(topic=topic_difficult_people_framework)\n  }\n  TURN t3 SPEAKER=USER REPLY_TO t2 {\n    UTTER ask(topic=topic_difficult_coworker_framework)\n  }\n  TURN t4 SPEAKER=USER REPLY_TO t3 {\n    UTTER ask(topic=topic_difficult_coworker_script)\n  }\n  TURN t5 SPEAKER=USER REPLY_TO t4 {\n    UTTER ask(constraints=[constraint_polite, constraint_professional], participants=[part_user, part_coworker], predicates=[pred_coworker_rude_when_corrected, pred_user_intends_assist], topic=topic_feedback_conversation_script)\n  }\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "The multi-turn escalation from framework to tailored script is sequenced. The crucial distinction between correction, perceived humiliation, rudeness, intent, and requested wording has no argument-role or event structure."}, {"item_id": "1775791789755", "source": "ThoughtTrace (SCAI-JHU/ThoughtTrace, Parquet column-projected sample, JSON-decoded messages)", "nl": "I want you to assist me with time management and prioritizing my workday.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT PlanWorkday\nTASK PlanWorkday : STRING {\n  GENERATE(constraints=[constraint_prioritize_report, constraint_prepare_tomorrow_slides, constraint_include_exercise_before_dinner, constraint_quick_healthy_dinner], duration=1, duration_unit=unit_day, ingredients=[food_chicken, food_vegetables, food_rice], target=art_workday_plan, topic=topic_workday_time_management) -> plan : STRING\n  RETURN plan\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "The task's main priorities and dinner constraint are captured, but relative sequencing, deadlines, available current time, exercise duration, and the relationship between schedule blocks and tasks are not modeled."}, {"item_id": "1775940921302", "source": "ThoughtTrace (SCAI-JHU/ThoughtTrace, Parquet column-projected sample, JSON-decoded messages)", "nl": "Plan a dinner for 4 people in 2 hours using pork chops, rice, and baked beans; pork chops should be fried crispy.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT PlanPorkChopDinner\nTASK PlanPorkChopDinner : STRING {\n  GENERATE(ingredients=[food_pork_chops, food_rice, food_baked_beans], max_prep_time=120, quantity=4, quantity_unit=unit_person, target=art_meal_plan, topic=topic_pork_chop_dinner) -> dinner_plan : STRING\n  RETURN dinner_plan\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "food-value, quantity, and max_prep_time improve meal-plan structure. No preparation-method, cooking-technique, texture, or serving-count category/signature field captures fried crispy pork chops; quantity_unit=unit_person also requires a missing unit member/category."}], "cross_check": {"used": true, "agreement": "partial", "notes": "Anthropic and Gemini agree on the core action sequence for limited-time offers and toilet-paper movement, differing only in noncanonical handles. They differ materially on held-support-1: Gemini requires lateness for expedited shipping while Anthropic does not. They also diverge between GAP fallback and newly minted symbols for the Sphinx feature request, and between opaque topic versus structured event/participant encodings for quahog tweets; the proposed selection rule does not yet force convergence."}, "required_changes": [{"target": "Action / Types & Lexicon", "change": "Replace overload-dependent provenance with a uniquely resolvable rule. Either prohibit category overlap in a single parameter position, namespace categories, require an explicit typed cast/category tag, or collapse same-operation overloads into one declared union/category rule. Apply this specifically to open_page, read_page, book_hotel, modify_code, run_tests, add_file, delete_file, check_support_status, pick_up, and place."}, {"target": "Action / operation-vocabulary", "change": "Restore no-regression transitional support for previously legal quoted entity-name and recipient-value inputs during the N2/N3 build phase, or add glossary-defined proper-name/entity literal forms accepted by every affected signature. Do not make named recipients or quoted entity selectors inexpressible before N1/N4/N5."}, {"target": "Action", "change": "Repair retained embodied coverage: make close_container accept a selected container REF or provide a typed select/open-container route; extend the multi-location relation form to permit REF destinations; and state that destinations[i] pairs with relations[i], including equal-length validation."}, {"target": "Generate / food-value", "change": "Add preparation-method, cooking-technique, texture/result, serving-count unit, and optionally appliance categories/attributes. A dinner plan must encode fried/crispy pork chops, not merely ingredients and an overall time bound."}, {"target": "predicate-value / participant-value / Utterance / Generate", "change": "Add a glossary-defined proposition/relation construct with typed argument roles (for example subject, object, ground, time, modality, polarity, quantifier) and use LIST[proposition] rather than independent participant and predicate lists. Define how causal, comparison, location, temporal, and evidence relations bind their arguments."}, {"target": "Task", "change": "Make canonical handle generation algorithmic, not illustrative, and expand canonical attribute selection to state when a request requires a proposition/event relation object rather than a newly minted topic/predicate atom. Add a canonical rule for conditional scope, including whether shared antecedents such as 'order late' apply to an 'instead' branch."}, {"target": "Action / attribute-name", "change": "Audit every formerly retained Action attribute and signature against the new exhaustive signatures before removal. Restore or explicitly migrate any current attributes such as procedure, food, meal, message_structure, policy, amenity, ticket, and seat that remain supported by retained constructs, so the N2 build-phase no-coverage-regression requirement is met."}], "logic_issues": ["The claim that signature position resolves overlapping glossary symbols is false for overloaded signatures with the same attribute name and different categories. `ACTION open_page(target=x)` has two valid candidate signatures if x belongs to entity-name and platform-name, and the expression contains no category discriminator.", "The proposed placement list form has no normative rule that relations[i] applies to destinations[i], no equal-length requirement, and cannot name REF destinations even though the scalar placement form can.", "The proposed Action table types close_container as close_container(target: REF[STRING]), while ALFRED-style tasks may require closing a fixed named cupboard that was never selected as a movable object.", "The N3 revision removes current transitional quoted-string acceptance from entity and recipient positions, contrary to N2's explicit build-phase requirement not to reduce coverage before the free-text closing phase.", "participant-value plus predicate-value is not a proposition representation: it cannot determine argument roles, scope, quantification, modality, negation, or which participants a predicate relates.", "The operation-vocabulary calls its member list both an initial/open index and 'strictly limited to enumerated members,' then permits custom_<domain>_<action>; this is internally contradictory about whether operations are closed and does not define deterministic NL-to-domain/action segmentation."], "notes_assessment": {"N2": {"status": "violated", "trend": "regressed", "reasoning": "The proposal expands descriptive vocabulary but removes current quoted-string transitional exceptions for entity and recipient values and drops legacy attributes from its exhaustive index/signatures. That reduces currently expressible build-phase requests, which N2 expressly forbids."}, "N3": {"status": "violated", "trend": "improved", "reasoning": "Most revised Action, Generate, and Utterance attributes now have named category or base-type signatures, and the new categories provide concrete governed values. N3 is still violated because overload ambiguity prevents reliably establishing category provenance for an attribute value, and retained transitional or un-audited constructs cannot be shown to have all been migrated."}, "N7": {"status": "violated", "trend": "improved", "reasoning": "Canonical attribute order and a useful topic/content/event/constraint selection direction improve convergence. Ambiguous overload resolution, non-algorithmic handle derivation, underspecified proposition encoding, and the divergent support-branch readings prevent one canonical expression per meaning."}, "N1": {"status": "violated", "trend": "unchanged", "reasoning": "Free identifiers, STRING literals, and open-category token minting remain. This is expected during the build phase, but context-only provenance does not make every emitted token independently lookupable."}, "N4": {"status": "violated", "trend": "unchanged", "reasoning": "The proposal appropriately removes unqualified STRING attributes from revised signatures, but STRING results and the Task rule allowing literal text payloads remain, and the language is not yet in its closing phase."}, "N5": {"status": "violated", "trend": "unchanged", "reasoning": "The custom operation rule relies on translator-selected domain/action English-derived fragments, and free identifiers/quoted strings remain. The proposed atomic category patterns help but do not yet make the language self-contained."}, "N6": {"status": "partial", "trend": "improved", "reasoning": "The signatures are increasingly defined over categories, allowing simulations to use complete-glossary category members. Several needed meanings still have no category at all, including preparation technique and proposition argument roles, so N6 cannot cover them merely by assuming missing members."}, "N8": {"status": "partial", "trend": "improved", "reasoning": "The added compositional values, typed code attributes, food/dietary fields, and multi-placement attempt increase richness. Flat participant/predicate lists and opaque newly minted atoms still collapse substantial relational meaning."}}, "rebuttal_rulings": [], "accept_changes": [], "chosen_position": null, "position_comparison": null, "reasoning": "This is real progress on N3: the proposal replaces many unqualified attribute STRING channels with category-qualified signatures and supplies useful new value categories. It cannot be accepted because overloaded signatures make the central category-provenance rule ambiguous, while removal of current transitional entity/recipient strings regresses hard note N2. The compositional additions improve planning quality, but relation arguments and meal-preparation constraints remain insufficiently structured."}
Doc hygiene: All changes carry a complete glossary entry (gloss + worked example), and every revise/remove targets an existing entry.
Decision: needs-rework


