Proposed changes available for this translation:
- [add] Canonical-Form: grammar `-- No independent grammar production. A normative meta-rule referenced by Task, Conversation, and Bind wherever those constructs leave a choice open to the translator.` — Three canonicalization rules. (1) Handle-Naming Rule (HNR): every Bind-site result IDENT produced by an Action `-> IDENT`, GENERATE `-> IDENT`, or task_call `-> IDENT` (LET is exempt, see below) is the lowercase snake_case form of its primary semantic symbol: for Action, the `target` attribute's entity-name/artifact-value symbol if present, else the operation-id; for GENERATE, the `target` artifact-value symbol; for task_call, the callee Task's IDENT lowercased. If that base name is already bound in the current lexical scope, the next occurrence appends `_2`, `_3`, ... in order of first use (first occurrence carries no suffix). UTTER results keep the existing `content`, `content_2`, ... convention unchanged. LET IDENTs are the one exception: they remain author-chosen labels for intermediate values with no single mechanically-derivable source symbol, a known residual gap flagged for a future sprint. (2) Construct-Selection Rule (CSR): a request is encoded as a CONVO document iff it exhibits at least one of: more than one distinct speaker turn, an explicit reply/follow-up/correction relationship between requests, or content that is purely a speech-act with no effectful/computed outcome. Otherwise — a single closed request with one outcome, even if it involves communication (e.g. 'email my boss') — it is encoded as a TASK. This is checked before translation begins and fixes the top-level declaration deterministically. (3) Step-Ordering Rule (SOR): within one Task body or one Turn body, steps linked by an explicit Bind data dependency are ordered producer-before-consumer (already required by Bind); among steps with no such dependency, order follows the left-to-right order their sub-goals are first mentioned in the source request; if two sub-goals have no discernible relative mention order, they are ordered alphabetically by operation-id.
- [revise] Bind: grammar `bind ::= "LET" IDENT ":" type "=" value
-- Additional Bind-site forms: action_result ::= "->" IDENT ":" type ; task_call_result ::= "->" IDENT ; utterance_result ::= "->" IDENT ; generation_result ::= "->" IDENT ":" type` — Bind is BrainCode's shared named data-flow rule. Its five Bind sites are: (1) `LET IDENT : type = value`; (2) an Action result `-> IDENT : type`; (3) a task_call result `-> IDENT`, whose type is the callee Task's declared result type; (4) an UTTER result `-> IDENT`, whose type is STRING and whose value is that UTTER's content; and (5) a GENERATE result `-> IDENT : type`. LET requires an exactly matching static type and may bind a REF only by copying an already visible REF of exactly the same type; it cannot create a reference from a descriptor STRING. Every `->` IDENT at sites (2), (3), and (5) must follow Canonical-Form's Handle-Naming Rule: it is the snake_case form of the operation's primary semantic symbol (Action's target, GENERATE's target, or the callee Task's name), with `_2`, `_3`, ... appended on repeat use within the same scope; a translator may not invent an alternate name for the same derivation. UTTER results keep the `content`/`content_2`/... convention. LET names remain translator-chosen (HNR does not apply to them). A binding in a Task body is visible only to lexically later steps in that Task body and its subsequently entered IF branch or FOR iteration child scopes. A binding in a Turn body is visible only to lexically later steps in that same Turn body and its subsequently entered IF branch or FOR iteration child scopes; an UTTER binding never crosses into another TURN. A binding in an IF THEN body is visible only to later steps of that same THEN body; one in an ELSE body is visible only to later steps of that same ELSE body. A binding in a FOR EACH body is visible only during that one iteration's body. Parameters and task-call results are scoped to their own task-call invocation. Shadowing any currently visible name is a static error. Source-order execution guarantees operation order and propagates bound descriptor values or REF identities.
- [revise] Task: grammar `document ::= entrypoint (task | conversation)*
task ::= "TASK" IDENT param_list? ("CONTEXT" execution_date_param)? (":" type)? ("DECREASES" IDENT)? "{" step+ return_stmt? "}"
execution_date_param ::= "execution_date" "=" ISO_DATE
param_list ::= "(" param (,"," param)* ")"
param ::= IDENT ":" type
return_stmt ::= "RETURN" value
step ::= action | flow_if | flow_for | bind | task_call | utterance | generation
task_call ::= IDENT arg_list? ( "->" IDENT )?
arg_list ::= "(" arg (,"," arg)* ")"
arg ::= IDENT "=" value
-- ISO_DATE ::= a STRING literal matching exactly YYYY-MM-DD.` — A BrainCode document declares exactly one ENTRYPOINT naming a top-level TASK or CONVO. Per Canonical-Form's Construct-Selection Rule, a request is encoded under TASK rather than CONVO whenever it is a single closed request with one outcome, even if that outcome is communicative (e.g. 'email my boss' is a TASK with one ACTION send_email, not a CONVO) — CONVO is reserved for requests with more than one speaker turn, an explicit reply/correction relationship, or content that is a pure speech-act with no computed outcome at all. If ENTRYPOINT names a TASK, that TASK must declare zero parameters and is invoked once; other top-level TASKs are inert library declarations. `CONTEXT execution_date=ISO_DATE` is a translation-time-only annotation for resolving relative-date phrases once, retained verbatim for auditability, never re-read at runtime; a malformed value is a static error. Task names and Variable names are disjoint namespaces; scoping is lexical and block-structured. `param_list` declares typed formal parameters; a task_call's `arg_list` supplies arguments by name with exact static type matching. A declared result type (`: type`) requires the body to end with a matching `RETURN`; if undeclared, no RETURN is allowed. Recursion requires `DECREASES paramName` plus a guarding IF-THEN per call. Within a single TASK body, steps are ordered by Canonical-Form's Step-Ordering Rule: data-dependent steps follow their producer; independent steps follow the left-to-right mention order of their sub-goals in the source request; steps with no discernible mention order are ordered alphabetically by operation-id.
- [revise] Conversation: grammar `conversation ::= "CONVO" IDENT "{" turn+ "}"
turn ::= "TURN" IDENT "SPEAKER" "=" speaker_lit ( "REPLY_TO" IDENT )? ( "REVISES" IDENT )? "{" step+ "}"
speaker_lit ::= "USER" | "AGENT"
-- document ::= entrypoint (task | conversation)* ; CONVO is a top-level declaration form, alongside TASK.` — CONVO is a document-level declaration used only when Canonical-Form's Construct-Selection Rule routes a request here: more than one distinct speaker turn, an explicit reply/follow-up/correction relationship between requests, or a request whose entire content is a pure speech-act with no effectful or computed outcome. A single closed, one-outcome request is a TASK instead, even if it is itself a message (see Task). Each CONVO has its own Turn-name namespace; a Turn IDENT is meaningful only as a REPLY_TO/REVISES target inside the same CONVO, referencing only a Turn already declared above it. A Turn's body may freely mix ACTION and UTTER, running in its own fresh, independent scope like a Task body; the only value crossing a Turn/Task boundary is a Task's RETURN via `->`. Within one Turn body, steps follow Canonical-Form's Step-Ordering Rule, identically to Task. SPEAKER labels human (`USER`) vs assistant (`AGENT`) origin without affecting execution. `REPLY_TO IDENT` marks structural adjacency with no data-flow effect, resolved exactly as written even if its target has since been revised. `REVISES IDENT` defines a strictly linear correction chain: (1) a turn may be the REVISES-target of at most one other turn; (2) revision is whole-body replacement; (3) `effective(T)` recursively resolves to the chain's unique tip, and any reader determining the current request in a lineage must use `effective(root)`.

