Searcher notes: {'search_mode': 'folder', 'relevant_precedents': ['Python', 'Why Agentic Systems Need Ontologies'], 'phenomena_present': ['determinism', 'synonym-resolution'], 'kpi_fit': {'Python': "Scores highly on determinism through its 'one obvious way to do it' philosophy and strict keyword argument rules, though it relies on convention rather than syntax for alphabetical ordering.", 'Why Agentic Systems Need Ontologies': 'Drives determinism and interpretability by mapping natural language synonyms to a single, canonical structured node, eliminating lexical ambiguity.'}, 'comparable_expression': {'precedent': 'Python + Structured Ontology API', 'nl': 'Urgently email my boss (which must encode identically to: Send an urgent message to the manager)', 'rendering': 'send_message(recipient=Role.MANAGER, tone=Tone.URGENT)'}, 'notes': "N7 enforces strict canonicalization of both syntax and vocabulary, touching BrainCode's Action, Generate, and Utterance constructs (which enforce canonical attribute ordering) as well as Types & Lexicon (glossary symbols). Python's design philosophy and formal Ontologies provide the precedent for mapping diverse NL phrasing into a single, canonical, structurally rigid representation.", 'new_source_suggestion': None, '_search_mode': 'folder'}
Shaper proposal: {"summary": "Adds a global Canonical-Form construct fixing (1) how bound handles are named, (2) the TASK-vs-CONVO construct-selection rule, and (3) the step-ordering rule for decomposing a goal into steps, then wires Bind, Task, and Conversation to reference it so encoding converges deterministically.", "note_position": "comply", "note_argument": "N7's build-phase items — which construct a request maps to, how a household goal decomposes into steps, and how handles are named — were previously left to translator judgment. Canonical-Form gives each a fixed, mechanical rule, and Bind/Task/Conversation now cite it as normative, closing the largest remaining sources of encoding non-determinism without touching decoding.", "foundations_overview": null, "rebuttals": [], "changes": [{"op": "add", "construct_name": "Canonical-Form", "grammar": "-- No independent grammar production. A normative meta-rule referenced by Task, Conversation, and Bind wherever those constructs leave a choice open to the translator.", "semantics": "Three canonicalization rules. (1) Handle-Naming Rule (HNR): every Bind-site result IDENT produced by an Action `-> IDENT`, GENERATE `-> IDENT`, or task_call `-> IDENT` (LET is exempt, see below) is the lowercase snake_case form of its primary semantic symbol: for Action, the `target` attribute's entity-name/artifact-value symbol if present, else the operation-id; for GENERATE, the `target` artifact-value symbol; for task_call, the callee Task's IDENT lowercased. If that base name is already bound in the current lexical scope, the next occurrence appends `_2`, `_3`, ... in order of first use (first occurrence carries no suffix). UTTER results keep the existing `content`, `content_2`, ... convention unchanged. LET IDENTs are the one exception: they remain author-chosen labels for intermediate values with no single mechanically-derivable source symbol, a known residual gap flagged for a future sprint. (2) Construct-Selection Rule (CSR): a request is encoded as a CONVO document iff it exhibits at least one of: more than one distinct speaker turn, an explicit reply/follow-up/correction relationship between requests, or content that is purely a speech-act with no effectful/computed outcome. Otherwise — a single closed request with one outcome, even if it involves communication (e.g. 'email my boss') — it is encoded as a TASK. This is checked before translation begins and fixes the top-level declaration deterministically. (3) Step-Ordering Rule (SOR): within one Task body or one Turn body, steps linked by an explicit Bind data dependency are ordered producer-before-consumer (already required by Bind); among steps with no such dependency, order follows the left-to-right order their sub-goals are first mentioned in the source request; if two sub-goals have no discernible relative mention order, they are ordered alphabetically by operation-id.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Rinse two mugs one after another, placing each in the coffee maker.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT RinseTwoMugs\n\nTASK RinseTwoMugs {\n  ACTION rinse(target=mug, destination=sink) -> mug : REF[STRING]\n  ACTION place(target=mug, destination=coffee_maker)\n  ACTION rinse(target=mug, destination=sink) -> mug_2 : REF[STRING]\n  ACTION place(target=mug_2, destination=coffee_maker)\n}"}, "glossary_gloss": "Canonical-Form is the set of global tie-breaking rules that make two independent translators write the exact same BrainCode for the same request: it fixes how a bound result gets its variable name, whether a request becomes a TASK or a CONVO, and what order un-linked steps go in.", "change_type": "MINOR", "rationale": "N7 names handle-naming, construct choice, and step decomposition as build-phase canonicalization targets. Rather than duplicating similar rules inside three separate constructs, one shared meta-construct keeps the rule singular and referenceable, avoiding drift between Task's and Conversation's versions of the same CSR.", "cites_source": "Python", "addresses_note": "N7"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Bind", "grammar": "bind ::= \"LET\" IDENT \":\" type \"=\" value\n-- Additional Bind-site forms: action_result ::= \"->\" IDENT \":\" type ; task_call_result ::= \"->\" IDENT ; utterance_result ::= \"->\" IDENT ; generation_result ::= \"->\" IDENT \":\" type", "semantics": "Bind is BrainCode's shared named data-flow rule. Its five Bind sites are: (1) `LET IDENT : type = value`; (2) an Action result `-> IDENT : type`; (3) a task_call result `-> IDENT`, whose type is the callee Task's declared result type; (4) an UTTER result `-> IDENT`, whose type is STRING and whose value is that UTTER's content; and (5) a GENERATE result `-> IDENT : type`. LET requires an exactly matching static type and may bind a REF only by copying an already visible REF of exactly the same type; it cannot create a reference from a descriptor STRING. Every `->` IDENT at sites (2), (3), and (5) must follow Canonical-Form's Handle-Naming Rule: it is the snake_case form of the operation's primary semantic symbol (Action's target, GENERATE's target, or the callee Task's name), with `_2`, `_3`, ... appended on repeat use within the same scope; a translator may not invent an alternate name for the same derivation. UTTER results keep the `content`/`content_2`/... convention. LET names remain translator-chosen (HNR does not apply to them). A binding in a Task body is visible only to lexically later steps in that Task body and its subsequently entered IF branch or FOR iteration child scopes. A binding in a Turn body is visible only to lexically later steps in that same Turn body and its subsequently entered IF branch or FOR iteration child scopes; an UTTER binding never crosses into another TURN. A binding in an IF THEN body is visible only to later steps of that same THEN body; one in an ELSE body is visible only to later steps of that same ELSE body. A binding in a FOR EACH body is visible only during that one iteration's body. Parameters and task-call results are scoped to their own task-call invocation. Shadowing any currently visible name is a static error. Source-order execution guarantees operation order and propagates bound descriptor values or REF identities.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Rinse one mug in the sink, then place that same selected mug in the coffee maker.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT RinseThenPlace\n\nTASK RinseThenPlace {\n  ACTION rinse(target=mug, destination=sink) -> mug : REF[STRING]\n  ACTION place(target=mug, destination=coffee_maker)\n}"}, "glossary_gloss": "Bind gives a value a name for later steps in the same allowed scope. Every Action/GENERATE/task_call result name is now derived mechanically from what it holds (the Handle-Naming Rule), so two translators always pick the same name; only LET names stay author-chosen.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Bind's `-> IDENT` previously let the translator pick any legal identifier, which is the clearest remaining encoding non-determinism the note calls out ('how handles are named'). Deriving the name from the bound operation's own symbol removes the choice entirely while staying legible for decoding.", "cites_source": "Python", "addresses_note": "N7"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Task", "grammar": "document ::= entrypoint (task | conversation)*\ntask ::= \"TASK\" IDENT param_list? (\"CONTEXT\" execution_date_param)? (\":\" type)? (\"DECREASES\" IDENT)? \"{\" step+ return_stmt? \"}\"\nexecution_date_param ::= \"execution_date\" \"=\" ISO_DATE\nparam_list ::= \"(\" param (,\",\" param)* \")\"\nparam ::= IDENT \":\" type\nreturn_stmt ::= \"RETURN\" value\nstep ::= action | flow_if | flow_for | bind | task_call | utterance | generation\ntask_call ::= IDENT arg_list? ( \"->\" IDENT )?\narg_list ::= \"(\" arg (,\",\" arg)* \")\"\narg ::= IDENT \"=\" value\n-- ISO_DATE ::= a STRING literal matching exactly YYYY-MM-DD.", "semantics": "A BrainCode document declares exactly one ENTRYPOINT naming a top-level TASK or CONVO. Per Canonical-Form's Construct-Selection Rule, a request is encoded under TASK rather than CONVO whenever it is a single closed request with one outcome, even if that outcome is communicative (e.g. 'email my boss' is a TASK with one ACTION send_email, not a CONVO) — CONVO is reserved for requests with more than one speaker turn, an explicit reply/correction relationship, or content that is a pure speech-act with no computed outcome at all. If ENTRYPOINT names a TASK, that TASK must declare zero parameters and is invoked once; other top-level TASKs are inert library declarations. `CONTEXT execution_date=ISO_DATE` is a translation-time-only annotation for resolving relative-date phrases once, retained verbatim for auditability, never re-read at runtime; a malformed value is a static error. Task names and Variable names are disjoint namespaces; scoping is lexical and block-structured. `param_list` declares typed formal parameters; a task_call's `arg_list` supplies arguments by name with exact static type matching. A declared result type (`: type`) requires the body to end with a matching `RETURN`; if undeclared, no RETURN is allowed. Recursion requires `DECREASES paramName` plus a guarding IF-THEN per call. Within a single TASK body, steps are ordered by Canonical-Form's Step-Ordering Rule: data-dependent steps follow their producer; independent steps follow the left-to-right mention order of their sub-goals in the source request; steps with no discernible mention order are ordered alphabetically by operation-id.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Go to the flight booking site, search flights from Tel Aviv to Berlin next Tuesday, and list the three cheapest options.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT BookFlight\n\nTASK BookFlight CONTEXT execution_date=\"2025-06-03\" : LIST[REF[STRING]] {\n  ACTION open_page(target=\"flight_booking_site\")\n  ACTION search_travel(origin=\"Tel Aviv\", destination=\"Berlin\", date=\"2025-06-10\") -> raw_flights : LIST[REF[STRING]]\n  ACTION sort(target=raw_flights, ordering=\"price_asc\") -> sorted_flights : LIST[REF[STRING]]\n  ACTION extract(target=sorted_flights, limit=3) -> cheapest_flights : LIST[REF[STRING]]\n  RETURN cheapest_flights\n}"}, "glossary_gloss": "Task is a document-level container of steps. It is now also the canonical home for any single-outcome request — including a one-shot message like 'email my boss' — because Canonical-Form only routes a request to CONVO when it genuinely has multiple turns or a pure, outcome-less speech act.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "N7 explicitly names TASK-vs-CONVO selection as a build-phase target. Without a fixed rule, a one-shot communicative request ('email my boss') could plausibly be encoded as either a TASK or a one-turn CONVO by different translators; the CSR removes that fork.", "cites_source": "Why Agentic Systems Need Ontologies", "addresses_note": "N7"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Conversation", "grammar": "conversation ::= \"CONVO\" IDENT \"{\" turn+ \"}\"\nturn ::= \"TURN\" IDENT \"SPEAKER\" \"=\" speaker_lit ( \"REPLY_TO\" IDENT )? ( \"REVISES\" IDENT )? \"{\" step+ \"}\"\nspeaker_lit ::= \"USER\" | \"AGENT\"\n-- document ::= entrypoint (task | conversation)* ; CONVO is a top-level declaration form, alongside TASK.", "semantics": "CONVO is a document-level declaration used only when Canonical-Form's Construct-Selection Rule routes a request here: more than one distinct speaker turn, an explicit reply/follow-up/correction relationship between requests, or a request whose entire content is a pure speech-act with no effectful or computed outcome. A single closed, one-outcome request is a TASK instead, even if it is itself a message (see Task). Each CONVO has its own Turn-name namespace; a Turn IDENT is meaningful only as a REPLY_TO/REVISES target inside the same CONVO, referencing only a Turn already declared above it. A Turn's body may freely mix ACTION and UTTER, running in its own fresh, independent scope like a Task body; the only value crossing a Turn/Task boundary is a Task's RETURN via `->`. Within one Turn body, steps follow Canonical-Form's Step-Ordering Rule, identically to Task. SPEAKER labels human (`USER`) vs assistant (`AGENT`) origin without affecting execution. `REPLY_TO IDENT` marks structural adjacency with no data-flow effect, resolved exactly as written even if its target has since been revised. `REVISES IDENT` defines a strictly linear correction chain: (1) a turn may be the REVISES-target of at most one other turn; (2) revision is whole-body replacement; (3) `effective(T)` recursively resolves to the chain's unique tip, and any reader determining the current request in a lineage must use `effective(root)`.", "worked_example": {"nl": "User: 'Tell me what's happening in New Zealand politics.' User: 'Are you up to date?' User, correcting the first ask: 'Actually, give me 2023 Aotearoa political events specifically, using current information.'", "braincode": "CONVO CurrentAotearoaPolitics {\n  TURN t1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER ask(content=\"Explain current political developments in New Zealand.\")\n  }\n  TURN t2 SPEAKER=USER REPLY_TO t1 {\n    UTTER ask(content=\"State whether your information is up to date.\")\n  }\n  TURN t3 SPEAKER=USER REVISES t1 {\n    UTTER ask(content=\"Explain current political events in Aotearoa for 2023 using current information.\")\n  }\n}\n-- effective(t1) = effective(t3) = t3's body, since t3 REVISES t1 and no turn revises t3."}, "glossary_gloss": "Conversation (CONVO/TURN) is reserved for requests that genuinely have multiple turns, a reply/correction relationship, or no computed outcome at all — a single one-shot message is encoded as a TASK instead, per the same fixed rule that decides Task's boundary.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Conversation must state the exact complement of Task's new selection criterion so the two constructs jointly partition every request with no overlap, keeping 'one construct per concept' true for the TASK/CONVO boundary.", "cites_source": "Why Agentic Systems Need Ontologies", "addresses_note": "N7"}], "_provider_used": "anthropic", "_model_used": "claude-sonnet-5"}
Critic assessment: {"decision": "needs-rework", "kpi_assessment": {"coverage": {"impact": "mixed", "reasoning": "The TASK/CONVO rule preserves the existing broad task/conversation split and does not itself remove coverage. However, SOR can impose source-wording order on operationally independent steps without defining how implied causal dependencies become Binds, and the proposal leaves several sampled closed-task gaps unchanged."}, "expressivity": {"impact": "mixed", "reasoning": "The CSR makes one-shot messages consistently TASKs and preserves multi-turn structures as CONVOs. But the mandated SOR can encode an incidental mention order rather than intended operational order, as in examining a book by lamp light, and the proposal's own examples violate its handle rule."}, "determinism": {"impact": "mixed", "reasoning": "HNR and CSR make genuine progress on N7 by removing two major translator choices. They do not yield one canonical expression: TASK, CONVO, TURN, and LET identifiers remain unconstrained; Action attribute ordering is still not globally specified; decomposition into steps is not specified; HNR is undefined when target is a REF or STRING; and the supplied independent translations still diverge on payload choice, handle names, task names, and decomposition."}, "interpretability": {"impact": "mixed", "reasoning": "A shared Canonical-Form rule is conceptually easier to locate than duplicated rules. But phrases such as \"primary semantic symbol,\" \"purely a speech-act,\" \"computed outcome,\" and \"no discernible relative mention order\" are not mechanically decidable from an expression or source request, while the normative rule conflicts with the proposal's own worked examples."}, "improvement": {"impact": "benefit", "reasoning": "A weaker executor benefits when dependent selected objects have predictable handles and when a multi-turn correction is consistently represented as CONVO. The benefit is limited because the rule does not yet reliably determine step decomposition or all identifiers."}}, "simulations": [{"item_id": "held-trip-1", "source": "seed_tasks", "nl": "Plan a 3-day itinerary in Kyoto that includes at least one temple per day and avoids anything more than a 20-minute walk from the last stop.", "braincode": "<no valid BrainCode expression: GENERATE's declared legal attributes omit stop_requirement, and its published signatures conflict with the existing worked examples using that attribute>", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "Canonical-Form is irrelevant because the current Generate signature/attribute rules remain internally inconsistent."}, {"item_id": "dev-email-2", "source": "seed_tasks", "nl": "If my manager hasn't replied by Friday, send a polite follow-up; otherwise do nothing.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT FollowUpManager\nTASK FollowUpManager CONTEXT execution_date=\"2025-06-03\" {\n  ACTION check_replied(target=role_manager, deadline=\"2025-06-06T23:59:59Z\") -> role_manager : BOOL\n  IF NOT role_manager THEN {\n    ACTION send_email(content=content_follow_up, recipient=role_manager, tone=tone_polite)\n  }\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "HNR forces the awkward but deterministic BOOL handle role_manager from the Action target. This demonstrates that HNR needs a namespace-safe rule when a target symbol is also used as a role/value."}, {"item_id": "8fb78266-8862-4608-9ff3-92f81e58b2ff", "source": "Mind2Web", "nl": "Find the cheapest non-stop or 1 stop flight from JFK, New York to Changi, Singapore for 1 traveler on April 11 which includes seat selection and free cancellation", "braincode": "<no valid BrainCode expression: no governed flight attributes/categories define stop count, traveler count, seat-selection inclusion, cancellation policy, or an unambiguous year for April 11>", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "Canonical-Form does not address the missing closed-task vocabulary/signature coverage. Independent providers likewise disagreed between GAP and an ad-hoc expression."}, {"item_id": "29f47ddb-3d25-4cfb-b1d6-d3987e998a13", "source": "Mind2Web", "nl": "Check the movie \"65\" that is digital at the AMC Grove City 14 showing at 7:30 pm on Tuesday, March 28, 2023 and buy four tickets for seats J10 through J7", "braincode": "<no valid BrainCode expression: the language lacks governed movie-showtime, digital-format, theatre, ticket-purchase, seat-range, and ticket-quantity action signatures>", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "No proposed change helps this missing closed-domain action model."}, {"item_id": "trial_T20190908_083350_272263#2", "source": "ALFRED", "nl": "Examine a book by the light of a small lamp.", "braincode": "<no valid BrainCode expression: no Action attribute represents using a lamp as an illumination instrument for examine, and SOR would otherwise order the mentioned examine subgoal before turning on the lamp>", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "SOR exposes a substantive issue: source mention order is not a substitute for causal decomposition or operational preconditions."}, {"item_id": "trial_T20190909_014042_055607#1", "source": "ALFRED", "nl": "rinse a rag off in the sink and put it inside of the tub", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT RinseRag\nTASK RinseRag {\n  ACTION rinse(destination=sink, target=rag) -> rag : REF[STRING]\n  ACTION place(destination=tub, relation=in, target=rag)\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "HNR produces rag from the selected target, and the Bind dependency fixes the required rinse-before-place order."}, {"item_id": "matplotlib__matplotlib-25775", "source": "SWE-bench_Verified", "nl": "[ENH]: Add get/set_antialiased to Text objects; adjust Annotations if needed; use the per-object information in drawing rather than global rcParams access.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT AddAntialiased\nTASK AddAntialiased {\n  ACTION modify_code(method=sym_text, revision=chg_add_get_set_antialiased, target=matplotlib)\n  ACTION modify_code(method=sym_annotation, revision=chg_adjust_antialiased_annotations, target=matplotlib)\n  ACTION modify_code(method=sym_graphics_context, revision=chg_replace_rcparams_antialiased_access, target=matplotlib)\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "Assuming N6's complete code-value category, the code structures can be expressed. SOR preserves the proposal's source-mentioned sequence, but does not explain why this is the sole valid decomposition."}, {"item_id": "django__django-16877", "source": "SWE-bench_Verified", "nl": "New template filter escapeseq that escapes each item of a sequence before join when autoescape is off.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT AddEscapeseq\nTASK AddEscapeseq {\n  ACTION modify_code(file=path_django_template_defaultfilters_py, revision=chg_add_escapeseq_filter, target=django)\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "No Bind or TASK/CONVO ambiguity is involved; Canonical-Form is largely irrelevant."}, {"item_id": "c2734", "source": "PRISM", "nl": "what do you think about the movie her?", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT MovieHerOpinion\nCONVO MovieHerOpinion {\n  TURN t1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER ask(content=\"what do you think about the movie her?\", recipient=role_agent)\n  }\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "CSR deterministically selects CONVO because this is a pure speech act, but content remains prohibited prose under N4."}, {"item_id": "c5739", "source": "PRISM", "nl": "can men get pregnant?", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT IdentityQuestions\nCONVO IdentityQuestions {\n  TURN t1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER ask(content=\"hi, who are you?\", recipient=role_agent)\n  }\n  TURN t2 SPEAKER=USER REPLY_TO t1 {\n    UTTER ask(content=\"what kind of topics can you talk about?\", recipient=role_agent)\n  }\n  TURN t3 SPEAKER=USER REPLY_TO t2 {\n    UTTER ask(content=\"what is a woman?\", recipient=role_agent)\n  }\n  TURN t4 SPEAKER=USER REPLY_TO t3 {\n    UTTER ask(content=\"can men get pregnant?\", recipient=role_agent)\n  }\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "CSR clearly selects CONVO for the multi-turn context, but all substantive turns remain prose payloads."}, {"item_id": "c6577", "source": "PRISM", "nl": "What do you think about MMORPG games?", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT VideoGameDiscussion\nCONVO VideoGameDiscussion {\n  TURN t1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER ask(content=\"What do you think of video games?\", recipient=role_agent)\n  }\n  TURN t2 SPEAKER=USER REPLY_TO t1 {\n    UTTER ask(content=\"what category of video games is best for an adult?\", recipient=role_agent)\n  }\n  TURN t3 SPEAKER=USER REPLY_TO t2 {\n    UTTER ask(content=\"What do you think about MMORPG games?\", recipient=role_agent)\n  }\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "The proposed CSR helps preserve the multi-turn relationship, but lacks canonical Turn naming and leaves content as prose."}, {"item_id": "c872", "source": "PRISM", "nl": "The refusal of neurosurgeons to perform conscious brain surgeries seems contradictory to me, since the only way to know the consequences of intervening in a certain brain area in the patient is by keeping him awake during the operation. The plasticity of the brain prevents us from being able to map a brain as a standard for all people.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT AwakeSurgeryDiscussion\nCONVO AwakeSurgeryDiscussion {\n  TURN t1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER ask(content=\"Why are there neurosurgeons who do not like conscious brain surgeries?\", recipient=role_agent)\n  }\n  TURN t2 SPEAKER=USER REPLY_TO t1 {\n    UTTER inform(content=\"The refusal seems contradictory because awake intervention reveals consequences and brain plasticity prevents a universal map.\", recipient=role_agent)\n  }\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "Conversation structure is retained, but the nuanced argument remains an N4-violating prose fragment."}, {"item_id": "wildchat1m_en3u-10809", "source": "PATHs", "nl": "我写了一篇英语作文，请将它修饰的更好，使用一些高级词汇和高级句式，注重句子之间的衔接和格式。以下是我的作文：Whereas, I prefer to recommend Bup T--the Beijing University of Posts and Telecommunications...", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT ImproveBUPTEssay\nCONVO ImproveBUPTEssay {\n  TURN t1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTE