      "semantics": "Five sub-rules, each fully mechanical over the source request's given structure (never over unconstrained semantic judgment). (1) CONSTRUCT-SELECTION RULE (CSR): if the source is presented as a transcript with more than one human-authored message, or any message explicitly marked as a reply/correction to an earlier one, or the transcript already contains an agent message to record, the request is a CONVO with exactly one TURN per transcript message in source order (never a synthesized AGENT turn beyond what the transcript shows); REPLY_TO links each message to the nearest earlier message it responds to, REVISES links a message containing an explicit correction marker (e.g. 'actually', 'I meant', 'correcting the above') to the message it corrects. Otherwise (a single human message) the request is a TASK if fulfilling it requires at least one ACTION or GENERATE step (any operation-vocabulary member used with a governed target/effect, or any GENERATE); it is a CONVO with exactly one TURN, SPEAKER=USER, only when the message requires no ACTION/GENERATE at all (its entire content is answerable via UTTER alone, e.g. an opinion or how-to question). A message needing both an effectful step and a communicative component stays a single TASK; the communicative component is expressed as an ACTION using an operation-vocabulary member (UTTER is reserved for CONVO Turn bodies only, per Utterance). (2) DOCUMENT/TURN NAMING RULE (DNR/TNR): the sole top-level TASK or CONVO IDENT, and the matching ENTRYPOINT name, is PascalCase of the first step's HNR base symbol (see rule 3) in the document's first Task body or first Turn body, after SOR has fixed step order; for a Turn's first step being UTTER, its base symbol is its speech-act operation-id (UTTER has no target). Every TURN IDENT is 'T' followed by its 1-based ordinal position among all turns in document order (T1, T2, T3, ...) — never an author-chosen label. (3) HANDLE-NAMING RULE (HNR): every Bind-site result IDENT at an Action `-> IDENT`, GENERATE `-> IDENT`, or task_call `-> IDENT` derives its base name as follows — Action: if `target`'s value is a bare vocabulary-category symbol (entity-name, artifact-value, or another governed category token, unquoted), base = that symbol verbatim; if `target`'s value is an already-bound IDENT (a REF from an earlier step), a quoted STRING, a LIST, or `target` is absent from the signature, base = the operation-id. GENERATE: base = its `target` artifact-value symbol verbatim (GENERATE's target is never absent). task_call: base = the callee Task's IDENT converted to snake_case. UTTER keeps the existing `content`, `content_2`, ... convention, unaffected by HNR. LET is the one Bind-site HNR does not derive structurally: `LET IDENT` is the snake_case form of the attribute-name under which this value is first consumed as a named argument in a later Action/Check/GENERATE call (e.g. a value later passed as `max_price=` is named `max_price`); if never consumed as a named argument (used only inside a Check), it is named `cond_1`, `cond_2`, ... sequentially by order of first appearance in that Task/Turn body. Suffixing: within one Task body or one Turn body (the whole body rooted at that TASK/TURN, spanning every nested IF/ELSE/FOR block inside it), the first occurrence of a given base name gets no suffix; each later occurrence of the identical base name anywhere later in source order in that same body gets the next integer suffix (`_2`, `_3`, ...), regardless of which nested branch it falls in. A derived handle IDENT is permitted to lexically coincide with a vocabulary-category symbol token used elsewhere in the same expression (e.g. `check_replied(target=role_manager) -> role_manager : BOOL`): this is not a Bind shadowing violation because Bind's shadowing rule forbids shadowing another *bound* name, and a vocabulary symbol is not a bound name; the static type at each use disambiguates it for a reader. (4) STEP-ORDERING RULE (SOR): within one Task body or one Turn body, a step is emitted only for a distinct operation-vocabulary member the request actually names (explicitly, or by exactly one nearest synonym mapping per that category's membership_rule) — SOR governs the order of steps the request already calls for; it never invents an additional preparatory or precondition step the source does not name (an unstated required precondition, e.g. turning on a light before examining something in the dark, is a vocabulary/signature gap to fix in a future sprint, not a decomposition SOR performs). Among the resulting steps: (a) a step consuming another step's bound result (its `target` or any attribute is that earlier step's `-> IDENT`) is always ordered after its producer; (b) among steps with no such Bind dependency, order follows the left-to-right order their corresponding operation-vocabulary member is first mentioned in the source request; (c) if two such steps have literally the same first-mention position (e.g. conjoined in one clause with no sequencing word), they are ordered alphabetically by operation-id. (5) ATTRIBUTE-ORDERING RULE (AOR), the single canonical ordering for every attribute list in the language (Action, UTTER, GENERATE alike): `target`/`audience` (whichever the construct declares as primary) first if present, then every other supplied attribute in strict alphabetical order by attribute-name; no other ordering is ever legal.",
