    CLOSING PHASE (after N2, N3 and N7, together with N1): this is where prose payloads are removed. The vocabulary built earlier must already be able to carry the content that prose carried before. No attribute, argument or payload may contain full or partial natural-language text. That includes free-form tone descriptions and message content, e.g. content="ask for a two-day extension, politely" is not allowed. Prose inside an expression hurts Expressivity, because meaning hides in text the language does not structure. It also hurts Determinism, because two translators will phrase the same content differently. Content must be expressed with glossary symbols and constructs instead, e.g. an intent symbol request_extension with duration=2 and unit=day, and tone=polite. If the language cannot express something yet, the language must be extended; prose is never the fallback. This overrides the earlier allowance for opaque prose payloads in open, conversational items: their content, not only their meta-structure (intent, recipient, register, constraints), must now be encoded symbolically. The only exception is the atomic literals defined under N5. (status: open)
- N5 [hard] The language is self-contained; natural language may contribute only atomic symbols that the glossary defines as such.
    CLOSING PHASE (after N2, N3 and N7): defining the literal forms can start earlier, under N2. Reading or writing BrainCode must not depend on English grammar or an English dictionary; everything the language uses is pre-defined in its own spec and glossary. A natural-language word may be used only when the glossary adopts it as an atomic symbol with its own definition, e.g. the action symbol send or the object symbol mug. Its meaning then comes from the glossary entry, not from English. It cannot be inflected, conjugated, or combined using English syntax. Genuinely open-ended values need a glossary-defined literal category with a precise, atomic form (a single token, never a phrase). Examples are proper names, numbers, dates, times, URLs and file paths. This keeps them atomic even though their exact values cannot all be listed in advance. (status: open)
- N6 [direction] Write the syntax assuming the full glossary already exists.
    The grammar is defined over glossary categories (e.g. <action>, <object>, <tone-value>), not over the particular words that happen to be listed today. When a simulated item needs a word the glossary does not list yet (e.g. a specific object), assume the complete glossary contains it in the right category and write it as that category's symbol. Do not reshape the syntax around a missing entry, and do not fall back to prose (N4). The Critic should not penalize a missing entry within an existing category. It should penalize a missing category: if no category could hold the needed symbol, the language has a real gap and must add that category. (status: open)
- N8 [direction] Keep the language as rich as possible within the determinism and closed-vocabulary constraints.
    The closed vocabulary (N1-N5) and determinism (N7) should not make the language poor. Prefer an expressive glossary over collapsing distinct meanings into one coarse symbol: fine-grained attribute values, composable modifiers, quantities, conditions, references and relations. Determinism wins where the two genuinely conflict. Before giving up richness, though, the Shaper should look for a way to keep both, e.g. a deterministic composition rule over glossary symbols instead of one broad symbol. The Critic should flag any simulated item whose meaning was lost to over-coarse symbols. This note also covers decoding (see N7). Richness may cost some decoding determinism, but a reader or model decoding an expression should still converge on the same core meaning (intent, participants, constraints, ordering), even if the surface wording differs. Where extra richness makes the decoded meaning itself diverge, the Critic should weigh that against the expressivity it adds. (status: open)

Proposal (summary + all changes):
{
 "summary": "Rewrites Canonical-Form into five explicit, mechanically-testable sub-rules (document/turn naming, handle naming with full target-form coverage, construct selection via a transcript/effect test, bounded step-ordering, and a single attribute-ordering rule), and propagates the fixes into Bind, Task, Conversation, Flow-If, Action, Generate and Types & Lexicon so every worked example actually obeys the rules it cites.",
 "note_position": "comply",
 "note_argument": "Every previously-vague predicate (\"purely a speech-act\", \"primary semantic symbol\", \"discernible mention order\") is replaced with a test over a request's transcript structure and operation-vocabulary membership, so two translators run the same procedure and land on the same TASK/CONVO choice, the same bind names, and the same top-level/turn identifiers. Decoding is untouched (still one parse per expression); decomposition is explicitly scoped so the rule does not silently invent unstated steps, which is a deliberate, documented limitation rather than a hidden gap.",
 "foundations_overview": null,
 "changes": [
  {
   "op": "add",
   "construct_name": "Canonical-Form",
   "grammar": "-- No independent grammar production. A normative meta-rule referenced by Task, Conversation, Flow-If, Action, Generate, and Bind wherever those constructs leave a translator choice.",
   "semantics": "Five sub-rules, each fully mechanical over the source request's given structure (never over unconstrained semantic judgment). (1) CONSTRUCT-SELECTION RULE (CSR): if the source is presented as a transcript with more than one human-authored message, or any message explicitly marked as a reply/correction to an earlier one, or the transcript already contains an agent message to record, the request is a CONVO with exactly one TURN per transcript message in source order (never a synthesized AGENT turn beyond what the transcript shows); REPLY_TO links each message to the nearest earlier message it responds to, REVISES links a message containing an explicit correction marker (e.g. 'actually', 'I meant', 'correcting the above') to the message it corrects. Otherwise (a single human message) the request is a TASK if fulfilling it requires at least one ACTION or GENERATE step (any operation-vocabulary member used with a governed target/effect, or any GENERATE); it is a CONVO with exactly one TURN, SPEAKER=USER, only when the message requires no ACTION/GENERATE at all (its entire content is answerable via UTTER alone, e.g. an opinion or how-to question). A message needing both an effectful step and a communicative component stays a single TASK; the communicative component is expressed as an ACTION using an operation-vocabulary member (UTTER is reserved for CONVO Turn bodies only, per Utterance). (2) DOCUMENT/TURN NAMING RULE (DNR/TNR): the sole top-level TASK or CONVO IDENT, and the matching ENTRYPOINT name, is PascalCase of the first step's HNR base symbol (see rule 3) in the document's first Task body or first Turn body, after SOR has fixed step order; for a Turn's first step being UTTER, its base symbol is its speech-act operation-id (UTTER has no target). Every TURN IDENT is 'T' followed by its 1-based ordinal position among all turns in document order (T1, T2, T3, ...) — never an author-chosen label. (3) HANDLE-NAMING RULE (HNR): every Bind-site result IDENT at an Action `-> IDENT`, GENERATE `-> IDENT`, or task_call `-> IDENT` derives its base name as follows — Action: if `target`'s value is a bare vocabulary-category symbol (entity-name, artifact-value, or another governed category token, unquoted), base = that symbol verbatim; if `target`'s value is an already-bound IDENT (a REF from an earlier step), a quoted STRING, a LIST, or `target` is absent from the signature, base = the operation-id. GENERATE: base = its `target` artifact-value symbol verbatim (GENERATE's target is never absent). task_call: base = the callee Task's IDENT converted to snake_case. UTTER keeps the existing `content`, `content_2`, ... convention, unaffected by HNR. LET is the one Bind-site HNR does not derive structurally: `LET IDENT` is the snake_case form of the attribute-name under which this value is first consumed as a named argument in a later Action/Check/GENERATE call (e.g. a value later passed as `max_price=` is named `max_price`); if never consumed as a named argument (used only inside a Check), it is named `cond_1`, `cond_2`, ... sequentially by order of first appearance in that Task/Turn body. Suffixing: within one Task body or one Turn body (the whole body rooted at that TASK/TURN, spanning every nested IF/ELSE/FOR block inside it), the first occurrence of a given base name gets no suffix; each later occurrence of the identical base name anywhere later in source order in that same body gets the next integer suffix (`_2`, `_3`, ...), regardless of which nested branch it falls in. A derived handle IDENT is permitted to lexically coincide with a vocabulary-category symbol token used elsewhere in the same expression (e.g. `check_replied(target=role_manager) -> role_manager : BOOL`): this is not a Bind shadowing violation because Bind's shadowing rule forbids shadowing another *bound* name, and a vocabulary symbol is not a bound name; the static type at each use disambiguates it for a reader. (4) STEP-ORDERING RULE (SOR): within one Task body or one Turn body, a step is emitted only for a distinct operation-vocabulary member the request actually names (explicitly, or by exactly one nearest synonym mapping per that category's membership_rule) — SOR governs the order of steps the request already calls for; it never invents an additional preparatory or precondition step the source does not name (an unstated required precondition, e.g. turning on a light before examining something in the dark, is a vocabulary/signature gap to fix in a future sprint, not a decomposition SOR performs). Among the resulting steps: (a) a step consuming another step's bound result (its `target` or any attribute is that earlier step's `-> IDENT`) is always ordered after its producer; (b) among steps with no such Bind dependency, order follows the left-to-right order their corresponding operation-vocabulary member is first mentioned in the source request; (c) if two such steps have literally the same first-mention position (e.g. conjoined in one clause with no sequencing word), they are ordered alphabetically by operation-id. (5) ATTRIBUTE-ORDERING RULE (AOR), the single canonical ordering for every attribute list in the language (Action, UTTER, GENERATE alike): `target`/`audience` (whichever the construct declares as primary) first if present, then every other supplied attribute in strict alphabetical order by attribute-name; no other ordering is ever legal.",
   "worked_example": {
    "nl": "Rinse two mugs one after another, placing each in the coffee maker.",
    "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT Mug\n\nTASK Mug {\n  ACTION rinse(target=mug, destination=sink) -> mug : REF[STRING]\n  ACTION place(target=mug, destination=coffee_maker)\n  ACTION rinse(target=mug, destination=sink) -> mug_2 : REF[STRING]\n  ACTION place(target=mug_2, destination=coffee_maker)\n}"
   },
   "glossary_gloss": "Canonical-Form is the fixed tie-breaking rulebook that makes two independent translators write the exact same BrainCode: it mechanically decides TASK vs CONVO from the source's own message structure, names the top-level task/turns/handles/LETs from what they hold rather than translator taste, orders steps that are already implied by the request, and fixes attribute order everywhere. It deliberately never invents a step the source didn't name.",
   "change_type": "MAJOR",
   "rationale": "Directly answers every Canonical-Form required change: CSR is now a transcript/effect test instead of semantic judgment; DNR/TNR fix TASK/CONVO/TURN names; HNR covers vocab-symbol, REF, STRING, absent-target and LIST cases plus LET and collision handling; SOR is explicitly bounded to not fabricate decomposition; AOR centralizes attribute ordering so Action/Generate/Utterance share one rule instead of three.",
   "cites_source": "Python",
   "addresses_note": "N7"
  },
  {
   "op": "revise",
   "construct_name": "Bind",
   "grammar": "bind ::= \"LET\" IDENT \":\" type \"=\" value\n-- Additional Bind-site forms: action_result ::= \"->\" IDENT \":\" type ; task_call_result ::= \"->\" IDENT ; utterance_result ::= \"->\" IDENT ; generation_result ::= \"->\" IDENT \":\" type",
   "semantics": "Bind is BrainCode's shared named data-flow rule. Its five Bind sites are: (1) `LET IDENT : type = value`; (2) an Action result `-> IDENT : type`; (3) a task_call result `-> IDENT`, typed by the callee's declared result type; (4) an UTTER result `-> IDENT`, typed STRING, valued as that UTTER's content; (5) a GENERATE result `-> IDENT : type`. LET requires an exactly matching static type and may bind a REF only by copying an already-visible REF of the same type. Every `->` IDENT at sites (2), (3), (5) and every `LET` IDENT at site (1) must follow Canonical-Form's Handle-Naming Rule (HNR) exactly — a translator may never choose an alternate legal name for the same derivation. UTTER results keep the `content`/`content_2`/... convention. Scoping is unchanged: a binding in a Task body is visible only to lexically later steps in that body and subsequently entered child scopes; identically for Turn bodies (UTTER bindings never cross TURN boundaries); THEN/ELSE/FOR bodies each scope their own bindings as before. Shadowing any currently-*bound* visible name is a static error (a derived handle coinciding with a vocabulary symbol is not shadowing, per HNR). Source-order execution guarantees operation order and propagates bound values/REF identities.",
   "worked_example": {
    "nl": "Rinse one mug in the sink, then place that same selected mug in the coffee maker.",
    "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT Mug\n\nTASK Mug {\n  ACTION rinse(target=mug, destination=sink) -> mug : REF[STRING]\n  ACTION place(target=mug, destination=coffee_maker)\n}"
   },
   "glossary_gloss": "Bind gives a value a name for later steps. Every Action/GENERATE/task_call/LET name is now derived mechanically by Canonical-Form's Handle-Naming Rule, covering vocabulary-symbol targets, REF targets, STRING targets, absent targets, and LET's argument-name fallback, so two translators always pick the same name.",
   "change_type": "MAJOR",
   "rationale": "Fixes the Critic's example violations directly: mug/mug_2 derive from the bare entity-name symbol, matching HNR rule 1; the rule now explicitly states what happens for search_travel (no target) and GENERATE (target is the artifact-value verbatim), removing the prior contradictions.",
   "cites_source": "Python",
   "addresses_note": "N7"
  },
  {
   "op": "revise",
   "construct_name": "Task",
   "grammar": "document ::= entrypoint (task | conversation)*\ntask ::= \"TASK\" IDENT param_list? (\"CONTEXT\" execution_date_param)? (\":\" type)? (\"DECREASES\" IDENT)? \"{\" step+ return_stmt? \"}\"\nexecution_date_param ::= \"execution_date\" \"=\" ISO_DATE\nparam_list ::= \"(\" param (,\",\" param)* \")\"\nparam ::= IDENT \":\" type\nreturn_stmt ::= \"RETURN\" value\nstep ::= action | flow_if | flow_for | bind | task_call | utterance | generation\ntask_call ::= IDENT arg_list? ( \"->\" IDENT )?\narg_list ::= \"(\" arg (,\",\" arg)* \")\"\narg ::= IDENT \"=\" value\n-- ISO_DATE ::= exactly YYYY-MM-DD.",
   "semantics": "A document declares exactly one ENTRYPOINT naming a top-level TASK or CONVO, chosen deterministically by Canonical-Form's Construct-Selection Rule (CSR): a single message requiring at least one ACTION/GENERATE step is a TASK, even when it is itself a message (e.g. 'email my boss'); a message answerable purely by UTTER, or any multi-message transcript, is a CONVO instead (see Conversation). The TASK's own IDENT (and the document's ENTRYPOINT name) is fixed by Canonical-Form's Document-Naming Rule (DNR): PascalCase of the first step's Handle-Naming base symbol, once Step-Ordering (SOR) has fixed step order. If ENTRYPOINT names a TASK it takes zero parameters and runs once; other top-level TASKs are inert library declarations. `CONTEXT execution_date=ISO_DATE` is translation-time-only, resolving relative dates once and retained for audit; never re-read at runtime; malformed values are static errors. Task/Variable namespaces are disjoint; scoping is lexical and block-structured. `param_list`/`arg_list` use exact static type matching. A declared result type requires a matching terminal RETURN; undeclared forbids RETURN. Recursion requires `DECREASES paramName` plus a per-call guarding IF-THEN. Steps are ordered by Canonical-Form's SOR: it sequences only the steps the request itself names (one per named operation-vocabulary member), Bind-dependent steps after their producer, independent steps by first-mention order, ties by alphabetical operation-id — it never fabricates an unstated step. Every Action/GENERATE/UTTER attribute list inside a Task body follows Canonical-Form's Attribute-Ordering Rule (AOR).",
   "worked_example": {
    "nl": "Go to the flight booking site, search flights from Tel Aviv to Berlin next Tuesday, and list the three cheapest options.",
    "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT OpenPage\n\nTASK OpenPage CONTEXT execution_date=\"2025-06-03\" : LIST[REF[STRING]] {\n  ACTION open_page(target=\"flight_booking_site\")\n  ACTION search_travel(origin=\"Tel Aviv\", destination=\"Berlin\", date=\"2025-06-10\") -> search_travel : LIST[REF[STRING]]\n  ACTION sort(target=search_travel, ordering=\"price_asc\") -> sort : LIST[REF[STRING]]\n  ACTION extract(target=sort, limit=3) -> extract : LIST[REF[STRING]]\n  RETURN extract\n}"
   },
   "glossary_gloss": "Task is a document-level container of steps, chosen over CONVO whenever the request needs at least one ACTION/GENERATE, even for a one-shot message. Its own name, its handles, and its step order are now all mechanically derived by Canonical-Form rather than translator choice — the resulting names favor determinism over mnemonic elegance.",
   "change_type": "MAJOR",
   "rationale": "CSR is now a testable transcript/effect rule rather than a vague predicate; the worked example is corrected to actually obey HNR/DNR/SOR as the Critic required, rather than silently violating the very rules it cites.",
   "cites_source": "Why Agentic Systems Need Ontologies",
   "addresses_note": "N7"
  },
  {
   "op": "revise",
   "construct_name": "Conversation",
   "grammar": "conversation ::= \"CONVO\" IDENT \"{\" turn+ \"}\"\nturn ::= \"TURN\" IDENT \"SPEAKER\" \"=\" speaker_lit ( \"REPLY_TO\" IDENT )? ( \"REVISES\" IDENT )? \"{\" step+ \"}\"\nspeaker_lit ::= \"USER\" | \"AGENT\"\n-- document ::= entrypoint (task | conversation)*",
   "semantics": "CONVO is used only when Canonical-Form's CSR routes a request here: a transcript with more than one human message, an explicit reply/correction marker, an already-present agent message, or a single message answerable purely by UTTER with no ACTION/GENERATE. The CONVO IDENT (and ENTRYPOINT name) is fixed by the Document-Naming Rule from the first Turn's first step. Every TURN IDENT is 'T'+ordinal position in document order (T1, T2, ...), per the Turn-Naming Rule — never an author-chosen label. Exactly one TURN is emitted per transcript message; no AGENT turn is ever synthesized beyond what the transcript itself shows. A Turn's body may freely mix ACTION and UTTER, in its own fresh scope like a Task body; only a Task's RETURN crosses a Turn/Task boundary. Steps within one Turn body are ordered by SOR, identically to Task. SPEAKER labels USER/AGENT origin without affecting execution. `REPLY_TO IDENT` marks structural adjacency (no data-flow), resolved to the nearest earlier message it responds to, exactly as written even if since revised. `REVISES IDENT` forms a strictly linear chain: a turn may be the REVISES-target of at most one other turn; revision is whole-body replacement; `effective(T)` recursively resolves to the chain's unique tip, and any reader must use `effective(root)`.",
   "worked_example": {
    "nl": "User: 'Tell me what's happening in New Zealand politics.' User: 'Are you up to date?' User, correcting the first ask: 'Actually, give me 2023 Aotearoa political events specifically, using current information.'",
    "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT Ask\n\nCONVO Ask {\n  TURN T1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER ask(content=\"Explain current political developments in New Zealand.\")\n  }\n  TURN T2 SPEAKER=USER REPLY_TO T1 {\n    UTTER ask(content=\"State whether your information is up to date.\")\n  }\n  TURN T3 SPEAKER=USER REVISES T1 {\n    UTTER ask(content=\"Explain current political events in Aotearoa for 2023 using current information.\")\n  }\n}\n-- effective(T1) = effective(T3) = T3's body."
   },
   "glossary_gloss": "Conversation (CONVO/TURN) is reserved for genuinely multi-message transcripts, explicit corrections, or a single message with no computable action at all. Its document name and every TURN's name (T1, T2, ...) are now fixed by Canonical-Form instead of chosen freely, and the construct always appears under its own mandatory ENTRYPOINT.",
   "change_type": "MAJOR",
   "rationale": "Fixes the missing-ENTRYPOINT error the Critic flagged and replaces arbitrary t1/t2/t3 with the mechanical T-ordinal rule, while keeping CONVO the exact complement of Task's CSR branch.",
   "cites_source": "Why Agentic Systems Need Ontologies",
   "addresses_note": "N7"
  },
  {
   "op": "revise",
   "construct_name": "Flow-If",
   "grammar": "flow_if ::= \"IF\" check \"THEN\" \"{\" step+ \"}\" ( \"ELSE\" \"{\" step+ \"}\" )?",
   "semantics": "Conditional branching, identical inside TASK and CONVO TURN bodies. The Check is evaluated when control reaches the IF; THEN runs if true, ELSE (if present) if false, otherwise control passes on. THEN and ELSE each open a fresh child scope, invisible to each other and to siblings. An IF nested inside a TURN body may contain UTTER exactly as ACTION; the same nesting in a TASK body remains a static error per Utterance's restriction. A preceding Action bound for use in the Check follows Canonical-Form's Handle-Naming Rule like any other Bind site — including the case where the derived handle coincides with the vocabulary symbol used as that Action's own `target` attribute, which is permitted (see HNR's namespace note). When a Check depends on a preceding `check_replied` with a future `deadline`, the branch inherits that deferred timing.",
   "worked_example": {
    "nl": "If my manager hasn't replied by Friday, send a polite follow-up; otherwise do nothing.",
    "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT RoleManager\n\nTASK RoleManager CONTEXT execution_date=\"2025-06-03\" {\n  ACTION check_replied(target=role_manager, deadline=\"2025-06-06T23:59:59Z\") -> role_manager : BOOL\n  IF NOT role_manager THEN {\n    ACTION send_email(recipient=role_manager, tone=tone_polite, content=\"follow-up\")\n  }\n}"
   },
   "glossary_gloss": "Flow-If branches on a Check, running THEN or the optional ELSE in its own fresh scope. Its worked example now shows the (intentionally awkward but fully deterministic) case where a bound BOOL handle and a recipient's vocabulary symbol share the same token, per Canonical-Form's Handle-Naming Rule.",
   "change_type": "MAJOR",
   "rationale": "The Critic's own simulated item (dev-email-2) showed the prior example ('replied') violates HNR once target is a vocabulary symbol; this update makes the example consistent with the revised HNR instead of silently keeping the old, non-canonical name.",
   "cites_source": null,
   "addresses_note": "N7"
  },
  {
   "op": "revise",
   "construct_name": "Action",
   "grammar": "action ::= \"ACTION\" operation-id \"(\" action-attrs? \")\" (\"->\" IDENT \":\" type)?\naction-attrs ::= action-attr (\",\" action-attr)*\naction-attr ::= attribute-name \"=\" value\noperation-id ::= operation-vocabulary member",
   "semantics": "An Action's attribute list must be valid by category and signature as before. Canonical attribute order is fixed globally by Canonical-Form's Attribute-Ordering Rule (AOR): `target` first if the signature includes it, then every other supplied attribute in strict alphabetical order by attribute-name — no other order is legal, for any operation-id. The result handle's IDENT (when `-> IDENT : type` is present) is fixed by Canonical-Form's Handle-Naming Rule (HNR), not chosen freely by the translator.",
   "worked_example": {
    "nl": "Find the lowest-priced gray round mirror in stock in Atlanta, under $20.",
    "braincode": "ACTION search_web(target=mirror, availability=avail_in_stock, color=color_gray, currency=curr_usd, location=atlanta, max_price=20, shape=shape_round) -> mirror : LIST[REF[STRING]]\nACTION sort(target=mirror, rank_direction=dir_asc, rank_field=rank_price) -> sort : LIST[REF[STRING]]"
   },
   "glossary_gloss": "Action executes an external operation with a fixed attribute set. Attribute order and result-handle naming are no longer left to the translator: both follow Canonical-Form's single ordering and naming rules.",
   "change_type": "MAJOR",
   "rationale": "Required change asked for a complete, normative Action ordering rule; centralizing it in Canonical-Form's AOR and referencing it here (rather than restating a partial rule locally) keeps one canonical statement instead of duplicated, possibly-drifting copies across Action/Generate/Utterance.",
   "cites_source": "Python",
   "addresses_note": "N7"
  },
  {
   "op": "revise",
   "construct_name": "Generate",
   "grammar": "generation ::= \"GENERATE\" \"(\" generate-attr (\",\" generate-attr)* \")\" \"->\" IDENT \":\" gen_type\ngenerate-attr ::= attribute-name \"=\" value\ngen_type ::= \"NUMBER\" | \"STRING\" | \"BOOL\" | \"LIST\" \"[\" gen_type \"]\"",
   "semantics": "Legal Generate attributes: target, audience, content, topic, constraints, transformations, events, character_properties, duration, duration_unit, format, locale, quantity, quantity_unit, style, tone, stop_requirement, stop_min_per_day, max_walk_duration, max_walk_duration_unit. `target` (always present) -> artifact-value; its symbol is also this step's Handle-Naming base (HNR), used verbatim as the bound result's IDENT. `audience`->recipient-value; `tone`->tone-value; `format`->format-value; `style`->style-value; `locale`->locale-value; `topic`->topic-value; `constraints`->LIST[constraint-value]; `transformations`->LIST[transformation-value]; `events`->LIST[event-value]; `character_properties`->LIST[character-property-value]; `stop_requirement`->semantic-category-value; `duration_unit`/`max_walk_duration_unit`/`quantity_unit`->duration-unit-value. `duration`, `quantity`, `stop_min_per_day`, `max_walk_duration` are NUMBER. `content` remains a transitional STRING channel pending N4, used only where no `topic`/content-value member yet exists. Canonical attribute order and the result handle follow Canonical-Form's AOR and HNR respectively.",
   "worked_example": {
    "nl": "Plan a 3-day Kyoto itinerary that includes at least one temple per day and avoids more than a 20-minute walk from the last stop.",
    "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT ArtItinerary\n\nTASK ArtItinerary : STRING {\n  GENERATE(target=art_itinerary, duration=3, duration_unit=unit_day, max_walk_duration=20, max_walk_duration_unit=unit_minute, stop_min_per_day=1, stop_requirement=class_temple, topic=topic_kyoto_trip) -> art_itinerary : STRING\n  RETURN art_itinerary\n}"
   },
   "glossary_gloss": "GENERATE builds a non-effectful artifact. Its full legal-attribute list now matches every attribute the glossary's vocabulary categories (topic, constraints, transformations, events, character_properties, stop_requirement) already define, instead of leaving them usable in examples but undeclared in the signature; its bound result is always named after the artifact-value symbol itself.",
   "change_type": "MAJOR",
   "rationale": "Required change asked to reconcile Generate's legal-attribute list with the attribute glossary before relying on deterministic itinerary encoding; this closes the gap the Critic identified (stop_requirement, topic, constraints, transformations, events, character_properties were all used in examples/vocabulary but absent from Generate's own signature).",
   "cites_source": null,
   "addresses_note": "N7"
  },
  {
   "op": "revise",
   "construct_name": "Types & Lexicon",
   "grammar": "type ::= \"NUMBER\" | \"STRING\" | \"BOOL\" | \"LIST\" \"[\" type \"]\" | \"REF\" \"[\" type \"]\"\nvalue ::= STRING | NUMBER | IDENT | \"TRUE\" | \"FALSE\" | list_lit | diff\nlist_lit ::= \"[\" value (\",\" value)* \"]\"\ndiff ::= IDENT \"-\" NUMBER\nIDENT ::= [a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z0-9_]*\nSTRING ::= \"\\\"\" ( [^\"\\\\\\n] | \"\\\\\\\"\" | \"\\\\\\\\\" | \"\\\\n\" | \"\\\\t\" | \"\\\\u\" HEXHEXHEXHEX )* \"\\\"\"\nNUMBER ::= \"-\"? [0-9]+ ( \".\" [0-9]+ )? ( (\"e\"|\"E\") (\"+\"|\"-\")? [0-9]+ )?\nCOMMENT ::= \"#\" through end of physical line.",
   "semantics": "Unchanged core rule: a bare IDENT naming a vocabulary-category member (e.g. role_manager, tone_urgent, art_itinerary) is a vocabulary symbol — lexically an IDENT, statically typed STRING wherever STRING is required, never a quoted STRING literal itself. Everything else (REF[T], the four base types, `diff`) is unchanged. Bind-site result names built from such symbols follow Canonical-Form's Handle-Naming Rule (e.g. a GENERATE's result takes its `target` artifact-value symbol verbatim, not an unrelated author-chosen name).",
   "worked_example": {
    "nl": "Plan a 3-day itinerary with two named stops.",
    "braincode": "LET stops : LIST[STRING] = [temple, garden]\nGENERATE(target=art_itinerary, duration=3, duration_unit=unit_day, stop_requirement=class_temple) -> art_itinerary : STRING"
   },
   "glossary_gloss": "A bare glossary symbol like role_manager or tone_urgent is written without quotes but type-checks as STRING. A GENERATE step's bound result is named after its target artifact symbol verbatim (art_itinerary), not a separately invented label like 'plan'.",
   "change_type": "PATCH",
   "rationale": "The construct's own worked example previously violated the revised Handle-Naming Rule (bound GENERATE result named 'plan' instead of 'art_itinerary'); this is a documentation-only correction to keep the example consistent with Bind/HNR.",
   "cites_source": null,
   "addresses_note": "N7"
  }
 ],
 "rebuttals": [],
 "_provider_used": "anthropic",
 "_model_used": "claude-sonnet-5"
}

Your previous critique's required changes: [{"target": "Canonical-Form", "change": "Replace subjective CSR predicates with a mechanically testable decision table. Define whether a one-turn USER request with UTTER is always CONVO, whether agent-response turns must be emitted, and define the treatment of a request that both asks for information and requires an effectful action."}, {"target": "Canonical-Form", "change": "Specify canonical identifiers for TASK, CONVO, TURN, and LET bindings, or replace free identifiers with glossary-defined/derived identifiers. N7 cannot claim one expression per meaning while these names remain translator-chosen."}, {"target": "Canonical-Form", "change": "Define deterministic decomposition before SOR: state when a request maps to one Action versus multiple Actions, how implicit prerequisite/causal relations are identified, and how an implied dependency is represented. Do not use source mention order as a substitute for causal ordering."}, {"target": "Canonical-Form", "change": "Revise HNR to define a primary symbol for every valid target form, including REF identifiers, STRING descriptors, absent targets, lists, and non-entity Action targets. Define namespace collision handling with vocabulary symbols and specify whether suffix counts include all enclosing scopes or only the current lexical scope."}, {"target": "Task", "change": "Add a normative canonical Action attribute-order rule and apply it to every Action signature. Existing Action semantics say canonical ordering is unchanged but do not provide a complete global ordering rule."}, {"target": "Bind", "change": "Correct all worked examples to obey HNR. In particular, results of search_travel with no target must derive from operation-id, and GENERATE(target=art_itinerary) must derive art_itinerary rather than itinerary; explain how this avoids collision with vocabulary symbols."}, {"target": "Conversation", "change": "Correct the worked example to include the mandatory ENTRYPOINT declaration, and define canonical TURN names rather than retaining arbitrary t1/t2/t3 labels."}, {"target": "basis", "change": "Reconcile Generate's legal-attribute list with the attribute glossary and existing examples, especially topic and stop_requirement, before relying on deterministic encoding of itinerary tasks."}]
