Previous changes: [{"op": "add", "construct_name": "Canonical-Form", "grammar": "-- No independent grammar production. A normative meta-rule referenced by Task, Conversation, Flow-If, Action, Generate, and Bind wherever those constructs leave a translator choice.", "semantics": "Five sub-rules, each fully mechanical over the source request's given structure (never over unconstrained semantic judgment). (1) CONSTRUCT-SELECTION RULE (CSR): if the source is presented as a transcript with more than one human-authored message, or any message explicitly marked as a reply/correction to an earlier one, or the transcript already contains an agent message to record, the request is a CONVO with exactly one TURN per transcript message in source order (never a synthesized AGENT turn beyond what the transcript shows); REPLY_TO links each message to the nearest earlier message it responds to, REVISES links a message containing an explicit correction marker (e.g. 'actually', 'I meant', 'correcting the above') to the message it corrects. Otherwise (a single human message) the request is a TASK if fulfilling it requires at least one ACTION or GENERATE step (any operation-vocabulary member used with a governed target/effect, or any GENERATE); it is a CONVO with exactly one TURN, SPEAKER=USER, only when the message requires no ACTION/GENERATE at all (its entire content is answerable via UTTER alone, e.g. an opinion or how-to question). A message needing both an effectful step and a communicative component stays a single TASK; the communicative component is expressed as an ACTION using an operation-vocabulary member (UTTER is reserved for CONVO Turn bodies only, per Utterance). (2) DOCUMENT/TURN NAMING RULE (DNR/TNR): the sole top-level TASK or CONVO IDENT, and the matching ENTRYPOINT name, is PascalCase of the first step's HNR base symbol (see rule 3) in the document's first Task body or first Turn body, after SOR has fixed step order; for a Turn's first step being UTTER, its base symbol is its speech-act operation-id (UTTER has no target). Every TURN IDENT is 'T' followed by its 1-based ordinal position among all turns in document order (T1, T2, T3, ...) — never an author-chosen label. (3) HANDLE-NAMING RULE (HNR): every Bind-site result IDENT at an Action `-> IDENT`, GENERATE `-> IDENT`, or task_call `-> IDENT` derives its base name as follows — Action: if `target`'s value is a bare vocabulary-category symbol (entity-name, artifact-value, or another governed category token, unquoted), base = that symbol verbatim; if `target`'s value is an already-bound IDENT (a REF from an earlier step), a quoted STRING, a LIST, or `target` is absent from the signature, base = the operation-id. GENERATE: base = its `target` artifact-value symbol verbatim (GENERATE's target is never absent). task_call: base = the callee Task's IDENT converted to snake_case. UTTER keeps the existing `content`, `content_2`, ... convention, unaffected by HNR. LET is the one Bind-site HNR does not derive structurally: `LET IDENT` is the snake_case form of the attribute-name under which this value is first consumed as a named argument in a later Action/Check/GENERATE call (e.g. a value later passed as `max_price=` is named `max_price`); if never consumed as a named argument (used only inside a Check), it is named `cond_1`, `cond_2`, ... sequentially by order of first appearance in that Task/Turn body. Suffixing: within one Task body or one Turn body (the whole body rooted at that TASK/TURN, spanning every nested IF/ELSE/FOR block inside it), the first occurrence of a given base name gets no suffix; each later occurrence of the identical base name anywhere later in source order in that same body gets the next integer suffix (`_2`, `_3`, ...), regardless of which nested branch it falls in. A derived handle IDENT is permitted to lexically coincide with a vocabulary-category symbol token used elsewhere in the same expression (e.g. `check_replied(target=role_manager) -> role_manager : BOOL`): this is not a Bind shadowing violation because Bind's shadowing rule forbids shadowing another *bound* name, and a vocabulary symbol is not a bound name; the static type at each use disambiguates it for a reader. (4) STEP-ORDERING RULE (SOR): within one Task body or one Turn body, a step is emitted only for a distinct operation-vocabulary member the request actually names (explicitly, or by exactly one nearest synonym mapping per that category's membership_rule) — SOR governs the order of steps the request already calls for; it never invents an additional preparatory or precondition step the source does not name (an unstated required precondition, e.g. turning on a light before examining something in the dark, is a vocabulary/signature gap to fix in a future sprint, not a decomposition SOR performs). Among the resulting steps: (a) a step consuming another step's bound result (its `target` or any attribute is that earlier step's `-> IDENT`) is always ordered after its producer; (b) among steps with no such Bind dependency, order follows the left-to-right order their corresponding operation-vocabulary member is first mentioned in the source request; (c) if two such steps have literally the same first-mention position (e.g. conjoined in one clause with no sequencing word), they are ordered alphabetically by operation-id. (5) ATTRIBUTE-ORDERING RULE (AOR), the single canonical ordering for every attribute list in the language (Action, UTTER, GENERATE alike): `target`/`audience` (whichever the construct declares as primary) first if present, then every other supplied attribute in strict alphabetical order by attribute-name; no other ordering is ever legal.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Rinse two mugs one after another, placing each in the coffee maker.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT Mug\n\nTASK Mug {\n  ACTION rinse(target=mug, destination=sink) -> mug : REF[STRING]\n  ACTION place(target=mug, destination=coffee_maker)\n  ACTION rinse(target=mug, destination=sink) -> mug_2 : REF[STRING]\n  ACTION place(target=mug_2, destination=coffee_maker)\n}"}, "glossary_gloss": "Canonical-Form is the fixed tie-breaking rulebook that makes two independent translators write the exact same BrainCode: it mechanically decides TASK vs CONVO from the source's own message structure, names the top-level task/turns/handles/LETs from what they hold rather than translator taste, orders steps that are already implied by the request, and fixes attribute order everywhere. It deliberately never invents a step the source didn't name.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Directly answers every Canonical-Form required change: CSR is now a transcript/effect test instead of semantic judgment; DNR/TNR fix TASK/CONVO/TURN names; HNR covers vocab-symbol, REF, STRING, absent-target and LIST cases plus LET and collision handling; SOR is explicitly bounded to not fabricate decomposition; AOR centralizes attribute ordering so Action/Generate/Utterance share one rule instead of three.", "cites_source": "Python", "addresses_note": "N7"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Bind", "grammar": "bind ::= \"LET\" IDENT \":\" type \"=\" value\n-- Additional Bind-site forms: action_result ::= \"->\" IDENT \":\" type ; task_call_result ::= \"->\" IDENT ; utterance_result ::= \"->\" IDENT ; generation_result ::= \"->\" IDENT \":\" type", "semantics": "Bind is BrainCode's shared named data-flow rule. Its five Bind sites are: (1) `LET IDENT : type = value`; (2) an Action result `-> IDENT : type`; (3) a task_call result `-> IDENT`, typed by the callee's declared result type; (4) an UTTER result `-> IDENT`, typed STRING, valued as that UTTER's content; (5) a GENERATE result `-> IDENT : type`. LET requires an exactly matching static type and may bind a REF only by copying an already-visible REF of the same type. Every `->` IDENT at sites (2), (3), (5) and every `LET` IDENT at site (1) must follow Canonical-Form's Handle-Naming Rule (HNR) exactly — a translator may never choose an alternate legal name for the same derivation. UTTER results keep the `content`/`content_2`/... convention. Scoping is unchanged: a binding in a Task body is visible only to lexically later steps in that body and subsequently entered child scopes; identically for Turn bodies (UTTER bindings never cross TURN boundaries); THEN/ELSE/FOR bodies each scope their own bindings as before. Shadowing any currently-*bound* visible name is a static error (a derived handle coinciding with a vocabulary symbol is not shadowing, per HNR). Source-order execution guarantees operation order and propagates bound values/REF identities.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Rinse one mug in the sink, then place that same selected mug in the coffee maker.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT Mug\n\nTASK Mug {\n  ACTION rinse(target=mug, destination=sink) -> mug : REF[STRING]\n  ACTION place(target=mug, destination=coffee_maker)\n}"}, "glossary_gloss": "Bind gives a value a name for later steps. Every Action/GENERATE/task_call/LET name is now derived mechanically by Canonical-Form's Handle-Naming Rule, covering vocabulary-symbol targets, REF targets, STRING targets, absent targets, and LET's argument-name fallback, so two translators always pick the same name.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Fixes the Critic's example violations directly: mug/mug_2 derive from the bare entity-name symbol, matching HNR rule 1; the rule now explicitly states what happens for search_travel (no target) and GENERATE (target is the artifact-value verbatim), removing the prior contradictions.", "cites_source": "Python", "addresses_note": "N7"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Task", "grammar": "document ::= entrypoint (task | conversation)*\ntask ::= \"TASK\" IDENT param_list? (\"CONTEXT\" execution_date_param)? (\":\" type)? (\"DECREASES\" IDENT)? \"{\" step+ return_stmt? \"}\"\nexecution_date_param ::= \"execution_date\" \"=\" ISO_DATE\nparam_list ::= \"(\" param (,\",\" param)* \")\"\nparam ::= IDENT \":\" type\nreturn_stmt ::= \"RETURN\" value\nstep ::= action | flow_if | flow_for | bind | task_call | utterance | generation\ntask_call ::= IDENT arg_list? ( \"->\" IDENT )?\narg_list ::= \"(\" arg (,\",\" arg)* \")\"\narg ::= IDENT \"=\" value\n-- ISO_DATE ::= exactly YYYY-MM-DD.", "semantics": "A document declares exactly one ENTRYPOINT naming a top-level TASK or CONVO, chosen deterministically by Canonical-Form's Construct-Selection Rule (CSR): a single message requiring at least one ACTION/GENERATE step is a TASK, even when it is itself a message (e.g. 'email my boss'); a message answerable purely by UTTER, or any multi-message transcript, is a CONVO instead (see Conversation). The TASK's own IDENT (and the document's ENTRYPOINT name) is fixed by Canonical-Form's Document-Naming Rule (DNR): PascalCase of the first step's Handle-Naming base symbol, once Step-Ordering (SOR) has fixed step order. If ENTRYPOINT names a TASK it takes zero parameters and runs once; other top-level TASKs are inert library declarations. `CONTEXT execution_date=ISO_DATE` is translation-time-only, resolving relative dates once and retained for audit; never re-read at runtime; malformed values are static errors. Task/Variable namespaces are disjoint; scoping is lexical and block-structured. `param_list`/`arg_list` use exact static type matching. A declared result type requires a matching terminal RETURN; undeclared forbids RETURN. Recursion requires `DECREASES paramName` plus a per-call guarding IF-THEN. Steps are ordered by Canonical-Form's SOR: it sequences only the steps the request itself names (one per named operation-vocabulary member), Bind-dependent steps after their producer, independent steps by first-mention order, ties by alphabetical operation-id — it never fabricates an unstated step. Every Action/GENERATE/UTTER attribute list inside a Task body follows Canonical-Form's Attribute-Ordering Rule (AOR).", "worked_example": {"nl": "Go to the flight booking site, search flights from Tel Aviv to Berlin next Tuesday, and list the three cheapest options.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT OpenPage\n\nTASK OpenPage CONTEXT execution_date=\"2025-06-03\" : LIST[REF[STRING]] {\n  ACTION open_page(target=\"flight_booking_site\")\n  ACTION search_travel(origin=\"Tel Aviv\", destination=\"Berlin\", date=\"2025-06-10\") -> search_travel : LIST[REF[STRING]]\n  ACTION sort(target=search_travel, ordering=\"price_asc\") -> sort : LIST[REF[STRING]]\n  ACTION extract(target=sort, limit=3) -> extract : LIST[REF[STRING]]\n  RETURN extract\n}"}, "glossary_gloss": "Task is a document-level container of steps, chosen over CONVO whenever the request needs at least one ACTION/GENERATE, even for a one-shot message. Its own name, its handles, and its step order are now all mechanically derived by Canonical-Form rather than translator choice — the resulting names favor determinism over mnemonic elegance.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "CSR is now a testable transcript/effect rule rather than a vague predicate; the worked example is corrected to actually obey HNR/DNR/SOR as the Critic required, rather than silently violating the very rules it cites.", "cites_source": "Why Agentic Systems Need Ontologies", "addresses_note": "N7"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Conversation", "grammar": "conversation ::= \"CONVO\" IDENT \"{\" turn+ \"}\"\nturn ::= \"TURN\" IDENT \"SPEAKER\" \"=\" speaker_lit ( \"REPLY_TO\" IDENT )? ( \"REVISES\" IDENT )? \"{\" step+ \"}\"\nspeaker_lit ::= \"USER\" | \"AGENT\"\n-- document ::= entrypoint (task | conversation)*", "semantics": "CONVO is used only when Canonical-Form's CSR routes a request here: a transcript with more than one human message, an explicit reply/correction marker, an already-present agent message, or a single message answerable purely by UTTER with no ACTION/GENERATE. The CONVO IDENT (and ENTRYPOINT name) is fixed by the Document-Naming Rule from the first Turn's first step. Every TURN IDENT is 'T'+ordinal position in document order (T1, T2, ...), per the Turn-Naming Rule — never an author-chosen label. Exactly one TURN is emitted per transcript message; no AGENT turn is ever synthesized beyond what the transcript itself shows. A Turn's body may freely mix ACTION and UTTER, in its own fresh scope like a Task body; only a Task's RETURN crosses a Turn/Task boundary. Steps within one Turn body are ordered by SOR, identically to Task. SPEAKER labels USER/AGENT origin without affecting execution. `REPLY_TO IDENT` marks structural adjacency (no data-flow), resolved to the nearest earlier message it responds to, exactly as written even if since revised. `REVISES IDENT` forms a strictly linear chain: a turn may be the REVISES-target of at most one other turn; revision is whole-body replacement; `effective(T)` recursively resolves to the chain's unique tip, and any reader must use `effective(root)`.", "worked_example": {"nl": "User: 'Tell me what's happening in New Zealand politics.' User: 'Are you up to date?' User, correcting the first ask: 'Actually, give me 2023 Aotearoa political events specifically, using current information.'", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT Ask\n\nCONVO Ask {\n  TURN T1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER ask(content=\"Explain current political developments in New Zealand.\")\n  }\n  TURN T2 SPEAKER=USER REPLY_TO T1 {\n    UTTER ask(content=\"State whether your information is up to date.\")\n  }\n  TURN T3 SPEAKER=USER REVISES T1 {\n    UTTER ask(content=\"Explain current political events in Aotearoa for 2023 using current information.\")\n  }\n}\n-- effective(T1) = effective(T3) = T3's body."}, "glossary_gloss": "Conversation (CONVO/TURN) is reserved for genuinely multi-message transcripts, explicit corrections, or a single message with no computable action at all. Its document name and every TURN's name (T1, T2, ...) are now fixed by Canonical-Form instead of chosen freely, and the construct always appears under its own mandatory ENTRYPOINT.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Fixes the missing-ENTRYPOINT error the Critic flagged and replaces arbitrary t1/t2/t3 with the mechanical T-ordinal rule, while keeping CONVO the exact complement of Task's CSR branch.", "cites_source": "Why Agentic Systems Need Ontologies", "addresses_note": "N7"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Flow-If", "grammar": "flow_if ::= \"IF\" check \"THEN\" \"{\" step+ \"}\" ( \"ELSE\" \"{\" step+ \"}\" )?", "semantics": "Conditional branching, identical inside TASK and CONVO TURN bodies. The Check is evaluated when control reaches the IF; THEN runs if true, ELSE (if present) if false, otherwise control passes on. THEN and ELSE each open a fresh child scope, invisible to each other and to siblings. An IF nested inside a TURN body may contain UTTER exactly as ACTION; the same nesting in a TASK body remains a static error per Utterance's restriction. A preceding Action bound for use in the Check follows Canonical-Form's Handle-Naming Rule like any other Bind site — including the case where the derived handle coincides with the vocabulary symbol used as that Action's own `target` attribute, which is permitted (see HNR's namespace note). When a Check depends on a preceding `check_replied` with a future `deadline`, the branch inherits that deferred timing.", "worked_example": {"nl": "If my manager hasn't replied by Friday, send a polite follow-up; otherwise do nothing.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT RoleManager\n\nTASK RoleManager CONTEXT execution_date=\"2025-06-03\" {\n  ACTION check_replied(target=role_manager, deadline=\"2025-06-06T23:59:59Z\") -> role_manager : BOOL\n  IF NOT role_manager THEN {\n    ACTION send_email(recipient=role_manager, tone=tone_polite, content=\"follow-up\")\n  }\n}"}, "glossary_gloss": "Flow-If branches on a Check, running THEN or the optional ELSE in its own fresh scope. Its worked example now shows the (intentionally awkward but fully deterministic) case where a bound BOOL handle and a recipient's vocabulary symbol share the same token, per Canonical-Form's Handle-Naming Rule.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "The Critic's own simulated item (dev-email-2) showed the prior example ('replied') violates HNR once target is a vocabulary symbol; this update makes the example consistent with the revised HNR instead of silently keeping the old, non-canonical name.", "cites_source": null, "addresses_note": "N7"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Action", "grammar": "action ::= \"ACTION\" operation-id \"(\" action-attrs? \")\" (\"->\" IDENT \":\" type)?\naction-attrs ::= action-attr (\",\" action-attr)*\naction-attr ::= attribute-name \"=\" value\noperation-id ::= operation-vocabulary member", "semantics": "An Action's attribute list must be valid by category and signature as before. Canonical attribute order is fixed globally by Canonical-Form's Attribute-Ordering Rule (AOR): `target` first if the signature includes it, then every other supplied attribute in strict alphabetical order by attribute-name — no other order is legal, for any operation-id. The result handle's IDENT (when `-> IDENT : type` is present) is fixed by Canonical-Form's Handle-Naming Rule (HNR), not chosen freely by the translator.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Find the lowest-priced gray round mirror in stock in Atlanta, under $20.", "braincode": "ACTION search_web(target=mirror, availability=avail_in_stock, color=color_gray, currency=curr_usd, location=atlanta, max_price=20, shape=shape_round) -> mirror : LIST[REF[STRING]]\nACTION sort(target=mirror, rank_direction=dir_asc, rank_field=rank_price) -> sort : LIST[REF[STRING]]"}, "glossary_gloss": "Action executes an external operation with a fixed attribute set. Attribute order and result-handle naming are no longer left to the translator: both follow Canonical-Form's single ordering and naming rules.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Required change asked for a complete, normative Action ordering rule; centralizing it in Canonical-Form's AOR and referencing it here (rather than restating a partial rule locally) keeps one canonical statement instead of duplicated, possibly-drifting copies across Action/Generate/Utterance.", "cites_source": "Python", "addresses_note": "N7"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Generate", "grammar": "generation ::= \"GENERATE\" \"(\" generate-attr (\",\" generate-attr)* \")\" \"->\" IDENT \":\" gen_type\ngenerate-attr ::= attribute-name \"=\" value\ngen_type ::= \"NUMBER\" | \"STRING\" | \"BOOL\" | \"LIST\" \"[\" gen_type \"]\"", "semantics": "Legal Generate attributes: target, audience, content, topic, constraints, transformations, events, character_properties, duration, duration_unit, format, locale, quantity, quantity_unit, style, tone, stop_requirement, stop_min_per_day, max_walk_duration, max_walk_duration_unit. `target` (always present) -> artifact-value; its symbol is also this step's Handle-Naming base (HNR), used verbatim as the bound result's IDENT. `audience`->recipient-value; `tone`->tone-value; `format`->format-value; `style`->style-value; `locale`->locale-value; `topic`->topic-value; `constraints`->LIST[constraint-value]; `transformations`->LIST[transformation-value]; `events`->LIST[event-value]; `character_properties`->LIST[character-property-value]; `stop_requirement`->semantic-category-value; `duration_unit`/`max_walk_duration_unit`/`quantity_unit`->duration-unit-value. `duration`, `quantity`, `stop_min_per_day`, `max_walk_duration` are NUMBER. `content` remains a transitional STRING channel pending N4, used only where no `topic`/content-value member yet exists. Canonical attribute order and the result handle follow Canonical-Form's AOR and HNR respectively.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Plan a 3-day Kyoto itinerary that includes at least one temple per day and avoids more than a 20-minute walk from the last stop.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT ArtItinerary\n\nTASK ArtItinerary : STRING {\n  GENERATE(target=art_itinerary, duration=3, duration_unit=unit_day, max_walk_duration=20, max_walk_duration_unit=unit_minute, stop_min_per_day=1, stop_requirement=class_temple, topic=topic_kyoto_trip) -> art_itinerary : STRING\n  RETURN art_itinerary\n}"}, "glossary_gloss": "GENERATE builds a non-effectful artifact. Its full legal-attribute list now matches every attribute the glossary's vocabulary categories (topic, constraints, transformations, events, character_properties, stop_requirement) already define, instead of leaving them usable in examples but undeclared in the signature; its bound result is always named after the artifact-value symbol itself.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Required change asked to reconcile Generate's legal-attribute list with the attribute glossary before relying on deterministic itinerary encoding; this closes the gap the Critic identified (stop_requirement, topic, constraints, transformations, events, character_properties were all used in examples/vocabulary but absent from Generate's own signature).", "cites_source": null, "addresses_note": "N7"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Types & Lexicon", "grammar": "type ::= \"NUMBER\" | \"STRING\" | \"BOOL\" | \"LIST\" \"[\" type \"]\" | \"REF\" \"[\" type \"]\"\nvalue ::= STRING | NUMBER | IDENT | \"TRUE\" | \"FALSE\" | list_lit | diff\nlist_lit ::= \"[\" value (\",\" value)* \"]\"\ndiff ::= IDENT \"-\" NUMBER\nIDENT ::= [a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z0-9_]*\nSTRING ::= \"\\\"\" ( [^\"\\\\\\n] | \"\\\\\\\"\" | \"\\\\\\\\\" | \"\\\\n\" | \"\\\\t\" | \"\\\\u\" HEXHEXHEXHEX )* \"\\\"\"\nNUMBER ::= \"-\"? [0-9]+ ( \".\" [0-9]+ )? ( (\"e\"|\"E\") (\"+\"|\"-\")? [0-9]+ )?\nCOMMENT ::= \"#\" through end of physical line.", "semantics": "Unchanged core rule: a bare IDENT naming a vocabulary-category member (e.g. role_manager, tone_urgent, art_itinerary) is a vocabulary symbol — lexically an IDENT, statically typed STRING wherever STRING is required, never a quoted STRING literal itself. Everything else (REF[T], the four base types, `diff`) is unchanged. Bind-site result names built from such symbols follow Canonical-Form's Handle-Naming Rule (e.g. a GENERATE's result takes its `target` artifact-value symbol verbatim, not an unrelated author-chosen name).", "worked_example": {"nl": "Plan a 3-day itinerary with two named stops.", "braincode": "LET stops : LIST[STRING] = [temple, garden]\nGENERATE(target=art_itinerary, duration=3, duration_unit=unit_day, stop_requirement=class_temple) -> art_itinerary : STRING"}, "glossary_gloss": "A bare glossary symbol like role_manager or tone_urgent is written without quotes but type-checks as STRING. A GENERATE step's bound result is named after its target artifact symbol verbatim (art_itinerary), not a separately invented label like 'plan'.", "change_type": "PATCH", "rationale": "The construct's own worked example previously violated the revised Handle-Naming Rule (bound GENERATE result named 'plan' instead of 'art_itinerary'); this is a documentation-only correction to keep the example consistent with Bind/HNR.", "cites_source": null, "addresses_note": "N7"}]
Critic's decision: needs-rework
Critic's reasoning: This is a meaningful N7-oriented improvement: it makes several formerly discretionary choices mechanically specified and repairs multiple prior example inconsistencies. It cannot be accepted because the central step-order rule directly contradicts its own example and leaves repeated actions, transcript scope, decomposition, and lexical collision resolution non-deterministic. The prior decomposition requirement was not made; it was explicitly deferred, so the bundle is not yet logically sound.
Required changes (address every one explicitly): [{"target": "Canonical-Form", "change": "Replace SOR's contradictory 'one per distinct operation-vocabulary member' rule with a normative decomposition and multiplicity algorithm. It must state when repeated quantified instances emit repeated Actions, a FOR EACH, or one plural operation; define how per-instance ordering and HNR suffixing work; and correct the rinse-two-mugs example to follow that rule."}, {"target": "Canonical-Form", "change": "Define the exact CSR input unit. State whether translation consumes only the supplied NL field, the entire recorded transcript, or an explicitly selected request span; for a transcript define whether every displayed assistant message is emitted as an AGENT TURN and how system/evaluation/feedback records are excluded."}, {"target": "Canonical-Form", "change": "Add a deterministic normalization/decomposition policy for action granularity and causal relations. It must decide, for example, whether a UI trajectory's clicks/filters are preserved or normalized to search_web, when a request's explicit final-state wording entails pick_up/slice/heat/place, and how necessary but unmentioned preconditions are recorded or rejected."}, {"target": "Bind", "change": "Remove or repair the vocabulary-symbol/handle collision exception. Define one unambiguous lexical resolution rule, preferably require a distinct derived handle such as role_manager_result when HNR's base collides with any vocabulary symbol in scope, and revise the Flow-If example accordingly. Expected static type alone cannot define which same-token binding a parser/name resolver selects."}, {"target": "Canonical-Form", "change": "Define canonical mapping from a source operation phrase to exactly one operation-id, including compound requests and the choice among overlapping existing operations such as select_filter, apply_filters, and search_web. The current membership rule only provides some synonyms and leaves operation segmentation to translator judgment."}, {"target": "Generate", "change": "Reconcile the new legal attributes with attribute-name vocabulary: add topic, constraints, transformations, events, and character_properties as governed attribute-name members with their stated categories, and add quantity_unit if Generate uses it. Do not claim the signature matches glossary categories while those attribute names are absent from the attribute glossary."}, {"target": "Canonical-Form", "change": "State the canonical representation of conversational follow-ups that alter a prior generation request: distinguish REPLY_TO, REVISES, and a fresh request, including whether replacing a length constraint or requesting continuation replaces the whole earlier body."}]
Logic issues: ["SOR requires one step per distinct named operation-vocabulary member, yet Canonical-Form's own worked example uses rinse twice and place twice. Thus the same request is both required to have two repeated steps and prohibited from having them.", "The claimed vocabulary/handle collision disambiguation is not defined by static type. In `check_replied(target=role_manager) -> role_manager : BOOL`, later `recipient=role_manager` can denote either a BOOL binding or a recipient vocabulary symbol; expected type is not a lexical or name-resolution rule.", "CSR refers to 'the source' being a transcript but does not define whether dataset trajectories, assistant responses, feedback, and the displayed NL prompt constitute that source. This materially changes TASK/CONVO selection and turn count.", "The proposed Generate signature names topic, constraints, transformations, events, and character_properties, but current attribute-name vocabulary does not define those attribute names or their governing categories.", "The DNR first-step rule depends on SOR, while SOR cannot consistently determine the first step for repeated, compound, or trajectory-normalized operations; consequently ENTRYPOINT and document identifiers remain non-deterministic in those cases.", "The previous required change concerning deterministic decomposition was not actually completed: the proposal explicitly says SOR never invents unnamed preparatory steps, but it supplies no rule for explicit final-state requests whose required operation sequence is not enumerated."]
Pre-KPI assessment: {"coverage": {"impact": "mixed", "reasoning": "Generate now declares several previously undeclared itinerary and generation attributes, which helps express itinerary constraints. However, SOR says only one step is emitted per distinct named operation-vocabulary member, while its own rinse-two-mugs example emits two rinse and two place steps; repeated operations, multiple favorite saves, and other multiplicity requests therefore have no consistent encoding rule."}, "expressivity": {"impact": "mixed", "reasoning": "The proposal preserves the current transitional STRING channels and improves explicit generation attributes such as topic and stop_requirement. But deterministic decomposition is explicitly declined: it cannot say when repeated instances of an operation become repeated Actions, a loop, or one plural Action, and it does not represent unstated but necessary causal prerequisites. Several multi-turn samples also remain only partially preserved because transcript scope is not defined."}, "determinism": {"impact": "benefit", "reasoning": "CSR, ordinal TURN names, AOR, and most HNR cases materially reduce translator discretion relative to the current specification. Nevertheless, exact convergence remains unlikely for repeated actions, transcript boundaries, source synonym segmentation, target-less operations, and lexical collisions between a bound handle and a vocabulary symbol; these defects prevent N7 from being upheld."}, "interpretability": {"impact": "mixed", "reasoning": "A single global attribute-order rule and T1/T2 naming are easy to read. In contrast, permitting a BOOL binding named role_manager beside the recipient vocabulary symbol role_manager leaves name resolution dependent on expected type without a normative resolution rule, and mechanically derived document names such as OpenPage or RoleManager do not reliably identify the task's intent."}, "improvement": {"impact": "mixed", "reasoning": "Canonical ordering and explicit data-flow names can help a weaker executor follow straightforward web, household, and generation tasks. The SOR conflict can instead make a weaker model omit required repeated operations or invent a loop, and the refusal to identify necessary causal decomposition leaves important household execution planning under-specified."}}
Simulated examples that exposed gaps: [{"item_id": "dev-web-1", "source": "seed_tasks", "nl": "Go to the flight booking site, search flights from Tel Aviv to Berlin next Tuesday, and list the three cheapest options.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT OpenPage\n\nTASK OpenPage CONTEXT execution_date=\"2025-06-03\" : LIST[REF[STRING]] {\n  ACTION open_page(target=\"flight_booking_site\")\n  ACTION search_travel(date=\"2025-06-10\", destination=\"Berlin\", origin=\"Tel Aviv\") -> search_travel : LIST[REF[STRING]]\n  ACTION sort(target=search_travel, ordering=\"price_asc\") -> sort : LIST[REF[STRING]]\n  ACTION extract(target=sort, limit=3) -> extract : LIST[REF[STRING]]\n  RETURN extract\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "Canonical-Form supplies the Task choice, source-order sequence, absent-target handle search_travel, and AOR. Independent translators can still differ over whether opening the site is an explicit operation required by the terse request or an implicit prerequisite."}, {"item_id": "dev-web-2", "source": "seed_tasks", "nl": "On the electronics store, filter laptops to 16 GB RAM or more under $1,200, and add the best-rated one to the cart.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT SelectFilter\n\nTASK SelectFilter {\n  ACTION select_filter(target=laptop, max_price=1200, min_ram_gb=16) -> laptop : LIST[REF[STRING]]\n  ACTION sort(target=laptop, rank_direction=dir_desc, rank_field=rank_rating) -> sort : LIST[REF[STRING]]\n  ACTION extract(target=sort, limit=1) -> extract : LIST[REF[STRING]]\n  ACTION add_to_cart(target=extract)\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "The action signatures for min_ram_gb and the electronics-store context are assumed to exist under N6. HNR and AOR help, but whether filtering maps to select_filter, apply_filters, or search_web remains under-specified by synonym mappings."}, {"item_id": "479bdc82-ec52-447b-a577-fa2bcdc3886f", "source": "Mind2Web (osunlp/Mind2Web, train, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "Show offers on home essentials under $20 and add the first 3 items to favorites.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT SearchWeb\n\nTASK SearchWeb {\n  ACTION search_web(target=home_essentials, category=cat_home_essentials, currency=curr_usd, max_price=20) -> home_essentials : LIST[REF[STRING]]\n  ACTION extract(target=home_essentials, limit=3) -> extract : LIST[REF[STRING]]\n  FOR EACH item IN extract {\n    ACTION save_favorite(target=item)\n  }\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "The actionable core is representable if the full glossary supplies home_essentials. SOR is inconsistent here: the request names save_favorite once but requires three effect instances; it does not say whether FOR EACH is mandated, permitted, or prohibited."}, {"item_id": "4af615be-4472-4357-8737-5286b06b385d", "source": "Mind2Web (osunlp/Mind2Web, train, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "Browse listings for a used 2006 Honda Civic.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT SearchWeb\n\nTASK SearchWeb {\n  ACTION search_web(target=honda_civic, condition=state_used, year=2006) -> honda_civic : LIST[REF[STRING]]\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "A complete glossary is assumed to provide honda_civic and state_used. The proposal does not determine whether the trajectory's four UI filter operations must be retained as four steps or normalized to one search_web Action."}, {"item_id": "trial_T20190911_135649_366423#0", "source": "ALFRED (json_2.1.0, train)", "nl": "Place a hot slice of tomato inside the trash can.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT PickUp\n\nTASK PickUp {\n  ACTION pick_up(target=knife) -> knife : REF[STRING]\n  ACTION slice(target=tomato, destination=trash_can) -> tomato : REF[STRING]\n  ACTION pick_up(target=tomato, state=state_sliced) -> tomato_2 : REF[STRING]\n  ACTION heat(target=tomato_2) -> heat : REF[STRING]\n  ACTION place(target=heat, destination=trash_can, relation=in)\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "The requested final state is represented, but the supplied trajectory requires repeated opening, closing, movement, and object handling steps that are not stated in the request and whose inclusion is expressly forbidden by SOR. The meaning of heating a slice in a microwave is also not preserved without an appliance/location attribute."}, {"item_id": "trial_T20190908_050921_727654#1", "source": "ALFRED (json_2.1.0, train)", "nl": "Put a heated apple in the fridge.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT PickUp\n\nTASK PickUp {\n  ACTION pick_up(target=apple) -> apple : REF[STRING]\n  ACTION heat(target=apple) -> heat : REF[STRING]\n  ACTION place(target=heat, destination=refrigerator, relation=in)\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "The task has a simple explicit causal chain. HNR makes the heat result predictable, though independent translators may instead use apple_2 if heat is modeled as changing the same selected entity rather than producing a new result."}, {"item_id": "django__django-11734", "source": "SWE-bench_Verified (princeton-nlp/SWE-bench_Verified, test, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "OuterRef in exclude() or ~Q() uses wrong model.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT Django\n\nTASK Django {\n  ACTION modify_code(target=django, assertion=assert_outerref_exclude_and_negated_q, call=call_outerref_exclude_negated_q, file=path_django_related_lookups, revision=chg_resolve_outerref_against_outer_model)\n  ACTION run_tests(target=django, assertion=assert_outerref_exclude_and_negated_q)\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "The full code-value category can hold the atomic code facts under N6. SOR gives modify_code before run_tests, but it does not determine whether the three affected files should be three modify_code Actions or a single Action with a list attribute."}, {"item_id": "sympy__sympy-15017", "source": "SWE-bench_Verified (princeton-nlp/SWE-bench_Verified, test, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "`len` of rank-0 arrays returns 0", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT Sympy\n\nTASK Sympy {\n  ACTION modify_code(target=sympy, expected_output=outcome_rank_zero_array_len_one, file=path_sympy_dense_ndim_array, method=sym_ndimarray_len, revision=chg_return_one_for_rank_zero_len)\n  ACTION run_tests(target=sympy, assertion=assert_rank_zero_array_len_one)\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "The software-fix core is expressible with code-value symbols. AOR produces a fixed attribute order, but the proposal does not define a canonical decision between encoding the reproduction, expected value, and implementation change as separate Actions or attributes."}, {"item_id": "c5210", "source": "PRISM (HannahRoseKirk/prism-alignment, conversations config, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "Best way to make mash potato", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT Ask\n\nCONVO Ask {\n  TURN T1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER ask(content=\"Best way to make mash potato\")\n  }\n  TURN T2 SPEAKER=USER REPLY_TO T1 {\n    UTTER ask(content=\"can you make it without peeling the potato\")\n  }\n  TURN T3 SPEAKER=USER REPLY_TO T2 {\n    UTTER correct(content=\"That does not answer the question\")\n  }\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "CSR and TNR improve the user-turn encoding. The supplied trajectory also contains many agent messages; CSR says exactly one TURN per transcript message, but does not define whether the sampled task is the NL prompt alone, all trajectory messages, or a selected user-only transcript."}, {"item_id": "c3804", "source": "PRISM (HannahRoseKirk/prism-alignment, conversations config, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "Why is Donald Trump such an idiot", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT Ask\n\nCONVO Ask {\n  TURN T1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER ask(content=\"Why is Donald Trump such an idiot\")\n  }\n  TURN T2 SPEAKER=USER REPLY_TO T1 {\n    UTTER ask(content=\"Why does Donald Trump promote hate rhetoric\")\n  }\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "The follow-up relation is captured, but the agent turns in the source trajectory are omitted because the scope of the source transcript is undefined. Content remains transitional prose, an existing N3 violation not worsened by this attempt."}, {"item_id": "c892", "source": "PRISM (HannahRoseKirk/prism-alignment, conversations config, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "The people in our country say that we are so generous and warm, but I know that people will look for a chance to take advantage of others as soon as they can.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT Inform\n\nCONVO Inform {\n  TURN T1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER inform(content=\"The people in our country say that we are so generous and warm, but I know that people will look for a chance to take advantage of others as soon as they can.\")\n  }\n  TURN T2 SPEAKER=USER REPLY_TO T1 {\n    UTTER inform(content=\"People here usually don't show their true face to others and that makes me feel like I was born in the wrong country.\")\n  }\n  TURN T3 SPEAKER=USER REPLY_TO T2 {\n    UTTER ask(content=\"Yeah, give me those suggestions.\")\n  }\n  TURN T4 SPEAKER=USER REPLY_TO T3 {\n    UTTER ask(content=\"Can you finish the fourth point?\")\n  }\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "Turn ordinal naming is deterministic, but the assistant messages required by a literal reading of CSR are absent. No canonical rule says whether a request for a continuation should be a new TURN, a REVISES relation, or a request over the previous agent output."}, {"item_id": "c1721", "source": "PRISM (HannahRoseKirk/prism-alignment, conversations config, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "Can you give me some ideas of fantasy books to read?", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT Ask\n\nCONVO Ask {\n  TURN T1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER ask(content=\"Can you give me some ideas of fantasy books to read?\")\n  }\n  TURN T2 SPEAKER=USER REPLY_TO T1 {\n    UTTER ask(content=\"could you tell me a book that is not a series?\")\n  }\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "The follow-up constraint is retained only in prose content; the existing glossary has no formal non-series recommendation constraint. Transcript scope and missing AGENT turns remain unresolved."}, {"item_id": "wildchat1m_en3u-139188", "source": "PATHs (microsoft/prototypical-hai-collaborations, wildchat1m_en3u-task_utterance_wintent_anns, model=gpt-4-1106-preview)", "nl": "hello, which version of chatgpt are you?", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT Ask\n\nCONVO Ask {\n  TURN T1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER ask(content=\"hello, which version of chatgpt are you?\")\n  }\n  TURN T2 SPEAKER=USER REPLY_TO T1 {\n    UTTER ask(content=\"review this research proposal and give me feedback\")\n  }\n  TURN T3 SPEAKER=USER REPLY_TO T2 {\n    UTTER ask(content=\"can you edit the research proposal for me, based on your own feedback?\")\n  }\n  TURN T4 SPEAKER=USER REVISES T3 {\n    UTTER correct(content=\"edit it again without considering the 3000 characters limit\")\n  }\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "CSR clearly selects CONVO because this is a multi-message transcript, and T1-T4 are fixed. It loses the supplied proposal text, all agent turns, and the later request to review again; representing every source message is required by CSR but the proposal does not define transcript extraction from the dataset record."}, {"item_id": "wildchat1m_en3u-34259", "source": "PATHs (microsoft/prototypical-hai-collaborations, wildchat1m_en3u-task_utterance_wintent_anns, model=gpt-3.5-turbo-0301)", "nl": "Write a story about me having a conversation with Priyanka Chopra, based on a real event.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT ArtStory\n\nTASK ArtStory : STRING {\n  GENERATE(target=art_story, content=\"story about conversation with Priyanka Chopra based on a real event\", events=[event_priyanka_cough_and_sound]) -> art_story : STRING\n  RETURN art_story\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "Generate, HNR, and AOR handle the artifact request. The continuation request in the trajectory is not sequenced, and the actual narrative substance remains STRING prose pending N4."}, {"item_id": "wildchat1m_en3u-63931", "source": "PATHs (microsoft/prototypical-hai-collaborations, wildchat1m_en3u-task_utterance_wintent_anns, model=gpt-3.5-turbo-0613)", "nl": "in point form for a slideshow, Political cooperation between Canada and the United States in major international conflicts.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT ArtSlideDeck\n\nTASK ArtSlideDeck : STRING {\n  GENERATE(target=art_slide_deck, constraints=[constraint_few_points], format=format_bullet_list, topic=topic_canada_usa_international_conflict_cooperation) -> art_slide_deck : STRING\n  RETURN art_slide_deck\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "The format and topic structure are captured, but \"less points\" and the later switch to USA contributions are trajectory follow-ups not represented. constraint_few_points must exist in the assumed complete constraint category."}, {"item_id": "wildchat1m_en3u-75393", "source": "PATHs (microsoft/prototypical-hai-collaborations, wildchat1m_en3u-task_utterance_wintent_anns, model=gpt-3.5-turbo-0613)", "nl": "how to cancel meetings without sounding like we just don't have enough information from them", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT ArtEmail\n\nTASK ArtEmail : STRING {\n  GENERATE(target=art_email, constraints=[constraint_avoid_implying_insufficient_information], format=format_email, tone=tone_professional, topic=topic_cancel_meeting) -> art_email : STRING\n  RETURN art_email\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "The follow-up's requested email format is represented, using assumed glossary symbols. Recipient, specific meeting details, and replacement scheduling choices remain absent from the source and cannot be canonically invented."}, {"item_id": "1773878910244", "source": "ThoughtTrace (SCAI-JHU/ThoughtTrace, Parquet column-projected sample, JSON-decoded messages)", "nl": "Hello", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT Acknowledge\n\nCONVO Acknowledge {\n  TURN T1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER acknowledge(content=\"Hello\")\n  }\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "For the literal NL prompt, CSR deterministically yields a one-turn CONVO. For the accompanying multi-turn trajectory, it should instead have one TURN for every user and agent message, demonstrating that the proposal never defines the input boundary on which CSR runs."}, {"item_id": "1775956517287", "source": "ThoughtTrace (SCAI-JHU/ThoughtTrace, Parquet column-projected sample, JSON-decoded messages)", "nl": "Hello, I would like to create a work schedule for month of may for 30 employees, monday to saturday two shifts day shift 6 to 2pm and afternoon shift 2pm to 10 pm shift they must all get 8 days off. I need atleast 10 employees per shift.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT ArtWorkSchedule\n\nTASK ArtWorkSchedule : STRING {\n  GENERATE(target=art_work_schedule, constraints=[constraint_may_schedule, constraint_monday_to_saturday, constraint_two_shifts, constraint_eight_days_off_per_employee, constraint_minimum_ten_employees_per_shift], quantity=30, topic=topic_employee_schedule) -> art_work_schedule : STRING\n  RETURN art_work_schedule\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "Artifact generation covers the output request, but exact shift hours, month/year interpretation, assignments, and staffing feasibility are compressed into assumed atomic constraints rather than structurally modeled. No canonical policy chooses between a generated schedule artifact and a Task that solves a scheduling problem."}, {"item_id": "1773873818460", "source": "ThoughtTrace (SCAI-JHU/ThoughtTrace, Parquet column-projected sample, JSON-decoded messages)", "nl": "Hi, I would like some help with creating a plan to focus on studying", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT ArtStudyPlan\n\nTASK ArtStudyPlan : STRING {\n  GENERATE(target=art_study_plan, topic=topic_focus_studying) -> art_study_plan : STRING\n  RETURN art_study_plan\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "The initial request is expressible as generation. The trajectory adds SQL, one month, one hour daily, night preference, and low motivation, but the proposal does not specify whether later elicited constraints revise a prior Generate request or create subsequent turns."}, {"item_id": "1775943457649", "source": "ThoughtTrace (SCAI-JHU/ThoughtTrace, Parquet column-projected sample, JSON-decoded messages)", "nl": "Hello, i want a detailed plan for the next 2 weeks to study my subjects (blood physiology, lower limb anatomy, blood histology, microbiology and biochemistry). Notice that i cant tolerate studying for long time and i want you to assure that i have a fair free time to work and to learn new soft skill.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT ArtStudyPlan\n\nTASK ArtStudyPlan : STRING {\n  GENERATE(target=art_study_plan, constraints=[constraint_short_study_sessions, constraint_fair_free_time, constraint_include_soft_skill, constraint_friday_full_rest, constraint_thursday_light_day], duration=2, duration_unit=unit_week, topic=topic_medical_subject_study_plan) -> art_study_plan : STRING\n  RETURN art_study_plan\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "The main constraints and subsequent Friday-rest/Thursday-light correction can be represented as atomic constraints, assuming the full glossary. The actual five-subject allocation and day-by-day timetable remain unstructured; moreover, nothing says whether the correction is a REVISES Turn or a new generation request."}]
