      "grammar": "-- No independent grammar production. A normative meta-rule referenced by Task, Conversation, Flow-If, Action, Generate, and Bind wherever those constructs leave a translator choice.",
      "semantics": "Seven sub-rules, each fully mechanical. (1) INPUT-SCOPE RULE (ISR): CSR and every other rule run only over the human-authored messages explicitly present in the item's supplied request field (the 'nl'/prompt/conversation-history input given to the translator) up to and including the final human message — never over a dataset's full recorded trajectory, agent replies, system messages, or evaluation annotations unless those are themselves part of the supplied request text. An assistant/agent message is given its own AGENT TURN only if it literally appears in the supplied request input; it is never fabricated, and it is never omitted if present. (2) CONSTRUCT-SELECTION RULE (CSR): under ISR's scope, if there is more than one human-authored message, or any message carries an explicit correction marker ('actually', 'I meant', 'correcting the above'), or an AGENT message is present in-scope, the request is a CONVO with exactly one TURN per in-scope message in order; otherwise a single human message is a TASK if fulfilling it needs at least one ACTION/GENERATE, else a one-TURN CONVO (UTTER only). REPLY_TO links a message to the nearest earlier message it responds to; REVISES links a correction-marked message to the message it corrects. (3) CONVERSATIONAL-FOLLOW-UP RULE (CFR): a later USER turn referencing an earlier GENERATE/ask is classified by whether it changes stated content: (a) if it alters, negates, or adds a constraint/attribute of the same requested artifact/topic (e.g. 'without peeling the potato', 'less points', 'without the 3000-char limit'), it is REVISES on the turn holding that GENERATE/ask, restating the whole step with the changed attribute (per Conversation's whole-body-replacement rule); (b) if it asks only for continuation/completion of the same artifact with no stated change (e.g. 'finish point 4', 'continue'), it is REPLY_TO (not REVISES) and its GENERATE/UTTER repeats the same topic plus the constraint-value member constraint_continuation; (c) an unrelated new topic is REPLY_TO only, for structural adjacency. (4) DOCUMENT/TURN NAMING RULE (DNR/TNR): the sole top-level TASK/CONVO IDENT and ENTRYPOINT name is PascalCase of the first step's HNR base symbol after SOR fixes order (UTTER's base is its speech-act operation-id). Every TURN IDENT is 'T'+its 1-based ordinal position in document order — never author-chosen. (5) OPERATION-SELECTION RULE (OSR): a source phrase maps to exactly one operation-id via operation-vocabulary's nearest-synonym lookup. Where retrieval criteria (price, rating, category, availability, etc.) are all covered by search_web's attribute signature, the canonical operation-id is always search_web with those criteria as attributes, even if the source describes it as 'filtering' or 'browsing' a UI — select_filter/apply_filters are reserved exclusively for narrowing an already-bound prior list/page where the step introduces no new retrieval target. (6) HANDLE-NAMING RULE (HNR): an Action/GENERATE/task_call `-> IDENT` derives its base name as before (bare vocabulary-category symbol on target -> that symbol verbatim; already-bound REF/STRING/LIST target or absent target -> operation-id; GENERATE -> its target artifact-value symbol; task_call -> callee IDENT snake_case). LET's base is the attribute-name it is first consumed under, else cond_1, cond_2, ... by first appearance. COLLISION RULE: if the computed base string is lexically identical to any bare vocabulary-category symbol that also appears as a literal argument value anywhere in the same Task/Turn body, the base is suffixed with `_ref` before any numeric suffixing (e.g. role_manager -> role_manager_ref) — this makes every bound handle lexically distinguishable from every vocabulary symbol by construction, never by expected type. Ordinary repeated-base numeric suffixing (`_2`, `_3`, ...) applies per body, in source order, after the `_ref` rule if both apply. (7) STEP-ORDERING, MULTIPLICITY AND DECOMPOSITION RULE (SOR): a step is emitted for each operation the request names, using OSR for phrase-to-operation-id mapping, ordered so that a step consuming an earlier step's bound result follows its producer, independent steps follow first-mention order, ties break alphabetically by operation-id. Multiplicity: (a) BOUNDED-LITERAL RULE — when the request states an explicit NUMBER directly modifying a physical-entity noun (e.g. 'two mugs', 'three shirts'), emit that many literal repeated Action sequences (one full sequence per unit) in source order, each independently HNR-suffixed; (b) COLLECTION RULE — when the multiplicity instead comes from a previously-bound LIST[T] (an extract/search/sort result, or an explicitly enumerated list), emit a single FOR EACH over that list with the operation as its body, never unrolled into repeated literal Actions. CAUSAL-CHAIN RULE (CCR) for explicit final-state requests (e.g. 'a hot slice of tomato in the trash can'): the target object's lifecycle is decomposed into the fixed canonical stage order ACQUIRE(pick_up) < TRANSFORM(slice) < CONDITION(heat|chill|rinse) < PLACE, including only the stages whose corresponding property or destination is actually stated in the request (e.g. 'slice' triggers TRANSFORM, 'hot' triggers CONDITION=heat, 'inside X' triggers PLACE), each consuming the prior stage's bound REF as its target. SOR/CCR never fabricate a step for an unstated instrument (e.g. a knife) or an unstated precondition (e.g. opening an appliance); those remain a vocabulary/signature gap for a future sprint, flagged not invented. UI-trajectory logs (recorded clicks, page-filter events) are never preserved as separate Action steps; only the request's own stated goal is decomposed, always normalized to the coarsest operation-id OSR selects. (8) ATTRIBUTE-ORDERING RULE (AOR): `target`/`audience` first if present, then every other supplied attribute alphabetically by attribute-name; no other order is legal.",
