      "construct_name": "Conversation",
      "grammar": "conversation ::= \"CONVO\" IDENT \"{\" turn+ \"}\"\nturn ::= \"TURN\" IDENT \"SPEAKER\" \"=\" speaker_lit ( \"REPLY_TO\" IDENT )? ( \"REVISES\" IDENT )? \"{\" step+ \"}\"\nspeaker_lit ::= \"USER\" | \"AGENT\"\n-- document ::= entrypoint (task | conversation)*",
      "semantics": "CONVO is used only when Canonical-Form's ISR+CSR route a request here: within the supplied request input (never a dataset's full trajectory unless that trajectory is itself the supplied input), more than one in-scope message, an explicit correction marker, an in-scope AGENT message, or a single message answerable purely by UTTER. Exactly one TURN is emitted per in-scope message — never a synthesized AGENT turn beyond what the input itself shows. The CONVO IDENT/ENTRYPOINT name is fixed by DNR from the first Turn's first step. Every TURN IDENT is 'T'+ordinal position (T1, T2, ...) per TNR. A Turn's body may freely mix ACTION and UTTER in its own fresh scope; only a Task's RETURN crosses a Turn/Task boundary. Steps within a Turn body are ordered by SOR. SPEAKER labels USER/AGENT origin without affecting execution. `REPLY_TO` marks structural adjacency to the nearest earlier message it responds to; `REVISES` forms a strictly linear whole-body-replacement chain resolved by `effective()`. Which relation a follow-up message gets (REPLY_TO vs REVISES vs a fresh unrelated turn) is fixed by Canonical-Form's Conversational-Follow-up Rule (CFR): a change to a prior GENERATE/ask's stated content is REVISES; a request only to continue/complete the same artifact is REPLY_TO plus constraint_continuation; an unrelated new ask is REPLY_TO only.",
