  {
   "op": "revise",
   "construct_name": "Bind",
   "grammar": "bind ::= \"LET\" IDENT \":\" type \"=\" value\n-- Additional Bind-site forms: action_result ::= \"->\" IDENT \":\" type ; task_call_result ::= \"->\" IDENT ; utterance_result ::= \"->\" IDENT ; generation_result ::= \"->\" IDENT \":\" type",
   "semantics": "Bind is BrainCode's shared named data-flow rule with five Bind sites: (1) `LET IDENT : type = value`; (2) Action result `-> IDENT : type`; (3) task_call result `-> IDENT`; (4) UTTER result `-> IDENT`, typed STRING; (5) GENERATE result `-> IDENT : type`. LET requires exact static type match; a REF may only be bound by copying an already-visible REF of the same type. Every derived IDENT at sites (1)(2)(3)(5) follows Canonical-Form's Handle-Naming Rule (HNR) exactly, including its mandatory `_ref` suffix whenever the computed base would otherwise collide lexically with a bare vocabulary symbol used elsewhere in the same body — a bound handle and a vocabulary symbol are therefore always lexically distinct tokens, never disambiguated by expected type. Scoping is unchanged: a Task-body binding is visible only to later steps in that body and subsequently entered child scopes; a Turn-body binding likewise never crosses TURN boundaries; THEN/ELSE/FOR bodies scope their own bindings. Shadowing any currently-bound name is a static error.",
   "worked_example": {
