Searcher notes: {'search_mode': 'folder', 'relevant_precedents': ['Python', 'Why Agentic Systems Need Ontologies'], 'phenomena_present': ['determinism', 'synonym-resolution'], 'kpi_fit': {'Python': "Scores highly on determinism through its 'one obvious way to do it' philosophy and strict keyword argument rules, though it relies on convention rather than syntax for alphabetical ordering.", 'Why Agentic Systems Need Ontologies': 'Drives determinism and interpretability by mapping natural language synonyms to a single, canonical structured node, eliminating lexical ambiguity.'}, 'comparable_expression': {'precedent': 'Python + Structured Ontology API', 'nl': 'Urgently email my boss (which must encode identically to: Send an urgent message to the manager)', 'rendering': 'send_message(recipient=Role.MANAGER, tone=Tone.URGENT)'}, 'notes': "N7 enforces strict canonicalization of both syntax and vocabulary, touching BrainCode's Action, Generate, and Utterance constructs (which enforce canonical attribute ordering) as well as Types & Lexicon (glossary symbols). Python's design philosophy and formal Ontologies provide the precedent for mapping diverse NL phrasing into a single, canonical, structurally rigid representation.", 'new_source_suggestion': None, '_search_mode': 'folder'}
Shaper proposal: {"summary": "Fixes the Critic's required changes: Canonical-Form now specifies an explicit multiplicity/decomposition algorithm, a defined CSR input scope, a causal-chain decomposition policy, an operation-selection tie-break rule, and a conversational-follow-up (REPLY_TO vs REVISES) rule; Bind's vocabulary-symbol collision is resolved with a mandatory _ref suffix instead of relying on static type; Generate's new attributes are added to attribute-name vocabulary.", "note_position": "comply", "note_argument": "N7 is a hard note; all required changes are implemented rather than rebutted.", "foundations_overview": null, "rebuttals": [], "changes": [{"op": "add", "construct_name": "Canonical-Form", "grammar": "-- No independent grammar production. A normative meta-rule referenced by Task, Conversation, Flow-If, Action, Generate, and Bind wherever those constructs leave a translator choice.", "semantics": "Seven sub-rules, each fully mechanical. (1) INPUT-SCOPE RULE (ISR): CSR and every other rule run only over the human-authored messages explicitly present in the item's supplied request field (the 'nl'/prompt/conversation-history input given to the translator) up to and including the final human message — never over a dataset's full recorded trajectory, agent replies, system messages, or evaluation annotations unless those are themselves part of the supplied request text. An assistant/agent message is given its own AGENT TURN only if it literally appears in the supplied request input; it is never fabricated, and it is never omitted if present. (2) CONSTRUCT-SELECTION RULE (CSR): under ISR's scope, if there is more than one human-authored message, or any message carries an explicit correction marker ('actually', 'I meant', 'correcting the above'), or an AGENT message is present in-scope, the request is a CONVO with exactly one TURN per in-scope message in order; otherwise a single human message is a TASK if fulfilling it needs at least one ACTION/GENERATE, else a one-TURN CONVO (UTTER only). REPLY_TO links a message to the nearest earlier message it responds to; REVISES links a correction-marked message to the message it corrects. (3) CONVERSATIONAL-FOLLOW-UP RULE (CFR): a later USER turn referencing an earlier GENERATE/ask is classified by whether it changes stated content: (a) if it alters, negates, or adds a constraint/attribute of the same requested artifact/topic (e.g. 'without peeling the potato', 'less points', 'without the 3000-char limit'), it is REVISES on the turn holding that GENERATE/ask, restating the whole step with the changed attribute (per Conversation's whole-body-replacement rule); (b) if it asks only for continuation/completion of the same artifact with no stated change (e.g. 'finish point 4', 'continue'), it is REPLY_TO (not REVISES) and its GENERATE/UTTER repeats the same topic plus the constraint-value member constraint_continuation; (c) an unrelated new topic is REPLY_TO only, for structural adjacency. (4) DOCUMENT/TURN NAMING RULE (DNR/TNR): the sole top-level TASK/CONVO IDENT and ENTRYPOINT name is PascalCase of the first step's HNR base symbol after SOR fixes order (UTTER's base is its speech-act operation-id). Every TURN IDENT is 'T'+its 1-based ordinal position in document order — never author-chosen. (5) OPERATION-SELECTION RULE (OSR): a source phrase maps to exactly one operation-id via operation-vocabulary's nearest-synonym lookup. Where retrieval criteria (price, rating, category, availability, etc.) are all covered by search_web's attribute signature, the canonical operation-id is always search_web with those criteria as attributes, even if the source describes it as 'filtering' or 'browsing' a UI — select_filter/apply_filters are reserved exclusively for narrowing an already-bound prior list/page where the step introduces no new retrieval target. (6) HANDLE-NAMING RULE (HNR): an Action/GENERATE/task_call `-> IDENT` derives its base name as before (bare vocabulary-category symbol on target -> that symbol verbatim; already-bound REF/STRING/LIST target or absent target -> operation-id; GENERATE -> its target artifact-value symbol; task_call -> callee IDENT snake_case). LET's base is the attribute-name it is first consumed under, else cond_1, cond_2, ... by first appearance. COLLISION RULE: if the computed base string is lexically identical to any bare vocabulary-category symbol that also appears as a literal argument value anywhere in the same Task/Turn body, the base is suffixed with `_ref` before any numeric suffixing (e.g. role_manager -> role_manager_ref) — this makes every bound handle lexically distinguishable from every vocabulary symbol by construction, never by expected type. Ordinary repeated-base numeric suffixing (`_2`, `_3`, ...) applies per body, in source order, after the `_ref` rule if both apply. (7) STEP-ORDERING, MULTIPLICITY AND DECOMPOSITION RULE (SOR): a step is emitted for each operation the request names, using OSR for phrase-to-operation-id mapping, ordered so that a step consuming an earlier step's bound result follows its producer, independent steps follow first-mention order, ties break alphabetically by operation-id. Multiplicity: (a) BOUNDED-LITERAL RULE — when the request states an explicit NUMBER directly modifying a physical-entity noun (e.g. 'two mugs', 'three shirts'), emit that many literal repeated Action sequences (one full sequence per unit) in source order, each independently HNR-suffixed; (b) COLLECTION RULE — when the multiplicity instead comes from a previously-bound LIST[T] (an extract/search/sort result, or an explicitly enumerated list), emit a single FOR EACH over that list with the operation as its body, never unrolled into repeated literal Actions. CAUSAL-CHAIN RULE (CCR) for explicit final-state requests (e.g. 'a hot slice of tomato in the trash can'): the target object's lifecycle is decomposed into the fixed canonical stage order ACQUIRE(pick_up) < TRANSFORM(slice) < CONDITION(heat|chill|rinse) < PLACE, including only the stages whose corresponding property or destination is actually stated in the request (e.g. 'slice' triggers TRANSFORM, 'hot' triggers CONDITION=heat, 'inside X' triggers PLACE), each consuming the prior stage's bound REF as its target. SOR/CCR never fabricate a step for an unstated instrument (e.g. a knife) or an unstated precondition (e.g. opening an appliance); those remain a vocabulary/signature gap for a future sprint, flagged not invented. UI-trajectory logs (recorded clicks, page-filter events) are never preserved as separate Action steps; only the request's own stated goal is decomposed, always normalized to the coarsest operation-id OSR selects. (8) ATTRIBUTE-ORDERING RULE (AOR): `target`/`audience` first if present, then every other supplied attribute alphabetically by attribute-name; no other order is legal.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Rinse two mugs one after another, placing each in the coffee maker.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT Mug\n\nTASK Mug {\n  ACTION rinse(target=mug, destination=sink) -> mug : REF[STRING]\n  ACTION place(target=mug, destination=coffee_maker)\n  ACTION rinse(target=mug, destination=sink) -> mug_2 : REF[STRING]\n  ACTION place(target=mug_2, destination=coffee_maker)\n}"}, "glossary_gloss": "Canonical-Form is the fixed rulebook making two independent translators write identical BrainCode: it defines exactly which messages count as 'the request' (ISR), picks TASK vs CONVO and turn/handle names mechanically, resolves which operation-id a phrase maps to, decides when repeated instances become repeated steps versus a FOR EACH loop, decomposes an explicit final-state request into a fixed causal stage order without inventing unstated steps, fixes how a conversational follow-up is REPLY_TO vs REVISES, and fixes attribute order everywhere.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Directly answers all seven required changes: the bounded-literal vs collection multiplicity split resolves the mugs/favorites contradiction; ISR fixes the transcript-scope gap; OSR fixes filter/search ambiguity; CCR gives a bounded, non-fabricating decomposition policy for final-state requests; the _ref collision rule removes reliance on static type for name resolution; CFR gives a canonical REPLY_TO/REVISES choice for generation follow-ups.", "cites_source": "Python", "addresses_note": "N7"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Bind", "grammar": "bind ::= \"LET\" IDENT \":\" type \"=\" value\n-- Additional Bind-site forms: action_result ::= \"->\" IDENT \":\" type ; task_call_result ::= \"->\" IDENT ; utterance_result ::= \"->\" IDENT ; generation_result ::= \"->\" IDENT \":\" type", "semantics": "Bind is BrainCode's shared named data-flow rule with five Bind sites: (1) `LET IDENT : type = value`; (2) Action result `-> IDENT : type`; (3) task_call result `-> IDENT`; (4) UTTER result `-> IDENT`, typed STRING; (5) GENERATE result `-> IDENT : type`. LET requires exact static type match; a REF may only be bound by copying an already-visible REF of the same type. Every derived IDENT at sites (1)(2)(3)(5) follows Canonical-Form's Handle-Naming Rule (HNR) exactly, including its mandatory `_ref` suffix whenever the computed base would otherwise collide lexically with a bare vocabulary symbol used elsewhere in the same body — a bound handle and a vocabulary symbol are therefore always lexically distinct tokens, never disambiguated by expected type. Scoping is unchanged: a Task-body binding is visible only to later steps in that body and subsequently entered child scopes; a Turn-body binding likewise never crosses TURN boundaries; THEN/ELSE/FOR bodies scope their own bindings. Shadowing any currently-bound name is a static error.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Rinse one mug in the sink, then place that same selected mug in the coffee maker.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT Mug\n\nTASK Mug {\n  ACTION rinse(target=mug, destination=sink) -> mug : REF[STRING]\n  ACTION place(target=mug, destination=coffee_maker)\n}"}, "glossary_gloss": "Bind gives a value a name for later steps. Every derived handle now follows Canonical-Form's Handle-Naming Rule, and a handle that would otherwise read identically to a vocabulary symbol always gets a `_ref` suffix, so a reader never has to infer which meaning a token has from context.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Replaces the rejected type-dependent collision exception with a purely lexical rule (_ref suffix), which is resolvable by a parser with no type inference at all.", "cites_source": "Python", "addresses_note": "N7"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Flow-If", "grammar": "flow_if ::= \"IF\" check \"THEN\" \"{\" step+ \"}\" ( \"ELSE\" \"{\" step+ \"}\" )?", "semantics": "Conditional branching, identical inside TASK and CONVO TURN bodies. The Check is evaluated when control reaches the IF; THEN runs if true, ELSE (if present) if false, otherwise control passes on. THEN/ELSE each open a fresh child scope, invisible to each other and to siblings. An IF nested in a TURN body may contain UTTER exactly as ACTION; the same nesting in a TASK body is a static error per Utterance's restriction. A preceding Action bound for use in the Check follows Canonical-Form's HNR, including the mandatory `_ref` suffix when the derived base would collide with a vocabulary symbol used as that Action's own `target`. When a Check depends on a preceding `check_replied` with a future `deadline`, the branch inherits that deferred timing.", "worked_example": {"nl": "If my manager hasn't replied by Friday, send a polite follow-up; otherwise do nothing.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT CheckReplied\n\nTASK CheckReplied CONTEXT execution_date=\"2025-06-03\" {\n  ACTION check_replied(target=role_manager, deadline=\"2025-06-06T23:59:59Z\") -> role_manager_ref : BOOL\n  IF NOT role_manager_ref THEN {\n    ACTION send_email(recipient=role_manager, tone=tone_polite, content=\"follow-up\")\n  }\n}", "glossary_gloss": "Flow-If branches on a Check, running THEN or the optional ELSE in its own fresh scope. Its worked example now shows the `_ref`-suffixed handle (role_manager_ref) produced whenever a bound result's base would otherwise read identically to the vocabulary symbol role_manager used elsewhere in the same body."}, "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Applies Bind's revised collision rule so the example is internally consistent rather than relying on type-based disambiguation.", "cites_source": null, "addresses_note": "N7"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Task", "grammar": "document ::= entrypoint (task | conversation)*\ntask ::= \"TASK\" IDENT param_list? (\"CONTEXT\" execution_date_param)? (\":\" type)? (\"DECREASES\" IDENT)? \"{\" step+ return_stmt? \"}\"\nexecution_date_param ::= \"execution_date\" \"=\" ISO_DATE\nparam_list ::= \"(\" param (\",\" param)* \")\"\nparam ::= IDENT \":\" type\nreturn_stmt ::= \"RETURN\" value\nstep ::= action | flow_if | flow_for | bind | task_call | utterance | generation\ntask_call ::= IDENT arg_list? ( \"->\" IDENT )?\narg_list ::= \"(\" arg (\",\" arg)* \")\"\narg ::= IDENT \"=\" value\n-- ISO_DATE ::= exactly YYYY-MM-DD.", "semantics": "A document declares exactly one ENTRYPOINT naming a top-level TASK or CONVO, chosen by Canonical-Form's Input-Scope Rule (ISR) and Construct-Selection Rule (CSR) over the supplied request input only. The TASK's IDENT (and ENTRYPOINT name) is fixed by the Document-Naming Rule (DNR) from the first step's HNR base, after SOR fixes step order. If ENTRYPOINT names a TASK it takes zero parameters and runs once; other top-level TASKs are inert library declarations. `CONTEXT execution_date=ISO_DATE` is translation-time-only, resolving relative dates once, retained for audit, never re-read at runtime; malformed values are static errors. Task/Variable namespaces are disjoint; scoping is lexical and block-structured. `param_list`/`arg_list` use exact static type matching; a declared result type requires a matching terminal RETURN, else RETURN is forbidden. Recursion requires `DECREASES paramName` plus a per-call guarding IF-THEN. Steps are ordered, deduplicated/multiplied, and decomposed per Canonical-Form's SOR (including its Operation-Selection, Multiplicity, and Causal-Chain sub-rules), and every attribute list follows AOR.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Go to the flight booking site, search flights from Tel Aviv to Berlin next Tuesday, and list the three cheapest options.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT OpenPage\n\nTASK OpenPage CONTEXT execution_date=\"2025-06-03\" : LIST[REF[STRING]] {\n  ACTION open_page(target=\"flight_booking_site\")\n  ACTION search_travel(date=\"2025-06-10\", destination=\"Berlin\", origin=\"Tel Aviv\") -> search_travel : LIST[REF[STRING]]\n  ACTION sort(target=search_travel, ordering=\"price_asc\") -> sort : LIST[REF[STRING]]\n  ACTION extract(limit=3, target=sort) -> extract : LIST[REF[STRING]]\n  RETURN extract\n}"}, "glossary_gloss": "Task is a document-level container of steps, chosen over CONVO whenever the supplied request input needs at least one ACTION/GENERATE. Its name, handles, and step order (including multiplicity and causal decomposition) are fixed mechanically by Canonical-Form.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Updates Task's cross-reference to the fully specified Canonical-Form (ISR/OSR/multiplicity/CCR) so the construct's own text no longer understates what SOR actually does.", "cites_source": "Why Agentic Systems Need Ontologies", "addresses_note": "N7"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Conversation", "grammar": "conversation ::= \"CONVO\" IDENT \"{\" turn+ \"}\"\nturn ::= \"TURN\" IDENT \"SPEAKER\" \"=\" speaker_lit ( \"REPLY_TO\" IDENT )? ( \"REVISES\" IDENT )? \"{\" step+ \"}\"\nspeaker_lit ::= \"USER\" | \"AGENT\"\n-- document ::= entrypoint (task | conversation)*", "semantics": "CONVO is used only when Canonical-Form's ISR+CSR route a request here: within the supplied request input (never a dataset's full trajectory unless that trajectory is itself the supplied input), more than one in-scope message, an explicit correction marker, an in-scope AGENT message, or a single message answerable purely by UTTER. Exactly one TURN is emitted per in-scope message — never a synthesized AGENT turn beyond what the input itself shows. The CONVO IDENT/ENTRYPOINT name is fixed by DNR from the first Turn's first step. Every TURN IDENT is 'T'+ordinal position (T1, T2, ...) per TNR. A Turn's body may freely mix ACTION and UTTER in its own fresh scope; only a Task's RETURN crosses a Turn/Task boundary. Steps within a Turn body are ordered by SOR. SPEAKER labels USER/AGENT origin without affecting execution. `REPLY_TO` marks structural adjacency to the nearest earlier message it responds to; `REVISES` forms a strictly linear whole-body-replacement chain resolved by `effective()`. Which relation a follow-up message gets (REPLY_TO vs REVISES vs a fresh unrelated turn) is fixed by Canonical-Form's Conversational-Follow-up Rule (CFR): a change to a prior GENERATE/ask's stated content is REVISES; a request only to continue/complete the same artifact is REPLY_TO plus constraint_continuation; an unrelated new ask is REPLY_TO only.", "worked_example": {"nl": "User: 'Best way to make mash potato.' User: 'can you make it without peeling the potato.'", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT Ask\n\nCONVO Ask {\n  TURN T1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER ask(topic=topic_mash_potato_method)\n  }\n  TURN T2 SPEAKER=USER REVISES T1 {\n    UTTER ask(constraints=[constraint_no_peeling], topic=topic_mash_potato_method)\n  }\n}"}, "glossary_gloss": "Conversation (CONVO/TURN) is reserved for a supplied multi-message request or a single message with no computable action. Document name and TURN names are fixed by Canonical-Form, and CFR now fixes whether a follow-up message is REPLY_TO, REVISES, or an unrelated new turn, instead of leaving that choice to the translator.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Applies ISR to fix transcript-scope ambiguity and CFR to fix the REPLY_TO-vs-REVISES ambiguity the Critic's mash-potato and wildchat examples exposed.", "cites_source": "Why Agentic Systems Need Ontologies", "addresses_note": "N7"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Action", "grammar": "action ::= \"ACTION\" operation-id \"(\" action-attrs? \")\" (\"->\" IDENT \":\" type)?\naction-attrs ::= action-attr (\",\" action-attr)*\naction-attr ::= attribute-name \"=\" value\noperation-id ::= operation-vocabulary member", "semantics": "An Action's attribute list must be valid by category and signature. `operation-id` is chosen by Canonical-Form's Operation-Selection Rule (OSR), which prefers search_web whenever retrieval criteria fall within its signature, over select_filter/apply_filters. Canonical attribute order follows Canonical-Form's Attribute-Ordering Rule (AOR): `target` first if present, then every other attribute alphabetically. The result handle's IDENT (`-> IDENT : type`) is fixed by Canonical-Form's Handle-Naming Rule (HNR), including its `_ref` collision suffix.", "worked_example": {"nl": "On the electronics store, filter laptops to 16 GB RAM or more under $1,200, and add the best-rated one to the cart.", "braincode": "ACTION search_web(target=laptop, max_price=1200, min_ram_gb=16) -> laptop : LIST[REF[STRING]]\nACTION sort(rank_direction=dir_desc, rank_field=rank_rating, target=laptop) -> sort : LIST[REF[STRING]]\nACTION extract(limit=1, target=sort) -> extract : LIST[REF[STRING]]\nACTION add_to_cart(target=extract)"}, "glossary_gloss": "Action executes an external operation. Its operation-id is picked by OSR's search_web-over-filter precedence, its attribute order and result-handle naming follow Canonical-Form's single AOR/HNR rules rather than translator choice.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Replaces the ambiguous select_filter-vs-search_web choice the Critic flagged with OSR's deterministic precedence, and shows the resolved handle naming.", "cites_source": "Python", "addresses_note": "N7"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Generate", "grammar": "generation ::= \"GENERATE\" \"(\" generate-attr (\",\" generate-attr)* \")\" \"->\" IDENT \":\" gen_type\ngenerate-attr ::= attribute-name \"=\" value\ngen_type ::= \"NUMBER\" | \"STRING\" | \"BOOL\" | \"LIST\" \"[\" gen_type \"]\"", "semantics": "Legal Generate attributes: target, audience, content, topic, constraints, transformations, events, character_properties, duration, duration_unit, format, locale, quantity, quantity_unit, style, tone, stop_requirement, stop_min_per_day, max_walk_duration, max_walk_duration_unit — every one of these is a governed attribute-name member (see attribute-name vocabulary). `target` (always present) -> artifact-value and is also this step's HNR base, used verbatim as the bound result's IDENT unless the `_ref` collision rule applies. `audience`->recipient-value; `tone`->tone-value; `format`->format-value; `style`->style-value; `locale`->locale-value; `topic`->topic-value; `constraints`->LIST[constraint-value]; `transformations`->LIST[transformation-value]; `events`->LIST[event-value]; `character_properties`->LIST[character-property-value]; `stop_requirement`->semantic-category-value; `duration_unit`/`max_walk_duration_unit`/`quantity_unit`->duration-unit-value. `duration`, `quantity`, `stop_min_per_day`, `max_walk_duration` are NUMBER. `content` remains a transitional STRING channel pending N4, used only where no topic/content-value member yet exists. Canonical attribute order and result handle follow AOR/HNR.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Plan a 3-day Kyoto itinerary that includes at least one temple per day and avoids more than a 20-minute walk from the last stop.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT ArtItinerary\n\nTASK ArtItinerary : STRING {\n  GENERATE(target=art_itinerary, duration=3, duration_unit=unit_day, max_walk_duration=20, max_walk_duration_unit=unit_minute, stop_min_per_day=1, stop_requirement=class_temple, topic=topic_kyoto_trip) -> art_itinerary : STRING\n  RETURN art_itinerary\n}"}, "glossary_gloss": "GENERATE builds a non-effectful artifact. Its legal attributes now all correspond to real governed attribute-name entries (topic, constraints, transformations, events, character_properties, quantity_unit included), and its bound result is named after the artifact-value symbol via HNR.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Reconciles Generate's signature with the attribute-name vocabulary, closing the gap the Critic identified between attributes used in examples and attributes actually declared as governed.", "cites_source": null, "addresses_note": "N7"}, {"op": "revise", "kind": "vocabulary", "construct_name": "attribute-name", "glossary_gloss": "This is the index of legal attribute names and the exact current category or literal rule for each attribute. An attribute marked N3 violation still accepts STRING during the build phase, but its listed replacement category is a required next step rather than a present governing value set.", "closed": false, "members": [{"symbol": "target", "gloss": "Action subject: entity-name; Generate artifact: artifact-value.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "recipient", "gloss": "Message recipient from recipient-value.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "audience", "gloss": "Artifact audience from recipient-value.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "tone", "gloss": "Tone from tone-value.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "format", "gloss": "Output form from format-value.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "style", "gloss": "Production style from style-value.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "locale", "gloss": "Locale from locale-value.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "topic", "gloss": "Subject matter from topic-value.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "constraints", "gloss": "LIST of requirements/exclusions from constraint-value.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "transformations", "gloss": "LIST of structural changes from transformation-value.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "events", "gloss": "LIST of narrative events from event-value.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "character_properties", "gloss": "LIST of character traits from character-property-value.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "duration_unit", "gloss": "Duration unit from duration-unit-value.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "max_walk_duration_unit", "gloss": "Walking-duration unit from duration-unit-value.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "quantity_unit", "gloss": "Quantity unit from duration-unit-value (e.g. unit_word, unit_paragraph).", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "currency", "gloss": "Currency from currency-value.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "state", "gloss": "Object condition from state-value.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "shape", "gloss": "Object shape from shape-value.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "color", "gloss": "Product color from product-attribute-value.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "size", "gloss": "Product size from product-attribute-value.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "gender", "gloss": "Product demographic facet from product-attribute-value.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "metric", "gloss": "Queryable status from metric-value.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "relation", "gloss": "Placement relation from spatial-relation.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "destination", "gloss": "Destination from entity-name or location-name.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "source", "gloss": "Source from entity-name or location-name.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "origin", "gloss": "Travel origin from location-name.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "location", "gloss": "Location from location-name.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "stop_requirement", "gloss": "Required itinerary-stop class from semantic-category-value.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "quantity", "gloss": "Count as NUMBER.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "duration", "gloss": "Duration magnitude as NUMBER.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "stop_min_per_day", "gloss": "Daily minimum count as NUMBER.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "max_walk_duration", "gloss": "Consecutive-stop walking bound as NUMBER.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "file", "gloss": "N3 violation: STRING pending code-path-value.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "method", "gloss": "N3 violation: STRING pending code-symbol-value.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "property", "gloss": "N3 violation: STRING pending code-symbol-value.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "call", "gloss": "N3 violation: STRING pending code-call-value.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "assertion", "gloss": "N3 violation: STRING pending code-assertion-value.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "expected_output", "gloss": "N3 violation: STRING pending code-outcome-value.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "actual_output", "gloss": "N3 violation: STRING pending code-outcome-value.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "reproduction_step", "gloss": "N3 violation: STRING pending code-repro-value.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "revision", "gloss": "N3 violation: STRING pending code-change-value.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "procedure", "gloss": "N3 violation: STRING pending procedure-value.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "food", "gloss": "N3 violation: STRING pending food-value.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "meal", "gloss": "N3 violation: STRING pending meal-value.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "message_structure", "gloss": "N3 violation: STRING pending message-structure-value.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "content", "gloss": "N3 violation: STRING pending symbolic message-content-value.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "policy", "gloss": "N3 violation: STRING pending policy-value.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "amenity", "gloss": "N3 violation: STRING pending amenity-value.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "ticket", "gloss": "N3 violation: STRING pending ticket-value.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "seat", "gloss": "N3 violation: STRING pending seat-value.", "nl_synonyms": []}], "membership_rule": "All other existing listed attributes retain their current explicitly named category or literal rule: dates use ISO_DATE, deadlines/showtimes use UTC_TIMESTAMP, booleans use BOOL, numeric bounds use NUMBER, search fields use search-value, platform uses platform-name. No STRING-governed member is N3-compliant; every such member must name a concrete replacement category.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Write exactly 2 paragraphs about a decision study, avoiding flowery language.", "braincode": "GENERATE(target=art_short_text, constraints=[constraint_exclude_flowery_language], quantity=2, quantity_unit=unit_paragraph, topic=topic_decision_study) -> art_short_text : STRING"}, "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Adds topic, constraints, transformations, events, character_properties, and quantity_unit as governed attribute-name members so Generate's signature no longer claims categories that the attribute glossary doesn't declare.", "addresses_note": "N7"}], "_provider_used": "anthropic", "_model_used": "claude-sonnet-5"}
Critic assessment: {"decision": "needs-rework", "kpi_assessment": {"coverage": {"impact": "mixed", "reasoning": "The proposed ISR correctly prevents recorded trajectories from being mistaken for request content, and OSR usefully normalizes retrieval requests. However, CCR gives no usable acquisition rule for imperative placement requests: \"Put two forks in the sink\" reaches PLACE from its destination but supplies neither a preceding pick_up nor a valid REF target. The unsupported relative date in held-trip Mind2Web input also has no deterministic resolution without a supplied execution date."}, "expressivity": {"impact": "mixed", "reasoning": "The new multiplicity, collection, correction, and generation-attribute rules preserve several constraints that were previously translator choices. But CCR loses the required acquisition action for ALFRED placement tasks, and the new rule that UI trajectories are never preserved can erase explicitly requested navigation or UI operations if they are part of the supplied request rather than merely evaluation logs."}, "determinism": {"impact": "benefit", "reasoning": "ISR, fixed TURN naming, AOR, bounded-literal versus collection multiplicity, and the REPLY_TO/REVISES policy are substantial progress toward N7. Nevertheless, N7 is not yet upheld: operation-vocabulary's \"nearest-synonym lookup\" has no defined distance, tie-break, phrase segmentation, or synonym coverage, while HNR examples contradict the mandatory collision rule. Different translators can still choose different operation segments and handles."}, "interpretability": {"impact": "mixed", "reasoning": "The explicit ISR and named subrules make the intended normalization policy easier to inspect. Conversely, the phrase \"as before\" in HNR leaves key handle derivation outside this rule, and several worked examples violate the stated collision rule, so a reader cannot reliably infer which form is normative."}, "improvement": {"impact": "mixed", "reasoning": "Canonical ordering and explicit data-flow naming would help a weaker model execute search, sorting, and conversational corrections. The missing acquisition policy produces an under-specified household plan, however, and is worse than a decomposition that retains pick_up followed by place."}}, "simulations": [{"item_id": "held-trip-1", "source": "seed_tasks", "nl": "Plan a 3-day itinerary in Kyoto that includes at least one temple per day and avoids anything more than a 20-minute walk from the last stop.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT ArtItinerary\n\nTASK ArtItinerary : STRING {\n  GENERATE(target=art_itinerary, duration=3, duration_unit=unit_day, max_walk_duration=20, max_walk_duration_unit=unit_minute, stop_min_per_day=1, stop_requirement=class_temple, topic=topic_kyoto_trip) -> art_itinerary_ref : STRING\n  RETURN art_itinerary_ref\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "Generate and attribute-name additions encode duration, temple minimum, and walking bound. HNR requires _ref because art_itinerary is also a bare vocabulary symbol in this body; the proposal's own Generate example does not follow that rule."}, {"item_id": "dev-email-2", "source": "seed_tasks", "nl": "If my manager hasn't replied by Friday, send a polite follow-up; otherwise do nothing.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT CheckReplied\n\nTASK CheckReplied CONTEXT execution_date=\"2025-06-03\" {\n  ACTION check_replied(target=role_manager, deadline=\"2025-06-06T23:59:59Z\") -> role_manager_ref : BOOL\n  IF NOT role_manager_ref THEN {\n    ACTION send_email(recipient=role_manager, tone=tone_polite, content=\"follow-up\")\n  }\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "Flow-If's revised example and collision suffix support this item. The exact date still depends on an externally supplied execution_date, which is not contained in the NL request itself."}, {"item_id": "fb7741f6-f388-4535-903d-d07315ea995e", "source": "Mind2Web (osunlp/Mind2Web, train, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "Find fitness events for this weekend.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT SearchWeb\n\nTASK SearchWeb {\n  ACTION search_web(target=fitness_event, date=\"this weekend\") -> fitness_event_ref : LIST[REF[STRING]]\n}", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "ISR correctly excludes the click trajectory. But \"this weekend\" cannot be deterministically resolved without a supplied execution_date, and date=\"this weekend\" is not an ISO_DATE value. Canonical-Form needs a required anchor or a defined relative-date representation."}, {"item_id": "3cb44998-5a0c-44c5-84b7-ffb729a356d8", "source": "Mind2Web (osunlp/Mind2Web, train, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "Find the schedule between New York and Washington DC sorted by shortest trip.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT SearchTransit\n\nTASK SearchTransit : LIST[REF[STRING]] {\n  ACTION search_transit(origin=new_york, destination=washington_dc) -> search_transit : LIST[REF[STRING]]\n  ACTION sort(rank_direction=dir_asc, rank_field=rank_duration, target=search_transit) -> sort : LIST[REF[STRING]]\n  RETURN sort\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "ISR appropriately omits the trajectory. This assumes the complete glossary has new_york, washington_dc, and rank_duration under existing categories as directed by N6."}, {"item_id": "trial_T20190907_070406_821176#1", "source": "ALFRED (json_2.1.0, train)", "nl": "Put two forks in the sink.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT Place\n\nTASK Place {\n  ACTION place(target=fork, destination=sink)\n  ACTION place(target=fork, destination=sink)\n}", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "Bounded-literal multiplicity mechanically produces two sequences, but CCR says destination triggers PLACE while supplying no rule that imperative placement entails ACQUIRE. The emitted place actions lack independently selected REF targets and do not preserve the actionable pick-up-and-place goal."}, {"item_id": "trial_T20190909_013633_346723#1", "source": "ALFRED (json_2.1.0, train)", "nl": "Place a baseball bat on a bed", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT Place\n\nTASK Place {\n  ACTION place(target=baseball_bat, destination=bed, relation=on)\n}", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "CCR has the same acquisition gap as the two-forks item. The recorded walking steps are correctly excluded by ISR, but the request itself needs selecting/acquiring the bat before placement."}, {"item_id": "django__django-14792", "source": "SWE-bench_Verified (princeton-nlp/SWE-bench_Verified, test, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "Reverse time zone conversion in Trunc()/Extract() database functions.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT ModifyCode\n\nTASK ModifyCode {\n  ACTION modify_code(target=django, file=path_django_utils_timezone_py, method=sym_timezone_mixin_get_tzname, revision=chg_reverse_trunc_extract_timezone_conversion)\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "The actionable core is expressible through the existing code-value category under N6. ISR correctly ignores the trajectory as a source of additional steps."}, {"item_id": "django__django-15561", "source": "SWE-bench_Verified (princeton-nlp/SWE-bench_Verified, test, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "AlterField operation should be noop when adding/changing choices on SQLite.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT ModifyCode\n\nTASK ModifyCode {\n  ACTION modify_code(target=django, method=sym_alterfield, revision=chg_alterfield_choices_sqlite_noop)\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "The core requested code behavior is captured with code-value members assumed available under N6. The detailed regression-test expectation would need an additional assertion if it were stated as an explicit requested step."}, {"item_id": "c6082", "source": "PRISM (HannahRoseKirk/prism-alignment, conversations config, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "How old should be my child before I give him alcohol?", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT Ask\n\nCONVO Ask {\n  TURN T1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER ask(content=\"How old should be my child before I give him alcohol?\")\n  }\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "Under ISR, the displayed trajectory is excluded because only nl is the supplied request field. The question's substance remains transitional STRING prose; this is an existing N4/N5 violation, not introduced by this proposal."}, {"item_id": "c1314", "source": "PRISM (HannahRoseKirk/prism-alignment, conversations config, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "Where does Christinaity originate from?", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT Ask\n\nCONVO Ask {\n  TURN T1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER ask(content=\"Where does Christianity originate from?\")\n  }\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "CSR deterministically routes this single non-effectful question to a one-turn CONVO. Its factual substance remains an opaque STRING channel."}, {"item_id": "c329", "source": "PRISM (HannahRoseKirk/prism-alignment, conversations config, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "What's your opinion about the neurodiversity movement?", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT Ask\n\nCONVO Ask {\n  TURN T1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER ask(content=\"What is your opinion about the neurodiversity movement?\")\n  }\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "ISR excludes later conversation turns. The request form is retained, but opinion subject and requested standpoint are prose because UTTER still does not permit topic or constraints despite Conversation examples using topic."}, {"item_id": "c5505", "source": "PRISM (HannahRoseKirk/prism-alignment, conversations config, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "Should I have had a fourth child?", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT Ask\n\nCONVO Ask {\n  TURN T1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER ask(content=\"Should I have had a fourth child?\")\n  }\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "The single supplied user request canonically becomes a one-turn CONVO. The personal-decision substance remains in transitional STRING."}, {"item_id": "wildchat1m_en3u-134541", "source": "PATHs (microsoft/prototypical-hai-collaborations, wildchat1m_en3u-task_utterance_wintent_anns, model=gpt-3.5-turbo-0125)", "nl": "Suggest colors for 51 Pegasi b", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT Ask\n\nCONVO Ask {\n  TURN T1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER ask(content=\"Suggest colors for 51 Pegasi b\")\n  }\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "ISR avoids fabricating turns from the displayed assistant/user history. The subject and requested color suggestions have no structured UTTER representation."}, {"item_id": "wildchat1m_en3u-84979", "source": "PATHs (microsoft/prototypical-hai-collaborations, wildchat1m_en3u-task_utterance_wintent_anns, model=gpt-3.5-turbo-0613)", "nl": "Please create a dialogue with more than 4500 characters between Amanda, Jessica and Jake.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT ArtDialogue\n\nTASK ArtDialogue : STRING {\n  GENERATE(target=art_dialogue, content=\"dialogue between Amanda, Jessica, and Jake with the supplied family, surgery, height, coin-flip, and one-year-later story premises\", quantity=4500, quantity_unit=unit_character) -> art_dialogue_ref : STRING\n  RETURN art_dialogue_ref\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "Generate and quantity_unit help represent artifact type and length, but unit_character is not currently listed in duration-unit-value and the extensive required plot is retained as prose. The later follow-up is outside ISR scope because it is not in nl."}, {"item_id": "wildchat1m_en3u-37470", "source": "PATHs (microsoft/prototypical-hai-collaborations, wildchat1m_en3u-task_utterance_wintent_anns, model=gpt-3.5-turbo-0301)", "nl": "Can ECS access SNTP server instead of using NTP server ?", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT Ask\n\nCONVO Ask {\n  TURN T1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER ask(content=\"Can ECS access an SNTP server instead of an NTP server?\")\n  }\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "CSR yields a one-turn question and ISR excludes the transcript. The technical comparison remains prose because no structured protocol/capability category is available to UTTER."}, {"item_id": "wildchat1m_en3u-45091", "source": "PATHs (microsoft/prototypical-hai-collaborations, wildchat1m_en3u-task_utterance_wintent_anns, model=gpt-3.5-turbo-0301)", "nl": "How would someone discibe the surface texture of the catwalk greatings on Oil Rigs? Then secondley how would you discribe the Specular Highlights on such gratings?", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT Ask\n\nCONVO Ask {\n  TURN T1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER ask(content=\"Describe the surface texture and specular highlights of oil-rig catwalk gratings.\")\n  }\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "The two related questions are retained as one utterance, but the requested physical-description content is prose."}, {"item_id": "1775708563506", "source": "ThoughtTrace (SCAI-JHU/ThoughtTrace, Parquet column-projected sample, JSON-decoded messages)", "nl": "i am planning a trip to sri lanka in may , could you help me , i have a total budget of 25,000rs", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT ArtPlan\n\nTASK ArtPlan : STRING {\n  GENERATE(target=art_plan, content=\"Sri Lanka trip in May with total budget 25000 INR\") -> art_plan_ref : STRING\n  RETURN art_plan_ref\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "The planned artifact is structured, but budget, currency, month, and trip subject cannot all be represented by the current Generate signature without content prose."}, {"item_id": "1775428662074", "source": "ThoughtTrace (SCAI-JHU/ThoughtTrace, Parquet column-projected sample, JSON-decoded messages)", "nl": "Hello, I wish to understand somoething in Polish...that might be a slang....will you help me with translation and context maybe?", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT Ask\n\nCONVO Ask {\n  TURN T1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER ask(content=\"Help translate and explain the context of possible Polish slang.\")\n  }\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "The requested speech act is captured, but language, translation, slang, and context requirements remain a STRING payload."}, {"item_id": "1775953097591", "source": "ThoughtTrace (SCAI-JHU/ThoughtTrace, Parquet column-projected sample, JSON-decoded messages)", "nl": "Hello there. What do we call you?", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT Ask\n\nCONVO Ask {\n  TURN T1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER ask(content=\"What do we call you?\")\n  }\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "ISR limits the translation to the supplied opening question, not the later children's-story exchange. The question substance remains transitional prose."}, {"item_id": "1776049257672", "source": "ThoughtTrace (SCAI-JHU/ThoughtTrace, Parquet column-projected sample, JSON-decoded messages)", "nl": "How to perform cpr on my dog?", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT Ask\n\nCONVO Ask {\n  TURN T1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER ask(content=\"How to perform CPR on my dog?\")\n  }\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "A one-turn information request is structurally represented, while the medical/veterinary procedure and safety context remain prose."}], "cross_check": {"used": false, "agreement": "n/a", "notes": "No independent provider translations were supplied. Self-simulation indicates likely agreement on ISR and basic CSR, but low convergence on operation segmentation, nearest-synonym selection, omitted causal prerequisites, and HNR collision handling."}, "required_changes": [{"target": "doc hygiene (scripted — blocks acceptance)", "change": "Doc hygiene FAILED — `Flow-If` missing glossary_gloss"}, {"target": "Canonical-Form", "change": "Repair HNR and every affected worked example. Define the computed base without referring to unspecified prior rules, and apply the collision rule consistently: if a bare vocabulary symbol is used as target/value in the body, a result handle derived from it must be suffixed _ref. Correct the rinse, Action search, and Generate examples, which currently violate their own mandatory rule."}, {"target": "Canonical-Form", "change": "Replace \"nearest-synonym lookup\" with a complete deterministic operation-mapping algorithm: define source phrase segmentation, the finite synonym table or a glossary-defined normalization relation, how compound clauses map to operations, and tie-breaking when multiple operation members match. Do not rely on an undefined notion of semantic nearness."}, {"target": "Canonical-Form", "change": "Repair CCR for imperative movement/placement. State whether a direct request to put/place an entity entails ACQUIRE followed by PLACE; if so, require pick_up and bind its REF before place. Define how plural per-unit sequences bind and consume each distinct REF. If acquisition is intentionally not implicit, reject direct placement as unrepresentable rather than emitting a place action with an unbound descriptor target."}, {"target": "Canonical-Form", "change": "Define deterministic treatment of relative temporal expressions when no execution_date is supplied. Either require translation input to supply an execution-date anchor and reject otherwise, or add a governed relative-date value representation; never emit a prose value such as \"this weekend\" for a date attribute."}, {"target": "Conversation", "change": "Reconcile UTTER's legal-attribute signature with the proposed and existing Conversation examples. Either add topic and constraints to UTTER with governed categories and canonical ordering, or rewrite those examples to use only UTTER's legal attributes."}, {"target": "Flow-If", "change": "Add Flow-If's glossary_gloss as the required top-level change field, not nested inside worked_example, so the scripted doc-hygiene check passes."}, {"target": "duration-unit-value", "change": "If Generate is meant to encode character counts, add unit_character with an exact rendering/counting definition, or prohibit quantity_unit=unit_character and retain the length constraint through a dedicated governed alternative."}], "logic_issues": ["Doc hygiene failed because Flow-If has no top-level glossary_gloss; acceptance is blocked independently of the substantive review.", "Canonical-Form's rinse example binds -> mug although bare mug appears as target in the same body, directly contradicting its mandatory _ref collision rule.", "The revised Action example binds -> laptop despite bare target=lap