      "construct_name": "canonical-form",
      "grammar": "-- No independent production. Normative translation rules used by TASK, CONVO, ACTION, GENERATE, UTTER, Bind, and Flow-If.",
      "semantics": "Canonical Form applies to the complete supplied input. A supplied dataset transcript is its ordered, explicitly delimited messages; a plain single text is one message and is never split at punctuation. One supplied message requiring ACTION or GENERATE emits a TASK; one supplied message containing only speech acts emits a CONVO; two or more supplied messages always emit one CONVO with one TURN per message in supplied order. Thus a final artifact request following earlier corrections remains a USER UTTER in its final CONVO turn; it does not discard earlier turns or emit a standalone TASK. TURN names are t1, t2, ... . A message explicitly replacing an earlier request uses REVISES that earlier turn's current lineage tip; every other later message uses REPLY_TO the immediately preceding turn. Declaration and ENTRYPOINT names are PascalCase of the first executable step base: a bound Action or Generate uses its result handle with terminal _ref and numeric suffix removed; an unbound Action uses operation-id; an unbound UTTER uses speech-act; an unbound GENERATE uses target. AOR writes target first when present, then all remaining attributes in ASCII alphabetical order. HNR: an Action result base is target when target is a literal vocabulary symbol, otherwise operation-id; a Generate base is target; a task-call base is callee; a bound UTTER base is speech-act; LET base is its declared IDENT. A literal vocabulary symbol is an unquoted member token written directly in a value position, not an IDENT reference previously bound by LET or ->. For an Action returning REF[T], append _ref to the base; all other results use the base unchanged. Append _2, _3, ... for repeated bases in source order. SOR puts each producer before a consumer; otherwise preserves supplied-message order and then source-span order; exact ties use operation-id order. OSR NFC-normalizes and Unicode-casefolds the source, separates punctuation and whitespace runs, and matches maximal operation-vocabulary synonym spans; overlaps choose the longest span then lexicographically smallest operation-id. An unmatched predicate emits custom_general_<normalized_predicate>, where normalized_predicate joins its normalized separator-delimited tokens with underscores; it is a legal custom operation. A custom operation requires target, permits only governed attributes whose categories match their values, has no result binding, and writes attributes under AOR. Explicit numeric physical counts emit one distinct acquire/transform/place sequence per unit; a prior LIST emits FOR EACH. Direct placement always expands to pick_up followed by place, with place consuming the acquired REF. rinse, heat, chill, slice, and pour preserve the input REF identity and return REF[STRING]. A requested transformed state requires the corresponding transform before place; if that transform needs a resource and none is stated, it uses the operation's canonical resource entity: rinse uses resource_sink, heat uses resource_heater, chill uses resource_chiller. Relative dates require TASK CONTEXT execution_date or are a translation error.",
