  "nl": "I need your help to create a study schedule for a month in which I can finish studying seven subjects completely.",
  "trajectory_steps": [
   "assistant: Sure! I can put together a ready‑to‑use 30‑day study plan for 7 subjects.  \nBelow you’ll find a **generic template** that you can fill in with the exact subject names, your daily‑study hours, and any upcoming test dates.  After the template I’ll walk you through a concrete example for a typical “4‑week” month so you can see how the pieces fit together.\n\n---\n\n## 1️⃣ Quick Q & A (optional – helps us fine‑tune the plan)\n\n| What I need to know | Why it matters |\n|----------------------|----------------|\n| **The 7 subject names** | Gives a clear list to rotate. |\n| **How many hours per day you can study (including breaks)** | Determines how many topics you can realistically cover each day. |\n| **Any upcoming exams, projects, or deadlines** | Allows us to front‑load harder subjects or add buffer days. |\n| **Preferred study style (e.g., Pomodoro, blocked‑time, mix‑subjects)** | Lets us tailor the schedule to the technique that works best for you. |\n| **Do you want a 5‑day‑study / 2‑day‑rest week, or a 6‑day‑study / 1‑day‑rest week?** | Adjusts the weekly rhythm. |\n\nIf you already have those details, just let me know and I’ll tweak the template below. If you’d rather start with a “plug‑and‑play” version, the rest of the answer gives you a fully‑worked example you can adapt.\n\n---\n\n## 2️⃣ Generic 30‑Day Study Blueprint (5‑day‑study / 2‑day‑rest)\n\n### 2.1 Structure Overview  \n\n| Week | Days | Theme | Main Activities |\n|------|------|-------|-----------------|\n| **Week 1** | 1‑7 | **Subjects 1 & 2** (Intro + practice) | Learn core concepts, solve problems, mini‑quiz. |\n| **Week 2** | 8‑14 | **Subjects 3 & 4** (Intro + practice) | Same as Week 1, plus a short review of subjects 1‑2. |\n| **Week 3** | 15‑21 | **Subjects 5 & 6** (Intro + practice) | Same as Week 2, plus a quick re‑cap of subjects 3‑4. |\n| **Week 4** | 22‑30 | **Subject 7 + Full‑Review** | Introduce the final subject, then rotate through all 7 for spaced‑repetition review, practice tests, and “weak‑spot” sessions. |\n\n> **Tip:** The “rest” days (usually Saturday & Sunday for a 5‑day‑study week) are reserved for light review,active‑recall flashcards, or completely off‑screen time to keep your brain fresh.\n\n### 2.2 Daily Template (≈ 3 h of focused study time)\n\n| Time Block | Activity | How to Use It |\n|------------|----------|---------------|\n| **Morning (≈ 45 min)** | **Deep‑learning** (new material) | Read notes, watch lecture, or work through a textbook chapter. |\n| **Break (10 min)** | Stretch, hydrate, short walk. |\n| **Mid‑Morning (≈ 45 min)** | **Active practice** (problems, case studies, coding exercises) | Apply concepts right away. |\n| **Break (10 min)** | Quick mindfulness or music. |\n| **Late‑Morning (≈ 30 min)** | **Flashcard / spaced‑repetition** (review of yesterday’s key points) | Use Anki, Quizlet, or a simple notebook. |\n| **Lunch (30‑60 min)** | Eat, unwind, screen‑free break. |\n| **Afternoon (≈ 45 min)** | **Deep‑learning** (next topic) | Same as morning. |\n| **Break (10 min)** | Move around. |\n| **Afternoon‑2 (≈ 45 min)** | **Practice & correction** (homework, past papers) | Mark mistakes, write “why‑I‑missed‑it” notes. |\n| **Break (10 min)** | Short walk or breathing exercise. |\n| **Evening (≈ 30 min)** | **Day‑end review** (5‑minute recap of the day’s key takeaways) | Write a bullet‑point summary; if you have a study partner, explain it aloud. |\n| **Evening‑off** | Rest, hobbies, sleep prep. |\n\n> **Pomodoro twist:** If 45‑minute blocks feel too long, split each block into two 20‑minute Pomodoros (20 min study → 5 min break).  Keep the 30‑minute “review” block at the end of the day as a single unit.\n\n### 2.3 Weekly Rhythm (example)\n\n| Day | Morning (≈ 1 h 15 min) | Mid‑day (≈ 1 h 15 min) | Afternoon (≈ 1 h 30 min) | Evening (≈ 30 min) |\n|-----|----------------------|-----------------------|--------------------------|-------------------|\n| **Mon** | Subject 1 – New concept | Subject 1 – Practice | Subject 2 – New concept | Review 1 & 2 |\n| **Tue** | Subject 2 – New concept | Subject 2 – Practice | Subject 1 – Practice | Review 1 & 2 |\n| **Wed** | Subject 1 – Review + problems | Subject 2 – Review + problems | Light quiz on both | Day‑end summary |\n| **Thu** | Subject 3 – New concept | Subject 3 – Practice | Subject 4 – New concept | Review 3 & 4 |\n| **Fri** | Subject 4 – New concept | Subject 4 – Practice | Subject 3 – Practice | Review 3 & 4 |\n| **Sat** | Light re‑cap of week 1 topics (flashcards) | **Rest / hobby** | – | – |\n| **Sun** | **Full rest** (no study) | – | – | – |\n\n*Swap subject numbers according to the week (see the 4‑week breakdown).*\n\n---\n\n## 3️⃣ Fully‑worked Example for a 30‑Day Month\n\nBelow is a **filled‑out** version assuming:\n\n- **Subjects:** Math, Physics, Chemistry, Biology, History, Geography, English (ordered by typical difficulty).\n- **Daily study time:** 3 h 30 min (split across the blocks above).  \n- **Goal:** Finish core content for each subject by Day 28; Days 29‑30 are “final polish” (mock tests, weak‑spot drills).\n\n| Day | Focus | Morning (≈ 1 h 15 min) | Mid‑day (≈ 1 h 15 min) | Afternoon (≈ 1 h 30 min) | Evening (≈ 30 min) |\n|-----|-------|------------------------|-----------------------|--------------------------|-------------------|\n| **1** | Math | New: Algebra basics | Practice: Solve equations | New: Geometry intro | Review Math key points |\n| **2** | Math | New: Functions & graphs | Practice: Graph problems | New: Trigonometry intro | Review Math key points |\n| **3** | Math | Review + mixed problems | Mock quiz (Math) | Light review of earlier Math topics | Day‑end summary |\n| **4** | Physics | New: Kinematics | Practice: Motion problems | New: Forces intro | Review Physics concepts |\n| **5** | Physics | New: Newton’s Laws | Practice: Force problems | Review + problem set | Review Physics |\n| **6** | Physics | Review day (flashcards) | **Rest** (no study) | – | – |\n| **7** | **Full rest** | – | – | – | – |\n| **8** | Chemistry | New: Atomic structure | Practice: Electron config. | New: Periodic table | Review Chemistry |\n| **9** | Chemistry | New: Chemical bonding | Practice: Bonding problems | Review + lab concepts | Review Chemistry |\n| **10** | Chemistry | Mixed practice (past paper Q) | Review of all Chem topics | Light quiz (Chemistry) | Day‑end summary |\n| **11** | Biology | New: Cell structure | Practice: Cell diagrams | New: Genetics intro | Review Biology |\n| **12** | Biology | New: DNA replication | Practice: Genetics problems | Review + case studies | Review Biology |\n| **13** | Biology | Review (flashcards) | **Rest** | – | – |\n| **14** | **Full rest** | – | – | – | – |\n| **15** | History | New: Ancient Civilizations | Practice: Timeline & key events | New: Medieval period | Review History |\n| **16** | History | New: Renaissance & Reformation | Practice: Source analysis | Review + essay outline | Review History |\n| **17** | History | Review (map work) | Mixed review of History & Geography | Light quiz (History) | Day‑end summary |\n| **18** | Geography | New: Plate tectonics | Practice: Map reading | New: Climate zones | Review Geography |\n| **19** | Geography | New: Human‑environment interaction | Practice: Case studies | Review + data interpretation | Review Geography |\n| **20** | Geography | Review (flashcards) | **Rest** | – | – |\n| **21** | **Full rest** | – | – | – | – |\n| **22** | English | New: Essay structure | Practice: Writing a paragraph | New: Grammar & style | Review English |\n| **23** | English | New: Literary analysis | Practice: Close reading | Review + peer review (if possible) | Review English |\n| **24** | English | Review (vocabulary flashcards) | Mixed practice (past exam Q) | Light quiz (English) | Day‑end summary |\n| **25** | **Spaced‑review** (All subjects) | Math flashcard session | Physics flashcard session | Chemistry flashcard session | Quick summary of the day |\n| **26** | **Spaced‑review** (All subjects) | Biology flashcard session | History flashcard session | Geography flashcard session | Day‑end summary |\n| **27** | **Mock Tests** | Full Math test (45 min) | Full Physics test (45 min) | Full Chemistry test (45 min) | Review mistakes |\n| **28** | **Mock Tests** | Full Biology test (45 min) | Full History test (45 min) | Full Geography test (45 min) | Review mistakes |\n| **29** | **Weak‑spot Drill** | Target Math & Physics weak areas | Target Chemistry & Biology weak areas | Target History & Geography weak areas | Day‑end summary |\n| **30** | **Final Polish** | Light English review (vocab) | Quick review of all subject “cheat‑sheets” | Relaxation & mental preparation | Reflection on the month |\n\n> **Key points to remember**  \n> 1. **Active recall & spaced repetition** are built into the daily flashcard blocks and the “spaced‑review” days (25‑26).  \n> 2. **Rest days are sacred** – they help consolidate memory and prevent burnout.  \n> 3. **Adapt the hours** to your own capacity. If 3 h 30 min is too much, cut the afternoon block and keep the evening review short (15 min).  \n> 4. **Track progress** with a simple checklist (e.g., a column in a spreadsheet or a bullet‑journal page). Tick off each sub‑topic after you’ve completed it.  \n> 5. **Stay flexible**: If a subject feels “easy” you can swap a practice day for a deeper‑learning day, or shift a rest day earlier if you’re exhausted.\n\n---\n\n## 4️⃣ How to Use This Template\n\n1. **Copy the “Daily Template”** into a spreadsheet (Google Sheets, Excel, or even a notebook).  \n2. **Label the columns** with the days of the month (1‑30).  \n3. **Fill the “Focus” row** with the subject you’ll tackle that day (use the example above or your own ordering).  \n4. **Populate the time blocks** with the specific topics (e.g., “Algebra – solving linear equations”).  \n5. **Add a “Done?” column** to check off each block once completed.  \n6. **Weekends** – you can keep them light (just a 10‑minute flashcard session) or treat them as full rest days.\n\n---\n\n## 5️⃣ Ready to Customize?\n\nIf you let me know:\n\n- **Your 7 subject names** (or any other specific topics you need to cover)  \n- **How many hours per day you can realistically study**  \n- **Any upcoming exams or deadlines**  \n\nI can generate a **fully customized calendar** (Google Calendar‑style or a printable PDF) with exact times, topics, and reminder notes.\n\nJust drop those details here, and I’ll turn this generic blueprint into your personal study‑plan in seconds! 🎯"
