  "nl": "I need help by managing my money because I always run out before the end of the month",
  "trajectory_steps": [
   "assistant: # Getting Control of Your Money 💰\n\nIt's great that you want to make a change! Running out of money before month-end is a really common problem. Here's a step-by-step approach:\n\n## 1. **Track Where Your Money Goes**\n- For one month, write down EVERY purchase\n- Use an app (like Mint, YNAB, or even just a spreadsheet)\n- You'll likely find surprising spending patterns\n\n## 2. **Build a Simple Budget**\nTry the **50/30/20 rule** as a starting point:\n- **50%** → Needs (rent, utilities, groceries, transport)\n- **30%** → Wants (eating out, entertainment, shopping)\n- **20%** → Savings & debt repayment\n\n## 3. **Common Money Leaks to Watch**\n- Subscriptions you forgot about\n- Daily coffee/takeout habits\n- Impulse online shopping\n- \"Small\" purchases that add up\n\n## 4. **Practical Tips**\n- **Pay bills first** right when you get paid\n- **Use cash envelopes** for spending categories — when it's gone, it's gone\n- **Wait 24-48 hours** before non-essential purchases\n- **Meal plan** to cut food costs\n- **Automate savings** even if it's just a small amount\n\n## 5. **If Money Is Truly Tight**\n- Look into ways to reduce fixed costs (cheaper phone plan, etc.)\n- Check if you qualify for any assistance programs\n- Consider a side income source\n\n---\n\nWould you like help with any specific area, like creating a budget, cutting expenses, or dealing with debt?",
   "  [feedback: explicit_affirmation] Very clear",
   "user: Mostly it food, transport, and small things like snacks",
   "assistant: # Tackling Your Top 3 Money Drains\n\nThese are the exact categories that quietly drain budgets! Let's break each one down:\n\n---\n\n## 🍳 Food\nThis is usually the **biggest area for savings**:\n\n- **Meal prep on weekends** — cook in bulk for the week\n- **Make a grocery list** and stick to it strictly\n- **Buy store brands** instead of name brands\n- **Plan meals around what's on sale**\n- **Pack lunch for work/school** — eating out daily adds up FAST\n- **Cook simple meals** — rice, beans, pasta, eggs, and frozen vegetables are cheap and filling\n\n**Example:** Buying lunch daily at $8 = **$160/month**. Packing lunch at ~$2 = **$40/month**. That's **$120 saved!**\n\n---\n\n## 🚌 Transport\n- **Can you walk or bike** for short trips?\n- **Use public transit** instead of rideshares when possible\n- **Carpool** with coworkers or friends\n- **Batch your errands** into one trip instead of multiple\n- If you drive, compare gas prices and keep tires properly inflated\n\n---\n\n## 🍫 Small Snacks & Impulse Buys\nThese are sneaky budget killers:\n\n- **Buy snacks in bulk** from the grocery store instead of convenience stores\n- **Carry snacks from home** so you're not tempted\n- **Set a weekly cash limit** for extras (like $10-15)\n- **Leave your card at home** when you don't need it\n- Ask yourself: *\"Do I need this or just want it right now?\"*\n\n---\n\n## Quick Action Plan\n1. **This week:** Track every small purchase\n2. **This weekend:** Meal prep and buy bulk snacks\n3. **Set a weekly \"fun money\" limit** in cash\n\nWould you like help creating a specific weekly meal plan on a budget?",
   "user: How much should I save each month?",
   "assistant: # How Much Should You Save? 💰\n\nThe honest answer is: **it depends on your situation**, but here are some guidelines:\n\n---\n\n## If You're Just Starting Out\n- **Start small!** Even saving **$20-50/month** is a win\n- The habit matters more than the amount at first\n- Something is always better than nothing\n\n## General Guidelines\n\n| Situation | Suggested Savings |\n|---|---|\n| Tight budget | 5-10% of income |\n| Comfortable | 15-20% of income |\n| Aggressive goals | 25%+ of income |\n\n---\n\n## Priority Order for Your Savings\n\n### 1. 🚨 Emergency Fund (First Priority)\n- Start with a goal of **$500-$1,000**\n- Eventually aim for **3-6 months** of expenses\n- This prevents debt when unexpected things happen (car repair, medical bill, etc.)\n\n### 2. 💳 Pay Off High-Interest Debt\n- Credit cards and loans eat your money\n- Paying these off IS a form of saving\n\n### 3. 🎯 Future Goals\n- Larger purchases you're planning\n- Retirement\n- Education\n\n---\n\n## A Realistic Approach For You\nSince you're running out of money each month:\n\n1. **Start with just 5%** of your income\n2. **Save it immediately** when you get paid\n3. **Increase by 1%** every few months\n4. **Treat savings like a bill** you MUST pay\n\n> *\"Pay yourself first\"* — put savings aside BEFORE spending on anything else\n\n---\n\nWould you like help calculating a specific savings amount based on your income?",
   "user: So what if I don't have money to save?",
   "assistant: # When There's Nothing Left to Save\n\nThis is a really honest and common situation — don't feel bad about it. Here's how to approach it:\n\n---\n\n## First: Find Hidden Money\nYou probably have **small leaks** you can redirect:\n\n- That daily snack habit? Even cutting **half** of it frees up money\n- One less rideshare per week could save $10-20\n- Unused subscriptions (Netflix, Spotify, gym)?\n- Buying water bottles instead of carrying one from home?\n\n> Even **$5/week = $20/month**. That's a start!\n\n---\n\n## Second: Look at the Big Picture\n\n### Either you need to:\n1. **Spend less** ← we've been working on this\n2. **Earn more** ← this might be your real answer\n3. **Or both**\n\n### Ways to Earn More:\n- Sell things you don't use anymore\n- Freelance or side gigs on weekends\n- Ask for overtime at work\n- Look for a better paying job\n- Offer skills like tutoring, cleaning, or errands\n\n---\n\n## Third: Redefine \"Saving\"\n\nSaving doesn't have to mean big amounts:\n\n- **Save coins** in a jar\n- **Round up** purchases and save the difference\n- **Save any unexpected money** (gifts, refunds, bonuses)\n- Use apps that auto-save tiny amounts\n\n---\n\n## Most Important Mindset Shift\n\n> Before thinking about saving, focus on **not going into debt** and **making it through the month**\n\nYour priorities should be:\n1. ✅ Cover basic needs\n2. ✅ Stop the money leaks\n3. ✅ Build income\n4. ✅ Then start saving even tiny amounts\n\n---\n\n**You're already on the right track** by asking these questions. Would you like help looking at your income vs expenses to find where money could be freed up?",
   "  [feedback: explicit_affirmation] Yes that's a good idea",
   "user: Thank you this will help me manage my money better",
   "assistant: # You're Welcome! 😊\n\nI'm glad this was helpful! Here's a quick recap of your action plan:\n\n---\n\n## Your Next Steps\n1. ✅ **Track your spending** this week\n2. ✅ **Meal prep and pack food** from home\n3. ✅ **Buy snacks in bulk** instead of daily\n4. ✅ **Cut transport costs** where possible\n5. ✅ **Set a small weekly cash limit** for extras\n6. ✅ **Save even tiny amounts** when you can\n\n---\n\n## Remember\n- **Small changes add up** to big results over time\n- **Be patient** with yourself — building new habits takes time\n- **Progress not perfection** — one bad week doesn't erase your effort\n- It's okay to start small\n\n---\n\n> 💡 **Pro tip:** Check in with your budget at the end of each week to see how you're doing. Adjust as needed!\n\nFeel free to come back anytime if you need help with:\n- Building a budget\n- Meal planning on a budget\n- Finding ways to earn extra income\n- Or anything else\n\n**You've got this! 💪**",
   "user: Any meal planning"
  ]
