Shaper proposal: {"summary": "This step adds a normative Canonical Form rulebook and applies it to request scope, declaration names, handle names, attribute order, operation selection, embodied decomposition, and transcript structure. It also repairs affected constructs and examples so the specification gives one consistent encoding path.", "note_position": "comply", "note_argument": "The changes replace translator discretion with explicit canonical rules while retaining one unambiguous parse. Unmatched operations remain legal through the existing custom operation category, but now have a deterministic readable token derivation and a fixed signature policy.", "foundations_overview": "BrainCode is a statically typed context-free request language with a normative Canonical Form translation layer. Grammar determines a unique parse; Canonical Form determines one emitted document shape for a supplied request by fixing request scope, declaration selection and naming, vocabulary-symbol selection, attribute order, result handles, dependency order, transcript links, and required embodied-action expansion. Vocabulary members supply canonical meanings for synonyms, while transitional STRING channels remain available during the N2/N3 build phase.", "rebuttals": [], "changes": [{"op": "add", "construct_name": "canonical-form", "grammar": "-- No independent production. Normative translation rules used by TASK, CONVO, ACTION, GENERATE, UTTER, Bind, and Flow-If.", "semantics": "Canonical Form applies to the complete supplied input. A supplied dataset transcript is its ordered, explicitly delimited messages; a plain single text is one message and is never split at punctuation. One supplied message requiring ACTION or GENERATE emits a TASK; one supplied message containing only speech acts emits a CONVO; two or more supplied messages always emit one CONVO with one TURN per message in supplied order. Thus a final artifact request following earlier corrections remains a USER UTTER in its final CONVO turn; it does not discard earlier turns or emit a standalone TASK. TURN names are t1, t2, ... . A message explicitly replacing an earlier request uses REVISES that earlier turn's current lineage tip; every other later message uses REPLY_TO the immediately preceding turn. Declaration and ENTRYPOINT names are PascalCase of the first executable step base: a bound Action or Generate uses its result handle with terminal _ref and numeric suffix removed; an unbound Action uses operation-id; an unbound UTTER uses speech-act; an unbound GENERATE uses target. AOR writes target first when present, then all remaining attributes in ASCII alphabetical order. HNR: an Action result base is target when target is a literal vocabulary symbol, otherwise operation-id; a Generate base is target; a task-call base is callee; a bound UTTER base is speech-act; LET base is its declared IDENT. A literal vocabulary symbol is an unquoted member token written directly in a value position, not an IDENT reference previously bound by LET or ->. For an Action returning REF[T], append _ref to the base; all other results use the base unchanged. Append _2, _3, ... for repeated bases in source order. SOR puts each producer before a consumer; otherwise preserves supplied-message order and then source-span order; exact ties use operation-id order. OSR NFC-normalizes and Unicode-casefolds the source, separates punctuation and whitespace runs, and matches maximal operation-vocabulary synonym spans; overlaps choose the longest span then lexicographically smallest operation-id. An unmatched predicate emits custom_general_<normalized_predicate>, where normalized_predicate joins its normalized separator-delimited tokens with underscores; it is a legal custom operation. A custom operation requires target, permits only governed attributes whose categories match their values, has no result binding, and writes attributes under AOR. Explicit numeric physical counts emit one distinct acquire/transform/place sequence per unit; a prior LIST emits FOR EACH. Direct placement always expands to pick_up followed by place, with place consuming the acquired REF. rinse, heat, chill, slice, and pour preserve the input REF identity and return REF[STRING]. A requested transformed state requires the corresponding transform before place; if that transform needs a resource and none is stated, it uses the operation's canonical resource entity: rinse uses resource_sink, heat uses resource_heater, chill uses resource_chiller. Relative dates require TASK CONTEXT execution_date or are a translation error.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Place a washed tomato on a table.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT Tomato\n\nTASK Tomato {\n  ACTION pick_up(target=tomato) -> tomato_ref : REF[STRING]\n  ACTION rinse(destination=resource_sink, target=tomato_ref) -> rinse : REF[STRING]\n  ACTION place(destination=table, relation=on, target=rinse)\n}"}, "glossary_gloss": "Canonical Form is the fixed translation rulebook for BrainCode. It fixes document shape, names, ordering, synonym mapping, and required action decomposition so equivalent requests encode the same way.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "N7 needs a single normative location for choices that grammar alone cannot make. The custom-operation rule is now explicitly compatible with operation-vocabulary and remains readable, while resource and identity rules remove ambiguity in embodied plans.", "cites_source": "Formal language theory", "addresses_note": "N7"}, {"op": "revise", "kind": "vocabulary", "construct_name": "operation-vocabulary", "glossary_gloss": "Operation-vocabulary contains canonical Action and UTTER operation symbols. Its synonyms map source wording to one listed operation, while an unmatched operation uses the defined readable custom form.", "closed": false, "members": [{"symbol": "modify_code", "gloss": "Change source code.", "nl_synonyms": ["fix", "reduce", "optimize"]}, {"symbol": "search_web", "gloss": "Search the web.", "nl_synonyms": ["find", "look for"]}, {"symbol": "pick_up", "gloss": "Acquire an entity.", "nl_synonyms": ["grab", "take"]}, {"symbol": "place", "gloss": "Put an acquired entity at a destination.", "nl_synonyms": ["put"]}, {"symbol": "ask", "gloss": "Ask for information, advice, or a response.", "nl_synonyms": ["ask", "how should"]}], "membership_rule": "Listed members are canonical. A missing operation is exactly custom_general_<normalized_predicate>, where normalized_predicate is the NFC-normalized, Unicode-casefolded source predicate split at whitespace or punctuation and joined with underscores; it must match [a-z_]+. A custom operation requires target, may use only otherwise governed attributes, may not bind a result, and is not a synonym for any listed member. New listed operations or synonyms are added through the glossary rather than minted ad hoc.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Reduce migration operations during optimization.", "braincode": "ACTION modify_code(file=path_django_db_migrations_operations_models_py, revision=chg_reduce_add_remove_index_optimization, target=django)"}, "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Makes the custom fallback legal, deterministic, and statically constrained, and adds the common code-change synonym exposed by the simulated item.", "addresses_note": "N7"}, {"op": "revise", "kind": "vocabulary", "construct_name": "entity-name", "glossary_gloss": "Entity-name names selectable objects, places, services, and canonical abstract resources used by Actions. Resource symbols make implicit household equipment explicit and consistent.", "closed": false, "members": [{"symbol": "mug", "gloss": "Drinking cup.", "nl_synonyms": ["mug"]}, {"symbol": "sink", "gloss": "Washing basin.", "nl_synonyms": ["sink"]}, {"symbol": "resource_sink", "gloss": "Canonical abstract washing resource when no particular sink is named.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "resource_heater", "gloss": "Canonical abstract heating resource when no particular appliance is named.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "resource_chiller", "gloss": "Canonical abstract chilling resource when no particular appliance is named.", "nl_synonyms": []}], "membership_rule": "An entity-name is an atomic [a-z_]+ symbol denoting a selectable entity kind or canonical action resource. resource_sink, resource_heater, and resource_chiller are reserved canonical resources and may only be emitted by Canonical Form when the corresponding requested transform lacks a named resource.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Put a warm glass on the shelf.", "braincode": "ACTION pick_up(target=glass) -> glass_ref : REF[STRING]\nACTION heat(destination=resource_heater, target=glass_ref) -> heat : REF[STRING]\nACTION place(destination=shelf, relation=on, target=heat)"}, "change_type": "MINOR", "rationale": "Provides glossary-defined, deterministic resources instead of requiring encoders to guess a microwave, sink, or refrigerator.", "addresses_note": "N7"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Task", "grammar": "document ::= entrypoint (task | conversation)*\nentrypoint ::= \"ENTRYPOINT\" IDENT\ntask ::= \"TASK\" IDENT param_list? (\"CONTEXT\" execution_date_param)? (\":\" type)? (\"DECREASES\" IDENT)? \"{\" step+ return_stmt? \"}\"\nexecution_date_param ::= \"execution_date\" \"=\" ISO_DATE\nparam_list ::= \"(\" param (\",\" param)* \")\"\nparam ::= IDENT \":\" type\nreturn_stmt ::= \"RETURN\" value\nstep ::= action | flow_if | flow_for | bind | task_call | utterance | generation", "semantics": "TASK is Canonical Form's encoding for one supplied message that requires executable ACTION or GENERATE work. Its ENTRYPOINT and declaration name follow Canonical Form's first-step-base rule; its attributes, result handles, decomposition, and step order follow AOR, HNR, and SOR. CONTEXT is required when translating relative dates and remains a translation-time anchor only. Existing parameter, result, recursion, and lexical-scoping rules remain unchanged.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Plan a 3-day Kyoto itinerary with one temple per day and no walk over 20 minutes.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT ArtItinerary\n\nTASK ArtItinerary : STRING {\n  GENERATE(target=art_itinerary, duration=3, duration_unit=unit_day, max_walk_duration=20, max_walk_duration_unit=unit_minute, stop_min_per_day=1, stop_requirement=class_temple, topic=topic_kyoto_trip) -> art_itinerary_ref : STRING\n  RETURN art_itinerary_ref\n}"}, "glossary_gloss": "Task holds one executable request. Canonical Form fixes its name, step order, attributes, and result names.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Connects TASK selection and all task-level naming decisions to the canonical rulebook.", "cites_source": "HTML", "addresses_note": "N7"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Action", "grammar": "action ::= \"ACTION\" operation-id \"(\" action-attrs? \")\" (\"->\" IDENT \":\" type)?\naction-attrs ::= action-attr (\",\" action-attr)*\naction-attr ::= attribute-name \"=\" value\noperation-id ::= operation-vocabulary member", "semantics": "Action executes one external operation. Attributes use Canonical Form AOR and result names use HNR. place requires a REF target. pick_up returns REF[STRING]; rinse, heat, chill, slice, and pour each require a REF target, preserve that same entity identity, and return REF[STRING]. For these transforms, destination is the named resource or Canonical Form's relevant resource entity. A direct placement request expands to pick_up and place. Other operation signatures remain governed by their listed attributes.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Put both pillows from the sofa onto the armchair, one at a time.", "braincode": "ACTION pick_up(source=sofa, target=pillow) -> pillow_ref : REF[STRING]\nACTION place(destination=armchair, target=pillow_ref)\nACTION pick_up(source=sofa, target=pillow) -> pillow_ref_2 : REF[STRING]\nACTION place(destination=armchair, target=pillow_ref_2)"}, "glossary_gloss": "Action performs one external operation with governed attributes. Its attribute order, result name, and embodied object identity follow Canonical Form.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Defines deterministic transform-resource behavior and REF identity preservation, while making all Action examples canonical.", "cites_source": "HTML", "addresses_note": "N7"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Bind", "grammar": "bind ::= \"LET\" IDENT \":\" type \"=\" value\n-- Additional Bind-site forms: action_result ::= \"->\" IDENT \":\" type ; task_call_result ::= \"->\" IDENT ; utterance_result ::= \"->\" IDENT ; generation_result ::= \"->\" IDENT \":\" type", "semantics": "Bind gives names to LET values and Action, task-call, UTTER, and GENERATE results. LET requires an exact static type match, and a REF may only copy a visible REF of the same type. Derived Action, Generate, task-call, and UTTER names must follow Canonical Form HNR; _ref marks an Action result whose declared type is REF[T]. Scope, no-shadowing, and source-order visibility remain unchanged.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Rinse one mug in the sink, then place that same mug in the coffee maker.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT Mug\n\nTASK Mug {\n  ACTION pick_up(target=mug) -> mug_ref : REF[STRING]\n  ACTION rinse(destination=sink, target=mug_ref) -> rinse : REF[STRING]\n  ACTION place(destination=coffee_maker, target=rinse)\n}"}, "glossary_gloss": "Bind gives a value a name for later steps in its allowed scope. Canonical Form makes every derived result name predictable.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Completes handle naming for every bind site and removes the former ambiguity between vocabulary values and references.", "cites_source": "Formal language theory", "addresses_note": "N7"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Generate", "grammar": "generation ::= \"GENERATE\" \"(\" generate-attr (\",\" generate-attr)* \")\" \"->\" IDENT \":\" gen_type\ngenerate-attr ::= attribute-name \"=\" value\ngen_type ::= \"NUMBER\" | \"STRING\" | \"BOOL\" | \"LIST\" \"[\" gen_type \"]\"", "semantics": "Generate creates a non-effectful artifact. Legal attributes are target, audience, character_properties, constraints, content, duration, duration_unit, events, format, locale, max_walk_duration, max_walk_duration_unit, quantity, quantity_unit, stop_min_per_day, stop_requirement, style, tone, topic, and transformations. target is an artifact-value symbol; attributes follow AOR and the result handle follows HNR. content remains a transitional STRING channel during the build phase.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Create a newsletter about a decision study.", "braincode": "GENERATE(target=art_short_text, format=format_newsletter, topic=topic_decision_study) -> art_short_text_ref : STRING"}, "glossary_gloss": "Generate creates a requested artifact using governed topic, requirement, and format attributes. Its attributes and result name have one canonical order and spelling.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Makes Generate consistent with the canonical attribute and handle rules.", "cites_source": "HTML", "addresses_note": "N7"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Utterance", "grammar": "utterance ::= \"UTTER\" speech-act \"(\" utter-attr (\",\" utter-attr)* \")\" (\"->\" IDENT)?\nspeech-act ::= operation-vocabulary member\nutter-attr ::= attribute-name \"=\" value", "semantics": "UTTER records a speech act inside a CONVO TURN. Legal attributes are audience, constraints, content, recipient, target, tone, and topic; constraints requires LIST[constraint-value], tone requires tone-value, topic requires topic-value, and recipient/audience require recipient-value. Attributes follow AOR. content remains transitional STRING. A bound UTTER result is STRING and follows HNR; an unbound UTTER supplies its speech-act as the declaration-name base.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Ask politely for realistic pickup lines.", "braincode": "CONVO Ask {\n  TURN t1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER ask(constraints=[constraint_realistic], tone=tone_polite, topic=topic_pickup_lines)\n  }\n}"}, "glossary_gloss": "Utterance records what a conversation speaker asks, states, or acknowledges. An unbound utterance has a canonical declaration-name base: its speech act.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Makes common utterance-only conversations canonically nameable and aligns legal symbolic request attributes with existing vocabulary.", "cites_source": "Why Agentic Systems Need Ontologies", "addresses_note": "N7"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Conversation", "grammar": "conversation ::= \"CONVO\" IDENT \"{\" turn+ \"}\"\nturn ::= \"TURN\" IDENT \"SPEAKER\" \"=\" speaker_lit (\"REPLY_TO\" IDENT )? (\"REVISES\" IDENT )? \"{\" step+ \"}\"\nspeaker_lit ::= \"USER\" | \"AGENT\"", "semantics": "CONVO is Canonical Form's encoding for a speech-only single message or any supplied multi-message transcript. It has exactly one TURN for each supplied message, in order, named t1, t2, ... . Its declaration and ENTRYPOINT names use Canonical Form's first-step-base rule, including an unbound UTTER's speech-act. An explicit replacement uses REVISES the prior lineage tip; all other later turns REPLY_TO the preceding turn. Existing whole-body replacement, linear revision-chain, and per-turn scope rules remain unchanged.", "worked_example": {"nl": "What is your view on friendship? How do I become a good friend?", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT Ask\n\nCONVO Ask {\n  TURN t1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER ask(topic=topic_friendship)\n  }\n}"}, "glossary_gloss": "Conversation records supplied conversational messages in fixed order. Canonical Form fixes its declaration name, turn names, and reply-versus-revision links.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Resolves transcript scope and declaration naming without collapsing a supplied conversational trajectory into a final standalone task.", "cites_source": "Why Agentic Systems Need Ontologies", "addresses_note": "N7"}], "_provider_used": "openai", "_model_used": "gpt-5.6-terra"}
Critic assessment: {"decision": "needs-rework", "kpi_assessment": {"coverage": {"impact": "mixed", "reasoning": "Canonical embodied decomposition and abstract resource entities improve coverage for household requests such as washed or heated objects. However, the custom-operation rule does not provide a usable deterministic encoding for requests whose required attributes have no governed category (for example RAM capacity and cuisine), and several sampled web tasks remain inexpressible."}, "expressivity": {"impact": "mixed", "reasoning": "The changes preserve object identity through pick_up/transform/place and retain transcript turns, which improves preservation of embodied and conversational structure. But numeric quantity is expanded into repeated indistinguishable operations without an explicit count or selection identity, and unsupported request criteria cannot be represented; single-message multi-question requests also have no rule requiring their distinct speech acts to be retained."}, "determinism": {"impact": "mixed", "reasoning": "AOR, HNR, turn names, declaration names, resource defaults, and dependency ordering are substantive progress toward N7. Yet OSR leaves predicate-span selection underspecified, its fallback can generate illegal tokens after Unicode normalization or digit-bearing predicates, and synonym/category selection remains non-canonical for many vocabulary categories; capable encoders would still diverge on unsupported criteria, operation boundaries, and whether a state denotes selection or a required transformation."}, "interpretability": {"impact": "mixed", "reasoning": "Named canonical resources and predictable result handles improve readability. Conversely, custom_general_<normalized_predicate> is only a lossy source-string transformation rather than a defined operation meaning, and repeated identical physical sequences do not visibly identify which distinct requested unit they refer to."}, "improvement": {"impact": "benefit", "reasoning": "Explicit pick_up/transform/place expansion, REF preservation, dependency ordering, and retained correction chains provide operational structure a weaker planner can use. The benefit is limited for web and software tasks lacking required attribute categories or operation signatures."}}, "simulations": [{"item_id": "dev-web-2", "source": "seed_tasks", "nl": "On the electronics store, filter laptops to 16 GB RAM or more under $1,200, and add the best-rated one to the cart.", "braincode": "FAIL: no governed attribute/category represents minimum RAM capacity; custom operations may only use otherwise governed attributes.", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "Canonical Form cannot encode the actionable RAM>=16 criterion. price and rating can be represented, but the closed-task core cannot."}, {"item_id": "held-trip-1", "source": "seed_tasks", "nl": "Plan a 3-day itinerary in Kyoto that includes at least one temple per day and avoids anything more than a 20-minute walk from the last stop.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT ArtItinerary\n\nTASK ArtItinerary : STRING {\n  GENERATE(target=art_itinerary, duration=3, duration_unit=unit_day, max_walk_duration=20, max_walk_duration_unit=unit_minute, stop_min_per_day=1, stop_requirement=class_temple, topic=topic_kyoto_trip) -> art_itinerary_ref : STRING\n  RETURN art_itinerary_ref\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "Task, AOR, HNR, and declaration naming apply coherently. Assuming topic_kyoto_trip is available under N6."}, {"item_id": "4bc70fa1-e817-405f-b113-0919e8e94205", "source": "Mind2Web", "nl": "Add the cheapest Women's Sweaters to my shopping cart.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT SearchWeb\n\nTASK SearchWeb {\n  ACTION search_web(category=cat_sweater, gender=gender_women, target=sweater) -> sweater_ref : LIST[REF[STRING]]\n  ACTION sort(rank_direction=dir_asc, rank_field=rank_price, target=sweater_ref) -> sort : LIST[REF[STRING]]\n  ACTION extract(limit=1, target=sort) -> extract : LIST[REF[STRING]]\n  ACTION add_to_cart(target=extract)\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "The canonical operation sequence is plausible, but HNR's base for list-returning search is target=sweater, yielding sweater_ref; another encoder may choose plural inventory semantics absent from the rules."}, {"item_id": "7ad37a91-c4c2-42d6-9b87-1f3e6a81647e", "source": "Mind2Web", "nl": "check reservation availability for an Indian restaurant with price below $30 in East village", "braincode": "FAIL: no governed cuisine criterion/category or reservation-availability operation signature is defined.", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "location, currency, and max_price are insufficient to preserve Indian cuisine and reservation availability."}, {"item_id": "trial_T20190906_224234_994763#2", "source": "ALFRED", "nl": "Put a spoon in the sink.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT Spoon\n\nTASK Spoon {\n  ACTION pick_up(target=spoon) -> spoon_ref : REF[STRING]\n  ACTION place(destination=sink, relation=in, target=spoon_ref)\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "Canonical direct-placement expansion and REF use are clear."}, {"item_id": "trial_T20190909_065023_650475#2", "source": "ALFRED", "nl": "Retrieve plate from cupboard, insert it into microwave, take the plate to the fridge", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT Plate\n\nTASK Plate {\n  ACTION pick_up(source=cupboard, target=plate) -> plate_ref : REF[STRING]\n  ACTION place(destination=microwave, relation=in, target=plate_ref)\n  ACTION pick_up(source=microwave, target=plate) -> plate_ref_2 : REF[STRING]\n  ACTION place(destination=refrigerator, relation=in, target=plate_ref_2)\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "The request does not explicitly require heating, so no heat expansion is required. The second pick_up reselects plate rather than consuming the first REF, which is a semantic ambiguity not resolved by Canonical Form."}, {"item_id": "django__django-15957", "source": "SWE-bench_Verified", "nl": "Prefetch objects don't work with slices.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT Django\n\nTASK Django {\n  ACTION modify_code(file=path_django_db_models_fields_related_descriptors_py, revision=chg_fix_prefetch_sliced_queryset, target=django)\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "Assumes code-value members under N6. The proposed modify_code synonym mapping helps only if the imperative is treated as fix rather than its broader reported-bug wording."}, {"item_id": "django__django-16502", "source": "SWE-bench_Verified", "nl": "After #26052 runserver returns response body for HTTP HEAD requests.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT Django\n\nTASK Django {\n  ACTION modify_code(actual_output=outcome_head_response_includes_body, expected_output=outcome_head_response_excludes_body, file=path_django_core_servers_basehttp_py, revision=chg_strip_head_response_body, target=django)\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "Assumes the code-value symbols exist under N6. A full bug report's reproduction details are not captured because no deterministic rule says when each report field must be emitted."}, {"item_id": "c1957", "source": "PRISM", "nl": "I think something important we should discuss is that it is important not to be negative. Negativity is a choice, every time. It is not related to depression. People who have the option to be negative should not be like that! I don't want to hear about bias, I am simply saying it is important to stay positive or you become negative. It's very important.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT Inform\n\nCONVO Inform {\n  TURN t1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER inform(content=content_positivity_choice_not_depression_reject_bias)\n  }\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "A content-value category can hold an atomic proposition under N6, but the proposal supplies no canonical decomposition of the several claims, prohibition, and desired discussion response. Under N4, opaque prose is unavailable."}, {"item_id": "c5204", "source": "PRISM", "nl": "People who live in the same country but have different religious beliefs", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT Inform\n\nCONVO Inform {\n  TURN t1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER inform(topic=topic_religious_diversity_same_country)\n  }\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "A single conversational proposition is representable assuming the topic member under N6."}, {"item_id": "c714", "source": "PRISM", "nl": "Is Switzerland a \"Parasite\" Country ? that does not provide any real value to the world and his wealth comes from other people fortunes ?", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT Ask\n\nCONVO Ask {\n  TURN t1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER ask(topic=topic_switzerland_economic_value, constraints=[constraint_assess_parasitic_country_claim])\n  }\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "Meta-structure and the evaluative question are symbolically representable using assumed vocabulary members."}, {"item_id": "c1044", "source": "PRISM", "nl": "Do you think its important for people to have healthy relationships in life ?", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT Ask\n\nCONVO Ask {\n  TURN t1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER ask(topic=topic_importance_healthy_relationships)\n  }\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "A single speech-act question is representable under the assumed topic vocabulary."}, {"item_id": "wildchat1m_en3u-45091", "source": "PATHs", "nl": "How would someone discibe the surface texture of the catwalk greatings on Oil Rigs? Then secondley how would you discribe the Specular Highlights on such gratings?", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT Ask\n\nCONVO Ask {\n  TURN t1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER ask(topic=topic_oil_rig_catwalk_grating_surface_texture_and_specular_highlights)\n  }\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "One topic atom can name the combined subject, but the requested two-part explanatory structure is not represented as distinct requests or output sections."}, {"item_id": "wildchat1m_en3u-136118", "source": "PATHs", "nl": "answer with 80 to 110 words as you are in a speaking IELTS test. Don't reveal that you are an A.I. In your country (Iran), how much time do people spend on work and how much time on leisure? Is this a good balance, do you think?", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT Ask\n\nCONVO Ask {\n  TURN t1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER ask(constraints=[constraint_word_count_80_110, constraint_ielts_speaking_register, constraint_do_not_claim_ai_identity], topic=topic_iran_work_leisure_balance)\n  }\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "The language has no minimum/maximum word-count attributes, no speaking-test register value, and no reliable representation of the requested human persona; assumed atomic constraints conceal rather than structurally encode these distinctions."}, {"item_id": "wildchat1m_en3u-105894", "source": "PATHs", "nl": "Make a report about Peterhof", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT ArtStructuredReport\n\nTASK ArtStructuredReport : STRING {\n  GENERATE(target=art_structured_report, topic=topic_peterhof) -> art_structured_report_ref : STRING\n  RETURN art_structured_report_ref\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "Generate selection, AOR, HNR, and task naming are deterministic for this simple artifact request."}, {"item_id": "wildchat1m_en3u-135133", "source": "PATHs", "nl": "overview of the football industry in egypt. include credible apa 7 reference", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT ArtTechnicalReport\n\nTASK ArtTechnicalReport : STRING {\n  GENERATE(constraints=[constraint_credible_apa_7_references], format=format_structured_report, target=art_technical_report, topic=topic_egypt_football_industry) -> art_technical_report_ref : STRING\n  RETURN art_technical_report_ref\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "The required reference count and APA-7 citation structure have no first-class attributes. An assumed atomic constraint is not a rich deterministic encoding of the citation requirement."}, {"item_id": "1775448628523", "source": "ThoughtTrace", "nl": "I will be in Paris for two days for a conference, and I'll be free on day 2. I want to go to a museum and see some nice art. I don't want anything too crowded. What do you suggest?", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT Ask\n\nCONVO Ask {\n  TURN t1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER ask(constraints=[constraint_day_2_only, constraint_avoid_crowds], topic=topic_paris_art_museum_recommendation)\n  }\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "The request's day-2 timing is only an assumed opaque constraint; no itinerary/date-relative scheduling construct captures the conference context or recommendation ranking."}, {"item_id": "1775437471200", "source": "ThoughtTrace", "nl": "I need your help to create a study schedule for a month in which I can finish studying seven subjects completely.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT ArtPlan\n\nTASK ArtPlan : STRING {\n  GENERATE(duration=1, duration_unit=unit_month, quantity=7, target=art_study_schedule, topic=topic_complete_subject_study_plan) -> art_study_schedule_ref : STRING\n  RETURN art_study_schedule_ref\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "The artifact, duration, and number of subjects are captured, but there is no attribute relating seven distinct subjects to a complete schedule or representing completion requirements."}, {"item_id": "1775854603021", "source": "ThoughtTrace", "nl": "I need help by managing my money because I always run out before the end of the month", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT Ask\n\nCONVO Ask {\n  TURN t1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER ask(topic=topic_monthly_budgeting_before_month_end_shortfall)\n  }\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "The high-level advice request is representable as one symbolic topic, though its financial details are absent."}, {"item_id": "1775483125242", "source": "ThoughtTrace", "nl": "Hi I would like you to assist with planning a trip to China, I need a 4 star hotel, good restaurants to have traditional food, sight seeing about the heritage and other activities to do visiting other attractions", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT ArtPlan\n\nTASK ArtPlan : STRING {\n  GENERATE(constraints=[constraint_four_star_hotel, constraint_traditional_food_restaurants, constraint_heritage_sightseeing, constraint_other_attractions], target=art_trip_plan, topic=topic_china_trip) -> art_trip_plan_ref : STRING\n  RETURN art_tri