   "model (HuggingFaceH4/zephyr-7b-beta, score=85): Incest laws typically prohibit sexual relations between close relatives who are biologically related by blood, adoption, or marriage. However, in many royal families, first cousins are allowed to marry and have children. This practice is not considered incest because in most countries, the laws defining incest do not apply to royal families.\n\nRoyal families are exempt from certain laws due to their special status and historical traditions. Marriages between first cousins, while not common in the general population, have been a common practice in royal families for centuries. This is partly because marrying a close relative can help to maintain wealth, power, and genetic traits within the family.\n\nWhile there are no legal repercussions for royals marrying their cousins, there can be health concerns for their children. First cousins share a higher risk of genetic disorders, such as cystic fibrosis and Huntington's disease, than couples who are un",
   "model (timdettmers/guanaco-33b-merged, score=38): The laws and definitions of incest can vary from country to country, and the concept of royalty has its own set of traditions and customs that have developed over time.\n\nIn some parts of the world, cousin marriages were once common, and some royal families have a long history of practicing this.\n\nHowever, in many modern societies, cousin marriages are now illegal or discouraged, and royals are expected to abide by the same laws as everyone else.\n\nIn some cases, this may mean that royal marriages need to be approved by the government or parliament, and they may be subject to additional scrutiny to ensure that they are legal and appropriate.\n\nSo, while cousin marriages were once common among royals, they are now seen as less acceptable in many parts of the world, and royals are expected to follow the same laws and traditions as everyone else. Home > About",
   "user (None, score=None): Why are there only kings and queens in england?",
   "model (gpt-3.5-turbo, score=94): The monarchy in England has a long history dating back centuries. Traditionally, the line of succession has been determined by primogeniture, which means that the eldest child, regardless of gender, inherits the throne. This has typically resulted in kings and queens reigning in England, following the rules of monarchical succession. However, recent changes have allowed for gender equality in succession, potentially allowing for kings and queen regnants in the future.",
