   "  [feedback: task_motivation] Asking a common question for students",
   "assistant: Engineering math can be challenging, but with a solid plan, you can master it. Here's a step-by-step plan to help you study engineering math:\n\n**Step 1: Review the Basics**\n\n* Start by reviewing the fundamental concepts of algebra, geometry, trigonometry, and calculus.\n* Make sure you understand the basics of equations, functions, graphs, and mathematical operations.\n\n**Step 2: Focus on Key Topics**\n\n* Identify the most important topics in engineering math, such as:\n\t+ Differential equations\n\t+ Linear algebra\n\t+ Vector calculus\n\t+ Probability and statistics\n* Focus on understanding the concepts, formulas, and techniques related to these topics.\n\n**Step 3: Practice, Practice, Practice**\n\n* Practice is key to mastering engineering math. Try to:\n\t+ Solve problems and examples from your textbook or online resources.\n\t+ Work on practice exams or quizzes to test your understanding.\n\t+ Use online resources, such as Khan Academy, MIT OpenCourseWare, or Wolfram Alpha, to practice and learn.\n\n**Step 4: Use Visual Aids and Resources**\n\n* Use visual aids, such as:\n\t+ Graphing calculators or software (e.g., Desmos, GeoGebra)\n\t+ Online 3D visualizers (e.g., Wolfram Alpha, Mathway)\n\t+ Video lectures or tutorials (e.g., 3Blue1Brown, Crash Course)\n* These resources can help you understand complex concepts and visualize mathematical relationships.\n\n**Step 5: Join a Study Group or Find a Study Buddy**\n\n* Join a study group or find a study buddy to:\n\t+ Discuss challenging topics and problems.\n\t+ Share knowledge and resources.\n\t+ Stay motivated and accountable.\n\n**Step 6: Review and Reflect Regularly**\n\n* Regular review and reflection are crucial to retaining information and understanding concepts.\n* Set aside time each week to:\n\t+ Review notes and textbook material.\n\t+ Reflect on what you've learned and what you need to work on.\n\n**Additional Tips**\n\n* Start early and pace yourself. Engineering math can be challenging, so don't wait until the last minute to start studying.\n* Use active learning techniques, such as summarizing notes in your own words, creating concept maps, or making flashcards.\n* Seek help when you need it. Don't be afraid to ask your instructor, teaching assistant, or classmates for help.\n\n**Recommended Resources**\n\n* Textbooks: \"Engineering Mathematics\" by Stroud, \"Mathematics for Engineers\" by Croft\n* Online resources: Khan Academy, MIT OpenCourseWare, Wolfram Alpha, Mathway\n* Video lectures: 3Blue1Brown, Crash Course, Engineering Mathematics lectures on YouTube\n\nRemember, mastering engineering math takes time and effort. Stay consistent, persistent, and patient, and you'll be well on your way to success!",
   "  [feedback: explicit_affirmation] he gave a pretty normal answer for my needs, nothing special",
   "user: what about if i am currently in the helwan university in Egypt?",
