Searcher notes: {'search_mode': 'folder', 'relevant_precedents': ['HTML', 'Why Agentic Systems Need Ontologies', 'Formal language theory'], 'phenomena_present': ['multi-step-dependency'], 'kpi_fit': {'HTML': 'Improves determinism and interpretability by strictly separating content from context/attributes, ensuring stylistic differences are encoded as canonical key-value pairs rather than varied phrasing.', 'Why Agentic Systems Need Ontologies': 'Directly addresses the determinism KPI by advocating for shared, structured ontologies over free-text, which forces natural language synonyms to resolve to a single canonical symbol.', 'Formal language theory': "Provides the mathematical foundation for unambiguous parsing (e.g., via context-free grammars), ensuring the 'exactly one parse' requirement for decoding determinism is met."}, 'comparable_expression': {'precedent': 'HTML', 'nl': 'Email my mom (informally) versus email my professor (formally).', 'rendering': '<email recipient="mom" register="informal">...</email>\n<email recipient="professor" register="formal">...</email>'}, 'notes': "N7 enforces strict encoding determinism by requiring one canonical construct per concept, fixed argument ordering, and canonical symbols for synonyms. HTML's separation of attributes from content and the structured ontologies advocated in 'Why Agentic Systems Need Ontologies' are the closest precedents, demonstrating how mapping varied natural language to a constrained set of attributes eliminates stylistic variation, even if HTML itself lacks BrainCode's strict attribute-ordering constraint.", 'new_source_suggestion': None, '_search_mode': 'folder'}
Shaper proposal: {"summary": "This step makes canonical encoding concrete for document selection, step and attribute order, handles, physical multiplicity, speech-act coordination, and safe custom operations. It also adds governed search criteria for RAM, cuisine, reservation availability, and ranking/filter pipelines.", "note_position": "comply", "note_argument": "The changes impose one parseable canonical form: fixed target-first/alphabetical attributes, deterministic handles and turn names, defined action decomposition, canonical multi-question preservation, and a restricted legal custom-operation fallback. Synonyms map only to listed canonical operation symbols.", "foundations_overview": null, "rebuttals": [], "changes": [{"op": "add", "construct_name": "canonical-form", "grammar": "-- No independent production. Normative translation rules for document selection, operation selection, ordering, decomposition, handles, and transcript structure.", "semantics": "Canonical Form applies to the complete supplied input. One supplied message requiring ACTION or GENERATE emits one TASK; one supplied message containing only speech acts emits one CONVO; two or more supplied messages emit one CONVO with one TURN per supplied message in order. TURN names are t1, t2, .... A replacement message uses REVISES the current lineage tip; every other later message uses REPLY_TO the immediately preceding turn. AOR writes target first when present, then remaining attributes in ASCII alphabetical order. SOR places every producer before its consumer; otherwise preserves source-clause order. HNR: scalar REF Action results use <target>_ref, LIST[REF] results use <target>_refs, Generate results use target, task-call results use callee, and bound UTTER results use speech-act; repeated bases receive _2, _3, .... A direct placement of an unbound entity expands to pick_up then place. Once a REF is bound, every later move or transform of that same entity consumes that REF; pick_up may not reacquire a visible REF. Explicit multiplicity emits pick_up(quantity=N) returning LIST[REF[STRING]], followed by FOR EACH <target>_ref IN <target>_refs; no repeated indistinguishable scalar acquisition is emitted. rinse, heat, chill, slice, and pour preserve their input REF identity; an unstated required resource uses resource_sink, resource_heater, or resource_chiller. A single message with multiple independently scoped speech acts emits one UTTER per speech-act clause in source order, including clauses joined by and, then, or numbered questions; punctuation alone does not split an otherwise single speech act. OSR first matches maximal listed operation-vocabulary synonym spans; ties select the lexicographically smallest operation symbol. Coordination produces one operation per matched predicate span, negated predicates are represented only through an existing governed constraint or Check, and noun-derived imperatives without a listed operation are rejected. A custom operation is permitted only for a single uncoordinated affirmative imperative whose predicate is exactly one ASCII token matching [a-z]+ and has an explicit target. Its symbol is custom_general_<predicate>; its defined meaning is one atomic external general-domain action named by that predicate, with target required, governed matching attributes only, no result, and no implied decomposition. Predicates containing digits, non-ASCII letters, punctuation, multiple words, negation, or coordination are translation errors unless a listed operation represents them. Relative dates require TASK CONTEXT execution_date.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Put two pillows from the sofa onto the armchair.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT Pillow\n\nTASK Pillow {\n  ACTION pick_up(quantity=2, source=sofa, target=pillow) -> pillow_refs : LIST[REF[STRING]]\n  FOR EACH pillow_ref IN pillow_refs {\n    ACTION place(destination=armchair, target=pillow_ref)\n  }\n}"}, "glossary_gloss": "Canonical Form is the fixed translation rulebook for BrainCode. It fixes document shape, ordering, names, action decomposition, multiplicity, and how synonymous wording maps to one expression.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "N7 requires decisions that grammar productions alone cannot resolve. The restricted custom path is now legal, total over its permitted input class, semantically defined, and rejected outside that class rather than silently emitting invalid identifiers.", "cites_source": "Formal language theory", "addresses_note": "N7"}, {"op": "revise", "kind": "vocabulary", "construct_name": "operation-vocabulary", "glossary_gloss": "Operation-vocabulary contains canonical symbols for external actions and speech acts. Listed synonyms always map to one listed symbol; custom operations use the restricted Canonical Form rule.", "closed": false, "members": [{"symbol": "open_page", "gloss": "Navigate to a page.", "nl_synonyms": ["go to"]}, {"symbol": "click", "gloss": "Click an element.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "select_filter", "gloss": "Apply one filter.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "apply_filters", "gloss": "Apply multiple filters.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "sort", "gloss": "Rank a list.", "nl_synonyms": ["rank", "order by"]}, {"symbol": "search_travel", "gloss": "Search travel.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "search_transit", "gloss": "Search transit.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "search_web", "gloss": "Search a catalog or web source.", "nl_synonyms": ["find", "look for"]}, {"symbol": "pick_up", "gloss": "Acquire an entity.", "nl_synonyms": ["grab", "take", "retrieve"]}, {"symbol": "place", "gloss": "Place an acquired entity.", "nl_synonyms": ["put", "insert"]}, {"symbol": "rinse", "gloss": "Rinse an entity.", "nl_synonyms": ["wash"]}, {"symbol": "heat", "gloss": "Heat an entity.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "chill", "gloss": "Chill an entity.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "slice", "gloss": "Cut an entity into pieces.", "nl_synonyms": ["chop", "cut"]}, {"symbol": "pour", "gloss": "Pour a liquid.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "modify_code", "gloss": "Change source code.", "nl_synonyms": ["fix", "reduce", "optimize"]}, {"symbol": "run_tests", "gloss": "Run tests.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "add_to_cart", "gloss": "Add a selected item to a cart.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "check_reservation_availability", "gloss": "Check whether reservations are available.", "nl_synonyms": ["check reservation availability"]}, {"symbol": "send_email", "gloss": "Send email.", "nl_synonyms": ["email"]}, {"symbol": "send_message", "gloss": "Send a message.", "nl_synonyms": ["text"]}, {"symbol": "ask", "gloss": "Ask for information or advice.", "nl_synonyms": ["ask", "how should"]}, {"symbol": "inform", "gloss": "State information.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "respond", "gloss": "Answer a request.", "nl_synonyms": ["answer"]}, {"symbol": "propose", "gloss": "Propose an idea.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "correct", "gloss": "Correct a statement.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "acknowledge", "gloss": "Acknowledge a statement.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "confirm", "gloss": "Confirm a statement.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "decline", "gloss": "Decline a request.", "nl_synonyms": []}], "membership_rule": "All previously listed operation members remain legal canonical members. A missing operation may be emitted only under Canonical Form OSR as custom_general_<predicate>, where predicate is exactly one ASCII token matching [a-z]+ from a single affirmative uncoordinated imperative with an explicit target. The custom symbol denotes one atomic external general-domain action, requires target, permits only governed attributes, has no result, and is never a synonym for a listed member. All other unmatched predicates are translation errors until a glossary operation is added.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Check reservation availability for an Indian restaurant.", "braincode": "ACTION check_reservation_availability(cuisine=cuisine_indian, target=restaurant) -> reservation_available : BOOL"}, "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "This aligns the vocabulary fallback exactly with Canonical Form's ASCII-safe OSR and adds the reservation operation needed by the sampled closed task.", "addresses_note": "N7"}, {"op": "revise", "kind": "vocabulary", "construct_name": "attribute-name", "glossary_gloss": "Attribute-name indexes legal named arguments and the category or literal form accepted by each. Search criteria have dedicated names so filtering and ranking are not hidden in custom operations.", "closed": false, "members": [{"symbol": "target", "gloss": "The acted-on entity or generated artifact.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "cuisine", "gloss": "Restaurant or food cuisine from cuisine-value.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "min_ram", "gloss": "Minimum RAM capacity as NUMBER.", "nl_synonyms": ["RAM or more", "at least RAM"]}, {"symbol": "ram_unit", "gloss": "Unit for min_ram from capacity-unit-value.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "reservation_availability", "gloss": "Required reservation state from reservation-status-value.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "rank_field", "gloss": "Field used by sort from search-value.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "rank_direction", "gloss": "Sort direction from search-value.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "quantity", "gloss": "Explicit count as NUMBER.", "nl_synonyms": []}], "membership_rule": "All existing attribute-name members and their existing governing categories remain valid. min_ram requires NUMBER and ram_unit requires capacity-unit-value; cuisine requires cuisine-value; reservation_availability requires reservation-status-value. rank_field and rank_direction are legal only on sort. quantity is legal on pick_up and GENERATE only where that operation signature permits it.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Find laptops with at least 16 GB RAM.", "braincode": "ACTION search_web(min_ram=16, ram_unit=cap_gb, target=laptop) -> laptop_refs : LIST[REF[STRING]]"}, "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "The existing attribute index has no governed representation for RAM, cuisine, reservation state, or explicit physical multiplicity.", "addresses_note": "N7"}, {"op": "add", "kind": "vocabulary", "construct_name": "capacity-unit-value", "glossary_gloss": "Capacity-unit-value supplies canonical units for hardware capacity criteria. It keeps a numeric hardware requirement separate from its unit.", "closed": true, "members": [{"symbol": "cap_gb", "gloss": "One gibibyte-equivalent RAM capacity unit.", "nl_synonyms": ["GB", "gigabytes"]}, {"symbol": "cap_tb", "gloss": "One tebibyte-equivalent capacity unit.", "nl_synonyms": ["TB", "terabytes"]}], "membership_rule": null, "worked_example": {"nl": "Filter to 16 GB RAM or more.", "braincode": "ACTION search_web(min_ram=16, ram_unit=cap_gb, target=laptop) -> laptop_refs : LIST[REF[STRING]]"}, "change_type": "MINOR", "rationale": "A numeric RAM bound is not interpretable without a governed capacity unit.", "addresses_note": "N7"}, {"op": "add", "kind": "vocabulary", "construct_name": "cuisine-value", "glossary_gloss": "Cuisine-value names the cuisine criterion for food and restaurant searches. Synonymous cuisine wording maps to one canonical symbol.", "closed": false, "members": [{"symbol": "cuisine_indian", "gloss": "Indian cuisine.", "nl_synonyms": ["Indian food", "Indian"]}, {"symbol": "cuisine_italian", "gloss": "Italian cuisine.", "nl_synonyms": ["Italian food", "Italian"]}], "membership_rule": "Members match cuisine_[a-z_]+ and name a cuisine classification. A source cuisine maps to its listed canonical member; a missing cuisine requires a glossary member addition rather than free text.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Find an Indian restaurant.", "braincode": "ACTION search_web(cuisine=cuisine_indian, target=restaurant) -> restaurant_refs : LIST[REF[STRING]]"}, "change_type": "MINOR", "rationale": "Cuisine is a distinct searchable criterion and cannot be reliably represented as an entity target or opaque content.", "addresses_note": "N7"}, {"op": "add", "kind": "vocabulary", "construct_name": "reservation-status-value", "glossary_gloss": "Reservation-status-value gives canonical reservation states for restaurant and venue requests.", "closed": true, "members": [{"symbol": "reservation_available", "gloss": "A reservation can be made.", "nl_synonyms": ["reservation availability", "available reservations"]}, {"symbol": "reservation_unavailable", "gloss": "A reservation cannot be made.", "nl_synonyms": ["no reservations"]}], "membership_rule": null, "worked_example": {"nl": "Find a restaurant with reservations available.", "braincode": "ACTION search_web(reservation_availability=reservation_available, target=restaurant) -> restaurant_refs : LIST[REF[STRING]]"}, "change_type": "MINOR", "rationale": "Reservation availability is not equivalent to general product stock availability and needs a separately look-upable value set.", "addresses_note": "N7"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Action", "grammar": "action ::= \"ACTION\" operation-id \"(\" action-attrs? \")\" (\"->\" IDENT \":\" type)?\naction-attrs ::= action-attr (\",\" action-attr)*\naction-attr ::= attribute-name \"=\" value\noperation-id ::= operation-vocabulary member", "semantics": "Action executes one external operation. AOR writes target first, then remaining attributes alphabetically; result handles follow Canonical Form HNR. search_web returns LIST[REF[STRING]] and accepts target plus availability, category, color, cuisine, currency, gender, genre, location, max_price, min_ram, min_rating, platform, ram_unit, reservation_availability, shape, size, and trending. Filtering criteria are always attributes of search_web; ranking is always a following sort(target=list, rank_field=search-value, rank_direction=search-value); selecting a ranked item is extract(target=list, limit=NUMBER); add_to_cart consumes the extracted selection. check_reservation_availability(target, [cuisine, location, max_price, currency]) returns BOOL. pick_up(target, [quantity, source, color, shape, state]) returns REF[STRING] when quantity is absent or 1 and LIST[REF[STRING]] when quantity is greater than 1. pick_up selects only an unbound entity descriptor; a visible REF must be consumed directly by place or a transform. place requires a REF target. rinse, heat, chill, slice, and pour require and return the same REF identity.", "worked_example": {"nl": "On the electronics store, filter laptops to 16 GB RAM or more under $1,200, and add the best-rated one to the cart.", "braincode": "ACTION search_web(currency=curr_usd, max_price=1200, min_ram=16, ram_unit=cap_gb, target=laptop) -> laptop_refs : LIST[REF[STRING]]\nACTION sort(target=laptop_refs, rank_direction=dir_desc, rank_field=rank_rating) -> sorted_laptop_refs : LIST[REF[STRING]]\nACTION extract(limit=1, target=sorted_laptop_refs) -> laptop_refs_2 : LIST[REF[STRING]]\nACTION add_to_cart(target=laptop_refs_2)"}, "glossary_gloss": "Action performs one external operation with governed attributes. Search filters, ranking, selection, multiplicity, and object-reference use have fixed canonical forms.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "This supplies deterministic signatures for the closed-task criteria identified by the Critic and prevents competing encodings of filtering, ranking, selection, and repeated physical acquisition.", "cites_source": "HTML", "addresses_note": "N7"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Task", "grammar": "document ::= entrypoint (task | conversation)*\nentrypoint ::= \"ENTRYPOINT\" IDENT\ntask ::= \"TASK\" IDENT param_list? (\"CONTEXT\" execution_date_param)? (\":\" type)? (\"DECREASES\" IDENT)? \"{\" step+ return_stmt? \"}\"\nexecution_date_param ::= \"execution_date\" \"=\" ISO_DATE\nparam_list ::= \"(\" param (\",\" param)* \")\"\nparam ::= IDENT \":\" type\nreturn_stmt ::= \"RETURN\" value\nstep ::= action | flow_if | flow_for | bind | task_call | utterance | generation", "semantics": "TASK is Canonical Form's encoding for one supplied message requiring executable ACTION or GENERATE work. Its ENTRYPOINT and declaration name are PascalCase of the first executable-step HNR base with terminal _ref, _refs, and numeric suffix removed. Step order, attributes, decomposition, and result handles follow Canonical Form. CONTEXT remains a translation-time anchor and is required for relative dates. Existing parameter, return, recursion, and lexical-scope rules remain unchanged.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Create a structured report about Peterhof.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT ArtStructuredReport\n\nTASK ArtStructuredReport : STRING {\n  GENERATE(target=art_structured_report, format=format_structured_report, topic=topic_peterhof) -> art_structured_report : STRING\n  RETURN art_structured_report\n}"}, "glossary_gloss": "Task holds one executable request. Canonical Form fixes its declaration name, action order, attributes, and result handles.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "This connects TASK selection and naming to the common canonical rules and repairs the generated-artifact example to use a defined report format.", "cites_source": "HTML", "addresses_note": "N7"}, {"op": "revise", "kind": "vocabulary", "construct_name": "format-value", "glossary_gloss": "Format-value gives canonical output layout symbols for GENERATE. Report layouts use one explicit report-format symbol rather than an undefined format word.", "closed": false, "members": [{"symbol": "format_plain_text", "gloss": "Unstructured prose text.", "nl_synonyms": ["plain text"]}, {"symbol": "format_bullet_list", "gloss": "Bulleted list.", "nl_synonyms": ["bullet points"]}, {"symbol": "format_numbered_list", "gloss": "Numbered list.", "nl_synonyms": ["numbered steps"]}, {"symbol": "format_email", "gloss": "Email layout.", "nl_synonyms": ["as an email"]}, {"symbol": "format_markdown", "gloss": "Markdown layout.", "nl_synonyms": ["in markdown"]}, {"symbol": "format_table", "gloss": "Tabular layout.", "nl_synonyms": ["as a table"]}, {"symbol": "format_pdf", "gloss": "PDF document.", "nl_synonyms": ["as a pdf"]}, {"symbol": "format_newsletter", "gloss": "Newsletter layout.", "nl_synonyms": ["newsletter"]}, {"symbol": "format_structured_report", "gloss": "Headed structured report layout.", "nl_synonyms": ["structured report", "report"]}], "membership_rule": "format-value is closed for encoding: a translator may emit only a listed format symbol. A missing layout is a language gap and must be added to the glossary before use.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Make a structured report.", "braincode": "GENERATE(target=art_structured_report, format=format_structured_report, topic=topic_peterhof) -> art_structured_report : STRING"}, "change_type": "MINOR", "rationale": "The TASK report example requires a defined format symbol and translators need one canonical report-layout value.", "addresses_note": "N7"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Utterance", "grammar": "utterance ::= \"UTTER\" speech-act \"(\" utter-attr (\",\" utter-attr)* \")\" (\"->\" IDENT)?\nspeech-act ::= operation-vocabulary member\nutter-attr ::= attribute-name \"=\" value", "semantics": "UTTER records one speech act inside a CONVO TURN. Legal attributes are audience, constraints, content, recipient, target, tone, and topic; attributes follow AOR. recipient and audience require recipient-value, tone requires tone-value, constraints requires LIST[constraint-value], and topic requires topic-value. content remains a transitional STRING channel. A bound result is STRING and follows HNR. Canonical Form emits separate UTTER steps for separate independently scoped speech acts in one supplied message.", "worked_example": {"nl": "What is the surface texture of oil-rig gratings, and how would you describe their specular highlights?", "braincode": "CONVO Ask {\n  TURN t1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER ask(topic=topic_oil_rig_grating_surface_texture)\n    UTTER ask(topic=topic_oil_rig_grating_specular_highlights)\n  }\n}"}, "glossary_gloss": "Utterance records one conversational speech act. Separate requests in the same message are retained as separate ordered utterances.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "The old legal attribute list omitted already-established symbolic topic and constraint categories, and it did not connect multi-question preservation to canonical encoding.", "cites_source": "Why Agentic Systems Need Ontologies", "addresses_note": "N7"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Conversation", "grammar": "conversation ::= \"CONVO\" IDENT \"{\" turn+ \"}\"\nturn ::= \"TURN\" IDENT \"SPEAKER\" \"=\" speaker_lit (\"REPLY_TO\" IDENT )? (\"REVISES\" IDENT )? \"{\" step+ \"}\"\nspeaker_lit ::= \"USER\" | \"AGENT\"", "semantics": "CONVO is Canonical Form's encoding for a speech-only single message or any supplied multi-message transcript. It has exactly one TURN per supplied message, named t1, t2, ... in supplied order. A TURN may contain multiple ordered UTTER steps when one supplied message contains independently scoped speech acts; Canonical Form's speech-act clause rule determines those boundaries. The CONVO and ENTRYPOINT names use the first executable-step base. Explicit replacement uses REVISES the earlier lineage tip; all other later turns use REPLY_TO the preceding turn. Existing whole-body replacement, linear revision-chain, and per-turn scope rules remain unchanged.", "worked_example": {"nl": "What is your view on friendship? How do I become a good friend?", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT Ask\n\nCONVO Ask {\n  TURN t1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER ask(topic=topic_friendship)\n    UTTER ask(topic=topic_becoming_good_friend)\n  }\n}"}, "glossary_gloss": "Conversation records supplied messages in fixed order. A message can contain several ordered speech acts, so distinct coordinated questions are not lost.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "This repairs the previous example and fixes the missing deterministic relation between a supplied message, its turn, and its independently requested speech acts.", "cites_source": "Why Agentic Systems Need Ontologies", "addresses_note": "N7"}], "_provider_used": "openai", "_model_used": "gpt-5.6-terra"}
Critic assessment: {"decision": "needs-rework", "kpi_assessment": {"coverage": {"impact": "mixed", "reasoning": "The added RAM, cuisine, reservation, ranking, multiplicity, and multi-question rules improve coverage for several closed samples. However, the restricted custom-operation rule rejects many previously encodable multiword, coordinated, negated, or non-imperative operations, and the revised Action signatures leave important operations such as booking without a governed signature. Transform actions also become difficult to encode consistently because they require and preserve a REF but have no canonical binding rule compatible with no-shadowing."}, "expressivity": {"impact": "mixed", "reasoning": "The proposal preserves physical multiplicity and independently scoped questions better than the current language, and search/ranking criteria are materially more expressive. But it does not define containment relations such as a box already containing a card, source-specific game-review retrieval, booking details, or the complete semantic structure of several open requests; its custom-operation restriction can turn previously expressible actionable requests into translation errors."}, "determinism": {"impact": "mixed", "reasoning": "Target-first attribute ordering, deterministic turn names, HNR bases, list acquisition, and explicit multi-UTTER splitting are real N7 progress. Determinism remains broken by conflicting rules: AOR requires target first but the canonical-form Pillow example puts target last; HNR requires scalar Action-result names while transforms must preserve an existing REF and no visible binding may be shadowed; and 'independently scoped speech act clause' is semantic rather than operationally defined. Different capable translators will also disagree on predicate-span boundaries, whether a request is a custom imperative, and what counts as a replacement message."}, "interpretability": {"impact": "mixed", "reasoning": "Dedicated criteria vocabulary and explicit resource symbols improve readability. Conversely, custom_general_<predicate> has only a source-word-derived label and an intentionally opaque 'external general-domain action' meaning, so a reader cannot determine its effect from the glossary; the new canonical rulebook is also internally inconsistent with its own worked example."}, "improvement": {"impact": "benefit", "reasoning": "For supported search, shopping, household multiplicity, and multi-question tasks, explicit filters, ranking pipelines, references, and ordered utterances give a weaker agent substantially more actionable structure than raw language. The benefit is limited by the undefined/custom operation cases and transform-reference inconsistency, which must be repaired before the core canonical rules can safely land."}}, "simulations": [{"item_id": "dev-embodied-1", "source": "seed_tasks", "nl": "Rinse the mug in the sink, then put it in the coffee maker.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT Mug\n\nTASK Mug {\n  ACTION pick_up(target=mug) -> mug_ref : REF[STRING]\n  ACTION rinse(destination=sink, target=mug_ref)\n  ACTION place(destination=coffee_maker, target=mug_ref)\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "Canonical target-first ordering and REF reuse help. The expression exposes the unresolved transform problem: rinse is said to return the same REF identity, while HNR would require a result name and Bind forbids rebinding mug_ref."}, {"item_id": "held-trip-1", "source": "seed_tasks", "nl": "Plan a 3-day itinerary in Kyoto that includes at least one temple per day and avoids anything more than a 20-minute walk from the last stop.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT ArtItinerary\n\nTASK ArtItinerary : STRING {\n  GENERATE(target=art_itinerary, duration=3, duration_unit=unit_day, max_walk_duration=20, max_walk_duration_unit=unit_minute, stop_min_per_day=1, stop_requirement=class_temple, topic=topic_kyoto_trip) -> art_itinerary : STRING\n  RETURN art_itinerary\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "No proposed change is essential except the general canonical target-first ordering; existing Generate attributes carry the request."}, {"item_id": "521aa0aa-e099-4fc9-bb4e-f8a593a29f81", "source": "Mind2Web (osunlp/Mind2Web, train, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "Find the user reviews for the game \"Cyberpunk 2077\"", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT GameRefs\n\nTASK GameRefs {\n  ACTION search_web(target=game_cyberpunk_2077) -> game_refs : LIST[REF[STRING]]\n  ACTION custom_general_retrieve(target=game_refs)\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "search_web can identify the game under N6, but no governed review/user-review criterion or retrieval operation is defined. custom_general_retrieve is legal only if the source is treated as one affirmative imperative with an explicit target; translators can reasonably instead choose search_web with an unstated review filter."}, {"item_id": "b770af80-75f0-470c-8797-1466d90c35ce", "source": "Mind2Web (osunlp/Mind2Web, train, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "Get the cheapest last-minute pet-friendly road trip hotel deal in May for a single person with a pet and book.", "braincode": "UNENCODABLE: search_travel has no stated governed attributes for last-minute, pet-friendly, road-trip, May, one guest, or pet; booking is not a listed operation and a coordinated trailing 'and book' predicate is explicitly rejected by Canonical Form.", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "The proposal improves ranking representation in general but regresses the former open custom-operation fallback for this coordinated request and supplies no booking signature."}, {"item_id": "trial_T20190908_153234_664631#2", "source": "ALFRED (json_2.1.0, train)", "nl": "Put a box with card on the red couch.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT Card\n\nTASK Card {\n  ACTION pick_up(source=table, target=card) -> card_ref : REF[STRING]\n  ACTION place(destination=box, target=card_ref)\n  ACTION pick_up(source=table, target=box) -> box_ref : REF[STRING]\n  ACTION place(destination=couch_red, relation=on, target=box_ref)\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "Sequencing can establish the card-in-box state, but there is no canonical containment/state relation saying that the subsequently acquired box is specifically the box containing that card. The source/table details also come from trajectory context rather than the one-line request."}, {"item_id": "trial_T20190907_160520_516302#1", "source": "ALFRED (json_2.1.0, train)", "nl": "Put a frying pan with a scoop in the sink.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT Scoop\n\nTASK Scoop {\n  ACTION pick_up(source=cabinet, target=scoop) -> scoop_ref : REF[STRING]\n  ACTION place(destination=frying_pan, target=scoop_ref)\n  ACTION pick_up(source=stove, target=frying_pan) -> frying_pan_ref : REF[STRING]\n  ACTION place(destination=sink, relation=in, target=frying_pan_ref)\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "The action sequence is representable, but the language has no first-class assertion that frying_pan is the pan containing scoop when it is acquired."}, {"item_id": "django__django-16032", "source": "SWE-bench_Verified (princeton-nlp/SWE-bench_Verified, test, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "__in doesn't clear selected fields on the RHS when QuerySet.alias() is used after annotate().", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT ModifyCode\n\nTASK ModifyCode {\n  ACTION modify_code(assertion=assert_queryset_alias_subquery_single_column, expected_output=outcome_subquery_returns_one_column, file=path_django_db_models_fields_related_lookups_py, method=sym_queryset_alias_filter_in_subquery, revision=chg_clear_rhs_selected_fields)\n  ACTION modify_code(assertion=assert_queryset_alias_subquery_single_column, expected_output=outcome_subquery_returns_one_column, file=path_django_db_models_sql_query_py, method=sym_queryset_alias_filter_in_subquery, revision=chg_clear_rhs_selected_fields)\n  ACTION run_tests(assertion=assert_queryset_alias_subquery_single_column, target=django)\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "N6 permits the needed code-value members. Proposed AOR would require target first where present; this request otherwise relies on existing code-value categories."}, {"item_id": "sympy__sympy-11618", "source": "SWE-bench_Verified (princeton-nlp/SWE-bench_Verified, test, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "distance calculation wrong\n``` python\n>>> Point(2,0).distance(Point(1,0,2))\n1\n```", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT ModifyCode\n\nTASK ModifyCode {\n  ACTION modify_code(actual_output=outcome_point_distance_1, expected_output=outcome_point_distance_sqrt_5, file=path_sympy_geometry_point_py, revision=chg_include_third_dimension_distance)\n  ACTION run_tests(assertion=assert_point_2d_3d_distance_sqrt_5, target=sympy)\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "Existing code-value categories plus N6 suffice; no proposed new construct is required."}, {"item_id": "c3930", "source": "PRISM (HannahRoseKirk/prism-alignment, conversations config, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "tell me an adult joke", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT Ask\n\nCONVO Ask {\n  TURN t1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER ask(topic=topic_adult_joke)\n  }\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "The request's speech act and topic are captured with an assumed topic-value member. The full supplied trajectory contains later distinct turns and feedback, but the sampled NL field itself is one speech act."}, {"item_id": "c10", "source": "PRISM (HannahRoseKirk/prism-alignment, conversations config, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "What is the likely cause of death of the late, great Matthew Perry?", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT Ask\n\nCONVO Ask {\n  TURN t1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER ask(topic=topic_matthew_perry_cause_of_death)\n  }\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "The initial question is representable as a symbolic topic. Later correction and feedback in the trajectory would need separate turns; Canonical Form improves their ordering but does not operationally define when a conversational elaboration is a REVISES replacement."}, {"item_id": "c6709", "source": "PRISM (HannahRoseKirk/prism-alignment, conversations config, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "how to deal with a boss is putting too much workload on you", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT Ask\n\nCONVO Ask {\n  TURN t1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER ask(topic=topic_manage_excessive_boss_workload)\n  }\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "The open request is captured through ask plus a topic-value symbol. The later follow-up questions in the trajectory would benefit from the new deterministic t1/t2 naming and REPLY_TO rule."}, {"item_id": "c1150", "source": "PRISM (HannahRoseKirk/prism-alignment, conversations config, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "If royals breed with their cousins, why is this not seen as incest and why aren't they imprisoned or arrested?", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT Ask\n\nCONVO Ask {\n  TURN t1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER ask(topic=topic_royal_cousin_marriage_legal_status)\n    UTTER ask(topic=topic_royal_cousin_marriage_criminal_liability)\n  }\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "The multi-question rule helps preserve two independently scoped questions. It remains non-deterministic whether these are two speech acts or one compound explanatory question because 'independently scoped' has no mechanical test."}, {"item_id": "wildchat1m_en3u-22516", "source": "PATHs (microsoft/prototypical-hai-collaborations, wildchat1m_en3u-task_utterance_wintent_anns, model=gpt-3.5-turbo-0301)", "nl": "hi", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT Inform\n\nCONVO Inform {\n  TURN t1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER inform(topic=topic_greeting)\n  }\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "The sampled greeting is a single speech act. The lengthy later image-prompt, marketing-copy, and cover-design transcript would require multiple turns and symbolic categories for detailed visual composition that are not established by this proposal."}, {"item_id": "wildchat1m_en3u-49918", "source": "PATHs (microsoft/prototypical-hai-collaborations, wildchat1m_en3u-task_utterance_wintent_anns, model=gpt-3.5-turbo-0301)", "nl": "my phd thesis is about “Financial Risk Modelling using time series analysis and machine learning models”. i did following works in my thesis: - market risk modelling (value at risk prediction) using time series analysis and Deep Belief Network. - liquidity risk class prediction (high liquidity vs low liquidity) using different machine learning models (including SVM, RF, KNN, ANN, and LSTM) for 4 crypto currencies (BitCoin, Ethereum, LiteCoin, Cardano). - Money Laundering detection using different machine learning models (including SVM, RF, KNN, ANN, and LSTM) for bitcoin using transaction graph data provided by Elliptic company.describe the aim of this research from scientific view", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT ArtTechnicalExplanation\n\nTASK ArtTechnicalExplanation : STRING {\n  GENERATE(target=art_technical_explanation, topic=topic_scientific_aim_financial_risk_modelling_time_series_machine_learning_cryptoassets) -> art_technical_explanation : STRING\n  RETURN art_technical_explanation\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "Under N6, the detailed research premise can be an atomic topic symbol. The later practical-view revision would need a separate turn or revised generation, but no deterministic criterion distinguishes a clarification from replacement."}, {"item_id": "wildchat1m_en3u-103814", "source": "PATHs (microsoft/prototypical-hai-collaborations, wildchat1m_en3u-task_utterance_wintent_anns, model=gpt-3.5-turbo-0613)", "nl": "what do you call it when cards run out in solitaire", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT Ask\n\nCONVO Ask {\n  TURN t1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER ask(topic=topic_solitaire_cards_run_out_term)\n  }\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "The speech act and subject are expressible. The later requests for alternate phrasing are separate follow-ups in the trajectory and would be sequenced by the proposed TURN rule."}, {"item_id": "wildchat1m_en3u-31706", "source": "PATHs (microsoft/prototypical-hai-collaborations, wildchat1m_en3u-task_utterance_wintent_anns, model=gpt-3.5-turbo-0301)", "nl": "Can you write a hypothetical crossover of Ouran High School Host Club and Fruits Basket, what would the characters interactions be like?", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT ArtStory\n\nTASK ArtStory : STRING {\n  GENERATE(target=art_story, constraints=[constraint_hypothetical_crossover, constraint_describe_character_interactions], topic=topic_ouran_high_school_host_club_fruits_basket) -> art_story : STRING\n  RETURN art_story\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "N6 permits atomic topic and constraint members. No change in this proposal specifically improves creative-artifact content representation."}, {"item_id": "1776016996729", "source": "ThoughtTrace (SCAI-JHU/ThoughtTrace, Parquet column-projected sample, JSON-decoded messages)", "nl": "Hello", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT Inform\n\nCONVO Inform {\n  TURN t1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER inform(topic=topic_greeting)\n  }\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "The one-line sampled greeting is representable. The later engineering-math planning and Egyptian-YouTuber request need additional turns; t1, t2 naming is deterministic but the proposed rules do not add a recommendation/search-criteria model."}, {"item_id": "1775769104830", "source": "ThoughtTrace (SCAI-JHU/ThoughtTrace, Parquet column-projected sample, JSON-decoded messages)", "nl": "Hi I need your help. I have an assignment where I need to write a report of about 20 pages. Their topic is about battery energy storage systems in South Africa, technologies and challenges.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT ArtStructuredReport\n\nTASK ArtStructuredReport : STRING {\n  GENERATE(target=art_structured_report, format=format_structured_report, quantity=20, quantity_unit=unit_page, topic=topic_battery_energy_storage_systems_south_africa, constraints=[constraint_cover_technologies, constraint_cover_challenges]) -> art_structured_report : STRING\n  RETURN art_structured_report\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "format_structured_report 